      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0008" n="art0008">
        <p>McDOUGALUS Pategt SELF—RAISING FLOUR I NFDRITEM</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0009" n="art0009">
        <head>S aved the Weakling of the Family.</head>
        <p>S aved the Weakling of the Family.</p>
        <p>Heir to the Duke of Norfolk.—The arrival of a son and heir to the Duke and Duchess of Norfolk, is a very important event in the family of the premier duke and earl, and also to the Roman Catholic Church, to which both the Duke and Duchess belong. The Duke of Norfolk was born in 1347, and his son by his first wife died in 1902. He married again in 1904, the Hon. Gwendolen Constable Maxwell, daughter and heiress of Baron Harries. A daughter was born to the Duke and Duchess in 1905, and by the birth of a son the ancient barony of lierries will ultimately be merged into the Norfolk title. It is the first occasion on which an infant Earl of Arundel and Surrey has been born and baptised at Arundel Castle, the Duke of Norfolk's well- known seat in Sussex, and it makes Lord Edmund Talbot—the Duke's brother—no longer heir presemptive to the dukedom, and the vast possessions which are attached to the title. Christian Names.—Pretty names are not so important as good looks, but still they count for something in a girl's matrimonial prospects. The homely names loved by parents of the Victorian era have mostly gone out of fashion, and it must be confessed that many of them were calculated to have a distressing effect upon sensi- tive natures. Perhaps the best plan for modern young parents to do is to follow the example of a distinguished poet who gave his daughters a long string of Christian names, so that the future husband might select the one he fancied most. That is a convenient plan which many husbands would wish had been more usual, but nowadays, the baptism registers show that the modern parent delights more and more in a multi- tude of names. A Chapel Royal Wedding.—Marriages in the Chapel Royal are a little more frequent nowadays than in Queen Victoria's time, but the privilege is still accorded to but very few, luring the present reign, five marriages have been solemnised there, and the sixth will be that of Mies Jean Reid and Mr. John Ward,which is to take place at the end of the month in the presence of the King and Queen, and several members of the Royal House. By a constitutional principle, the King carries his Caurt with him, wherever he goes and the Chapel Royal which consists of a dean, sub-dean, and Inumerons clergy, was founded for the purpose of lattendance on the Sovereign, wherever he might be. But custom has parted from the original intention, and in these days the Chapel Royal never hold any service or worship except in the elaborate little chapel in St. James's Palace, which has come to be recognized as the only °Mehl palace of the monarch. Queen Victoria was married in the Chapel Royal at St. James's, and the Prince and Princess of Wales were the last Royal couple to be wedded there. When to Marry.—Switzerland has been giving the world some statistics of its matrimonial affairs. From these it seems that the Swiss pay little regard to the rule that a man, however old, or young he may be, should marry a woman half his age, plus seven years. fn an unusually large number of cases, the brides were ten to fifteen years older than the bridegrooms, and the former appear to have taken the German professor's advice, that the most successful marriages are those in which the woman waits until she is thirty-five. That is very likely true, for most women at thirty-five are better able to judge of character and circumstances than at the age of indulgence in all sorts of wild dreams. In one respect the Swiss statistics accord with most other countries, in showing that the great majority of widows marry again. Men apparently are not much disposed to take the warning to heart which appears in the "Pickwick Papers," but then probably Tony Weller would have said "its the widows who marry the men, not the men who marry the widows.". Lady Dudley's Irish Nurs'ng Scheme.—Some four or five years ago the Countess of Dudley started a scheme for securing the services of fully trained nurses for the sick poor in the impoverished districts of the Wese-eaf Ireland. As a result some fourteen or fifteen qualified nurses are working in remote parts, along the coasts from Donegal to County Cork, and the value of their services can scarcely be realised unless the places where they live, fee from rail or post were visited. As an example one nurse is stationed at Aranmore off the Coast of Donegal which in stormy weather is frequently cut off from communication with the mainland for a week or more. There is no doctor on the island, so the lives of the sick often depend on the nurse, who also does a lot of good by teaching the people the value of attention to the laws of sanitation. It is desired to increase the number of nurses at work, and for this Tariff Reform an Aggravation of Social Evils., purpose an We°l for help is made. The initial expense j o equipping one of Lady Dudley's nurses, is about £5O , Mr. William C. Anderson (Labour candidate for the 'or £6O and her up-keep costs about £lOO a year. The ; Hyde Division of Cheshire, and member of the National Committee which includes Lady Aberdeen, Lady Mayo, I Administrative Council of the Independent Labour and Lady Arnott, have recently appointed additional Party) addressed a series of meetings in Warrington nurses in co. Mayo, and co. Donegal, but the funds are last week-end. At a meeting held on Friday evening insufecient to extend the work as far as is desired. on Sankey Green over which the Rev. John Francis How Infection is Spread.—The wholesale destruction Matthews presided, Mr. Anderron drew attention to of wood pigeons from diphtheria, which has occurred the growth of monopoly in British industry. In ship- in several districts within the past year or two, is, it building, coal, steel and iron trades, in the tobacco and seems, fraught with considerable danger to the public cement industry, in the manufacture of cotton thread, I health. Dr. Sembon, of the School of Tropical in fine cotton-spinning, bleaching, dyeing, indeed in all Medicine, t