      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0014" n="art0014">
        <head>WOMEN'S CHAT.</head>
        <p>WOMEN'S CHAT.</p>
        <p>The Examinek Saturday, June 6, 1908. WOMA N'S REALM. ISCIENCE NOTES AND NEWS.</p>
        <p>THE PARENT OF RADIUM.</p>
        <p>Professor B. B. Boltwood, of ,the Sloane Laboratory, New Haven, concludes, as the result of experiments made by. him, that there is good cause for believing that uranium minerals contain an element emitting alpha rays, and resembling thorium in it chemical properties, but which is different from the other radio-active elements that have been identified. This element appears to have about the same degree of activity as the ra- dium with which it is associated in minerals, and Professor, Boltwood thinks that it is, without doubt, a product of uranium, and probably "the immediate parent of radium."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0015" n="art0015">
        <head>DRESS-FASHION-HOME,</head>
        <p>DRESS-FASHION-HOME,</p>
        <p>THE RISE OF THE RUFFLE.</p>
        <p>We are all under the spell of the ruffle. No doubt it is the Directoiro revival that has led to it, says the Globe. It began with small, old- fashioned frillings at the neck, which grew wider and wider, and then spread out at the base of the collar as well, until now nothing but a very wide standing ruche of tulle, chiffon, or net round the neck will satisfy our ruffle propensi- ties. It may be admitted at once that most of those worn just now are exceedingly exaggera- ted, but it really does not matter, because they are all remarkably becoming. Some of them are in mixtures of ostrich feather and ribbon; others, and these are more becoming, are semi- transparent in spotted net edged with ribbon of some bright shade. But though the all-round ruffle is charming, it does not suit the face and figure nearly so well as those which are tied closely round the neck with a band of ribbon round the middle and a big bow in the middle or at the back. These show that there is a neck, and that one's head is not being carried about on a ruffle instead of a charger, with only a casual connection with the body beneath the ruffle.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0016" n="art0016">
        <head>CANVAS FROCK' FOR TENNIS.</head>
        <p>CANVAS FROCK' FOR TENNIS.</p>
        <p>In our sketch is shown a tennis dress com- posed of cream canvas, though, as it will be seen, it would work out equally well in linen or zephyr. The blouse bodice is cut low at the neck, the opening being filled in with a yoke of cream embroidered muslin. The yoke is out- lined with a stitched strap of the canvas, which is carried down the front of the blouse. The ful- ness is arranged in tucks at each side of tho</p>
        <p>strap, and there are straps over the shoulders, stitched at Eno edge only, the other being left to stand out over the top of the sleeve. The. sleeves are full at the top, and drawn into long, tight- fitting cuffs, which finish at the wrists with a small pointed strap. The skirt is tucked round the hips, and trimmed with straps of the mate- rial to correspond with those on, the bodice. At the bottom there is one large tuck above the hem.</p>
