      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0017" n="art0017">
        <head>Tm ROTTEN Row SENSATION.</head>
        <p>Tm ROTTEN Row SENSATION.</p>
        <p>I was fortunate enough, says a writer in the Lady's Pictorial, to be out an the morning that the wonderful equestrienne galloped up and down the Row, clad from head to foot in dead white, Court plumes, also white, and quantities of diamonds blazing on her golden hair. So en- tranced were the riders with this marvellous apparition that even Mr., Winston Churchill for the moment lost his head, and, whilst turning round to gaze in speechless wonder, allowed his own horse to Cannon into another rider, who nearly came to Mother Earth. It was all too quaint for worth—the great ladies, taking their morning constitutional, rushing to the railings, with everyone else, to get a nearer view of the show. In fact, the mornings on which rode the "Woman in White" will for ever be memorable in the annals of Rotten Row. The next morn- ing the Row was crowded from end to end with folks of all classes. Lest in wonderment, I was soon enlightened, as one and all of my friends rushed up to know if the diamond lady was about, and bitterly disappointed were they that she did not appear that day. But the climax came when, the next morning, there she was again, this time dreßsed in azure blue from head to foot, and all fashionable London agape at her. LINEN Sun' FOE HOT WEATIIEII.</p>
        <p>Could anything be nicer for the hot summer weather than this picturesque little suit of butcher-blue linen? The tunic fastens on the left shoulder ami clown the left side with large buttons, covered with linen embroidered with white thread, and connected by big loops of blue cord. The same effect is relocated on the right</p>
        <p>;icle also, the seams being left open below the belt. The collar is detachable, and is of white linen. The small bishQp sleeves are tucked at the wrist, and finish withomall white linen cuffs. The belt can be of linen or white suede. The short knickers are quite plain, except for a couple of embroidered buttons placed at each side.</p>
        <p>SOME NEW NOTIONS IN BODICE&amp;</p>
        <p>The fashion is creeping in, says the Omen, of wearing lawn bodices of the same shade as a silk or cloth skirt, r..nd linen bodices of the same hue, sometimes embroidered, and often prettily trimmed with lace, have a very stylish appear- ance. A touch of colour to carry out the idea of the skirt is mostly introduced into the white blouses for all but quite young girls; the white blouses, whether lace. cambric, or linen, were found to be too trying. Bodice and skirts are often of different materials, chiffon cloth and chiffon velvet mingle with faced cloth skirts, and sometimes with filet lace dyed the colour. Silk and crepe de chine bodices aro still the vogue, and a very pretty vogue too; unlined silk coats serve for bodices, and when after tho rouis XV. order have a charm all their ows</p>
