      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0018" n="art0018">
        <head>TO-Milr'S RECIPE.</head>
        <p>TO-Milr'S RECIPE.</p>
        <p>CROQLETTES DE BcErF.—The remains of Sun- day's cold beef can be turned into a meet delec- table little entrée for Monday's late dinner if this recipe be followed, says a Daily Graphic writer. Simmer FOMO thick slices of peeled onion, turnip. celery, and carrot in ltfilf a pint of milk until it is strongly. flavoured. Strain off. Make half a pint of sauce with the milk, using one ounce of flour and an ounce and three- quarters of butter to the half-pint of milk. Sea- son to taste with pepper, salt, a very little Cayenne, and a few drops of lemon juice. Take Four ounces of finely-minced cold beef, mutton, or veal. (Note.—lt must be minced by band, and be quite free from fat, but a little ham or tongue may be mixed with it if liked.) Let the sauce cool a little. Mix the meat with it. Spread the mixture out on a dish to the depth of an inch. Leave till cold. Shape into balls about the size of a small tangerine. Egg and breacl- crumb these in the usual way, and fry in des') tat from wnien tile blue smCnce is rising freely. Drain well on kitchen paper. Garnish with pars- ley, and serve as hot as possible. The mixture should be made early in the moping, as it Wass some hours to cool.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0019" n="art0019">
        <head>BRAINS IN MARS.</head>
        <p>BRAINS IN MARS.</p>
        <p>Whatever • its actual age, says Professor Lowell, in the Century, any life now existent on Mars must, be in the land stage of its •de- velopment, on the whole, a much higher one than the marine, But, more than this, it should probably have gone much further if it exist at all, for in its evolving of terra firma Mare has far outstripped the earth. Mars' surface is now all land. Its.forms of life must be not only terrestrial as against aquatic, but even as opposed to terraqueous ones. They must have reached not simply the stage of land dwelling wnere the possibilities are greater for those able to embrace them, but that further point of pinching poverty where brain is needed to survive at all.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0020" n="art0020">
        <head>DEADLY CHLOROFORM.</head>
        <p>DEADLY CHLOROFORM.</p>
        <p>Dr. Freyberger, analysing seventy-four cases of sudden death under the influence of an anEesthetic, says, according to the Health Record, forty-five were "males and twenty- nine females. Chloroform was responsible for five times as many deaths as any other single. aniesth.etie. In twenty eases death occurred before the operation was begun; usually on ac- count of a too great amount of chloroform being suddenly inhaled during the second or excitable stage of anesthesia. In thirty-two cases death occurred during the operation, seventeen of these from ehlorofcrm. In eight eases death came after the operation was finished. Dr. F. W. Hewitt, the well-known antesthetist, contends that a large number of the deaths from anesthesia are absolutely preventable, and that the view that a certain percentage of fatalities must occur is erroneous.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0021" n="art0021">
        <head>TILTED BY STORMS.</head>
        <p>TILTED BY STORMS.</p>
        <p>It was the, invention of the seismograph for the study of earthquakes that led to the discovery of the surprising sensitiveness of the crust of the globe to forces that might have been thought too insignificant to cause distortion. Among these forces is the altera- tion in the pressure of the atmosphere during the passage or storms, causing a perceptible tilting of large areas of ground. A curious case of such tilting in an unexpected direc- tion has been recorded by Professor Oinori in Japan. A storm parsing over the sea east of Tokio caused the bordering land to tilt downward, notwithstanding the fact that the atmospheric pressure is lessened within a storm area. This is explained by the fact that the sea rises with release of atmospheric pressure, and the aecumulation of• water more than sufficed to counterbalance the de- crease in. weight of the air.</p>
        <p>SPONTANEOUS COMBUSTION OF HAY.</p>
        <p>The familiar phenomenon of the heating of hay in the stack has nimally been ascribed to purely chemical reactions. Researches carried out by Miche, however, shew that the process is a physiological one, and. due to bacteria, and other lowly organisms. It was found that sterilised hay would not heat, but that if sprinkled with water —contaminated with earth or ordinary hay it did so. A species of bacillus and a fungus were found to be chiefly responsible for temperatures up to 50cleg. Cent. For higher temperatures another species of bacillus was found to be responsi- ble. The work of the bacteria in the heating of hay illustrates the curious fact that these organisms usually end by killing themselves. In the centre of a large heated stack the hay is found to be sterile. And this is attributed to the killing of the bacteria by the heat which they have themselves evolved. In other cases they are poisoned by the toxins which they themselves produce.</p>
        <p>PROSPECT OF COAL AT NEWHAVEN.</p>
        <p>The finding of coal beneath the chalk of Dover has led to the hope of its occurrence at workable depths in other parts of the south of England. Among other places New- haven has been suggested as the site of an experimental boring. Professor J. W. Gre- gory has been investigating the subject from a geological standpoint, and the substance, of hi a report seems to be that though eoal may exist anywhere beneath the younger riocks be- tween Newhaven and' Dover, there is no posi- tive evidence that the former lies over a coal basin. On tire whole, it is concluded that Newhaven is ,not the most suitable locality for a trial boring. As a matter of fact, remarks the Globe, all that geologists can say is that the Somersetshire coalfield was originally continuous across the south of England, with that of Northern France and Belgium. Before this area of coal received its cover of younger rocks it was extensively denuded, and only patches of it remained. And no geologist can say exactly wnere these patches occur.</p>
        <p>SUBJECTIVE COLOURS SEEN IN DISEASE</p>
        <p>z Abnormal perception of subjective colours Occurs in diseases of the brain, spinal cord, and optic nerve, after operations for cataract, in various infectious and other diseases, and occasionally without any apparent canes. It is produced, sometimes, though rarely, by poisonous drugs. About 100 years ago Dr. Patoniket d2seribed a case of red vision pro- duced by henbane (Hyoseyamus). Since then yellow vision has often been observed as a re- sult of the administration of eantonin, the ac- tive principle of European wormseed (Arte- misia). Most eases of subjective colour vision due to drugs escaped , notice until recent years. The phenomena, according to Dr. Hil- bert in Unwhalt, may now be classified as follows: Violet vision produced by hasheesh or Indian hemp (Cannabis indica) and by toadstools; blue by alcohol; red by atropin, duboisin, and seopolamin (three alkaloids much used by oculists.), and by excessive use of tobacco and quinine; yellow by picric and salicylic acids, digitalis, and phenacetin, the external application of chromic acid and iodoform, the inllalation of carbon monoxide, snake bites, and abuse of tobacco. Mescal, the Mexican beverage obtained from the cac- tus, produces polychromic spectra, according to Dr. Ulis. No cubstance which causes the sensation of green has yet been discovered. .</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0022" n="art0022">
        <head>PAINLESSNESS OF DEATH.</head>
        <p>THE PAINLESSNESS OF DEATH.</p>
        <p>Lord Bacon, in an ersay Cu death, wrote: "It is as natural to die as to be born, and to a little infants perhaps, the one is as pain- ful as the other." A Viennese professor, has delivered a lecture before • the Society of Vienna authors and journalfsts, in which ho maintained that death is almost invariably actually painless, that the articulus mortis is, except in quite exceptional circumstances, free from even the feeling of distress. The fear of death common to all is not, of courso, a part of death itself, it is a physical scare of life. The actual and ultimate 'cause of death is, undoubtedly, in the large majority of cases, the failure of the heart's action, and consciousness is generally lost'. before the heart ceases to beat. • Even in gunshot in- juries causing death, the fatal result occurs before the painful impression has had time to reach the brain from the skin. In drowning, and in all forms of suffocation, the antesthetic effect of the carbonicTacid gas which has ac- cumulated in the blood exerts its full influ- ence long before life terminates. , In death from acute diseases, the toxins which accu- mulate in the blood so dull the mechanism or consciousness as to anticipate by, it may be, hours, the bitterness of death. Thus, in a large majority of cases, that state of feeling described as the fear of death is itself not erperiencedt</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0023" n="art0023">
        <head>(ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</head>
        <p>(ALL RIGHTS RESERVED.]</p>
        <p>LORD RONALD'S WIFE.</p>
        <p>By FITZGERALD MOLLOY,</p>
        <p>Author of "The Devil's Head," "By an Um known Hand," " Thro' Gates of Flame," "Just at Moonrise," er,e.</p>
        <p>Not even his wife ever ventured to disturb Lord Ronald Clyde whilst. he worked in the library from breakfast till lunch; but after that Meal he always sat with her in her special sanctum, where they gossiped like the good comrades they were, or where she read and played to him whilst he drank his coffee and smoked his cigar. To-day they were silent, as he, with a contemptuous smile on his well-cut lips, read a newspaper article on the Asiatic Loan, that had become a matter of international importance, and which was to be decided that afternoon at the Cabinet Council he was -ab6ut to attend.</p>
        <p>Whilst the smile gave place to a frown, she watched him with something of the eager pathetic affection of a dog for his master; for during the three years of their married life her bilye had but struck deeper roots, until her de- votion to him absorbed her life, and made its every day a dream of happiness. Compared with this fascination, the transient fancies and harmless flirtations that had beguiled the weari- ness of her monotonous girlhood, were light as thistledown; and with a- shudder she sometimes wondered what her fate would have been had they never met, or having met, if he hadlinot loved her. „ .</p>
        <p>Suddenly ho flung down the py.per. "What a talent for misrepresentation the man must have," said he, referring to the leader writer. "Perhaps ho doesn't understand." "To misrepresent without understanding would be still more clever. I sometimes wish I had never gone into Parliament." said he, be- lieving that he spoke with sincerity. Her heart gave a bound at' the thought of having him all to herself without -sharing him with that wretched Westminster that absorbed so much of him; but then her pride in his position. her ambition for his future flouted such selfish desires as attacked her, and she said, "The Government needs you." "Oh, they could' get on well enough without me," he answered, whilst unconscious of vanity he waited for contradiction. "The nation doesn't think so," she answered, alertly. "The nraion is the name a handful of people of my way of thinking give themselves," he told her as ho rose up, a tall, handsome man with clear-cut; regular features, a wide forehead, whose sparse hair made him look older than his forty-five years, grave, gentle eyes, and an air cf distinction which those who spoke to him for the first time generally described as hauteur. "I suppose to-day's Cabinet meeting will de- cide the Asiatic Loan question." she suggested. "I shall be able to tell you when I get back," he replied; for, confident of her discretion, de- sirous of sharing his interests with her and of relieving his mind, he invariably talked with her over the political movements that were dis- cussed by the Cabinet. "If so, Parliament will be prorogued?" she said, eagerly. "And I can escape. We will find out some wilderness where neither friends nor letters nor telegrams can find us, and stay there all through autumn." "It sounds as if we were plotting an elope- ment," said she. "Doesn't it? " elevating his eyebrows. " When / I was a romantic girl I thought an elopement must be the height of bliss," she con: fessed. "And now that yen are a romantic woman I suppose you haven't changed your views." "Not a bit," she laughed. "Then we will elope. "Oh, lei us! " she replied, glowing with delight. "It's a bargain," he said, and to seal it, ho kissed her. .</p>
        <p>When he bad gone she took up the newspaper that she might read the article on the Asiatic Loan, and be able to discuss it with her husband on his return; but before she came to the end she was interrupted by a servant, who presented a card. Taking it from the salver, she read with some surprise the name, Surgeon-Major Ernestine. _ _ .</p>
        <p>--::gKe—w—him into the Red-room, and say I shall be down presently," she said after a moment's hesitation. _ _ _</p>
        <p>As the footman disappeared she smiled at the memories the name recalled, and wondered how she could ever have imagined herself in love with this conceited little man. But then, she reflected, such an error of judgment was pardon- able in a girl who had not known the difference between liking and loving, until the fateful hour when the man destined to be her husband ap- peared on the scene. She could not imagine why. Surgeon-Major Ernestine, whom she had not seen since her marriage, should call on her; and she wondered if ho would find traces of the change which her fuller and happier life must have made on her. Before going down to him she glanced in the looking-glass at her face, naturally colourless, but transparently clear, smoothed down her soft, brown hair, and gave a final touch to the lace round her throat which was fastened by a little brooch formed of her own and her husband's initials in diamonds... . _</p>
        <p>Her meeting with Ernestine seemed a little awkward to both. As they exchanged _common- places she noticed that he had become much stouter, that his nose had grown red, that his lips looked horribly coarse, and she wondered how she could have thought him handsome. As he went on to talk of South Africa, from which he had just returned, she debated with herself as to whether it was her own standard of judgment or his appearance which had really changed. She felt relieved that he made no attempt to revive memories of their foolish flirtation, and did not even refer to the past; but gradually she per- ceived that his visit was paid for some purpose. At the mention of her husband's name her at- tention became alert.</p>
        <p>" Yes, thank you, he is quite well. You have just missed him. He would, I'm sure, hare been glad to see you," she said, doubting the truth of her last sentence. "Gone to attend a Cabinet meeting, I suppose?" "Yes."</p>
        <p>" It';" about that I came to see you," Ernestine told her abruptly, with the air of a. man who had not time to lose in beating about the bush. "To see me about the Cabinet meeting?" she said in surprise. "Yes. I want you to do me a favour," ho re- plied, decisively. "If I can," she answered, somewhat puzzled. "I am sure a woman so clover as you, can have no difficulty in doing what I ask," said ho, resorting to emphasis. She was sure his intention was to be ironical rather than polite, and, with rigid coldness, she asked what was tho favour he wanted.</p>
        <p>"It's generally believed," said he, lowering, his voice, as if he feared to be overheard, "that to-day's meeting will decide the Asiatic Loan business."</p>
        <p>_ She .nodded her head, wondering what was coming. "Whatever the result, red - tapeism will pre- vent its being publicly known before certain for- malities are gone through. The favour I ask is for you to learn the decision from your husband, and let me know, to-night, if possible," he said, with the deliberate air of one who had con- sidered his words. She looked at him in amazement and anger. "You suggest that I should learn a State secret from my husband in order that I-might betray it to you?" "That's what I ask," ho answered, with im- perturbable coolness. Ho placed himself in front of her as she went forward to ring the bell. "Listen to me before you order sue from your house ! " said he, in a voice that commanded. rather than entreated.</p>
        <p>"I don't wish to hear any more," she said, "Your refusal may ruin my life—and your own!" The words struck her like a blow, and whilsc she hesitated, he seized his opportunity, and continued, "What I ask cannot do you or any- one else any harm; and I swear to you no one shall ever know what you have clone. And think of what it will mean to, me! If I know the decision of the Cabinet., twenty-four hours before the general public, I shall be able to rig the market, put half-a-million or more in my pocket, and save myself from a scrape that otherwise will irrecoverably ruin me." Her blue eyes, usually so calm, glowed with anger and contempt as she looked at him. " Howr dare you make such a proposal to me?" sho asked, scarcely able to get her words out of her dry throat. "Because I'm desperate—because it means life or death to me!" he answered with an earnestness that impressed her. "Better death, than life with dishonour," she suggested. Oh, that's all very well on the stage; but in real life it's another matter. I have been a fool, and got into a horrible mess about money. If I can put it back within the next few days, no one but yourself will ever know about it; if not, I shall have to By, and take the chances of spending seven years in penal servitude. I tell you this to shew how desperate I am. The money I can make by the secret you can tell me will save me anti give me an inaepencienos for the remainder of my days. You can cla both without trouble and cost to yourself;" 1' Save t!ie betrayal of my husband—"</p>
        <p>"Even so, you owe me something." he an- swered, remembering the time when he believed herself and Ler fortune would be his; "for at one time I thought I should be to you what he is now." "Oh, I am sorry, so sorry that I misled you," she said, moved by self reproach. "But I was mistaken in my own feelings. I never really loved you." "Your letters made me think you did." "My letters," she said, a chill feeling of fear suddenly stealing on her, and creeping into her eyes. "I often read them, and always regret having lost the affection they speak of," said he, with deliberate slowness. " But surely you kept your word, and burnt them----" "Well, when it came to the point I found I couldn't part with them; besides," he, con- tinued, as he fixed his keen eyes on her with a meaning that could not be misunderstood, "I fancied they might prove useful some day." She would rather have died than appeared to dread him; so striving- to calm her fluttering heart and to assume a brave air, she said, "And I suppose you think that day has now come?" He nodded his assent. "What do you intend to do with then?" she asked, though she already guessed and dreaded his answer. "If you don't do what I ask, I will send some specimens of them to your husband, so that he i can ssee for himself he s not the only man who has been favoured by your affection." Foreseen as it was, the threat produced a fear that nearly deprived her of her senses; and for the first time in her life she wished she were a man, that she might be able to strangle this con- temptible scoundrel. Surgeon-Major Ernestine narrowly watched the effect of his words as he said, "It may, surprise Lord Ronald to know he was not first in the field." "I will give you all the money I have in re- turn for my letters," she said, striving to speak calmly as she grasped at the wild hope that suddenly dawned on her. "They are worth more than that. All you could give or get would be as nothing in com- parison with the haul I can make on the Stock Exchange if only you will let me know the de- cision of the Cabinet meeting. It cannot injure you or leave you a penny the poorer, and it can save roe and make my fortune. You'll do it, if only for the sake of old times." She drew back in disgust as he bent forward, "Never, never!" she said, resentfully. "Don't come to a decision so hastily," he ad- vised, in his deliberate manner. "A little con- sideration will perhaps help you to change your mind, and save yourself and me. When you do, please remember the sooner I have the news the better. Here is my card, with the address where I shall remain for the remainder of the day in the expectation of hearing from you." As she refused to take the card he held out, he placed it on a table beside her. Then, with a triumphant glance at her white face and frightened eyes, he' turned away, believing that he would conquer. When she heard the hall door close behind him, she sank into a chair, a. shivering, nerveless figure. Her terror at ErneS- tine's threats led her to magnify the importance of the effusive letters which she, a motherless romantic girl, in her ignorance of the world and trust in ins holiour, had written to him; and she lay appalled at the thought that Lord Ronald, who was the most fastidious of men, scrupulous concerning all that touched his honour, proud and jealous, should see them. An ordinary husband would not think the less of his wife because of her pre-nuptial flirtations with other men; but it was her pride and glory that he was unlike his fellows; that usage with ' the world had not lessened his chivalrous ideal of what women should be; that lie could not re- spect anyone of her sex whom he found wanting in dignity and reserve; and that he considered his own wife, the bearer of his name, and the mother of his children, as having always pos- sessed that exclusiveness which he revered. She therefore knew that the shock he would receive on reading her letters would fling her from the pedestal where he had placed her; and in her nervousness she believed that She would lose his affection—that he would turn from her, not only in disappointment, but in disgust. But not even to avoid such a calamity, or, what was more to her, not even to save him from the pain her effusions would give him, would she betray the confidences he gave her. Her punishment for such a slight indiscretion seemed out of all proponlion; but she must meet it. There was none to help or advise her, for she dared not trust any of her women friends, and her only near relative, her brother, was at the English Embassy in Russia. If Ernestine did not know she was unprotected and at hii mercy, ' he would not have dared to blackmail her; as it was, she was his helpless victim. Something of his hateful presence seemed to linger in the room, and to escape it she rose to leave, when she heard sounds of footsteps out- side. Almost faint from nervousness, she re- mained standing, her face turned towards the door, which opened to admit a broad-shouldered, ! loosely-built young man, with a ruddy face, . closely-cropped hair, keen blue eyes, a heavy reoustacife, and a robust air. "They told me you were here, and I came in unannounced," he said, as he advanced with •a swinging gait and kissed her. "Oh, Phil, what a surprise!" she gasped, as she clung to her brother. "Yes, I daresay; but when Rosy wrote to me about the sad death of her sister, I was anxious to come over and comfort her, poor girl; so I got three weeks' leave, arrived last night, put up , at Claridge's, spent the early part of the day with Rosy and her mother, and then dropped in , to see you," he said, rattling out his words like , a man who was always in a hurry. "How are they?" she asked, forgetful for the moment of her own trouble in thinking of the , grief of her old friends. "Terribly cut up," he answered, briefly. "I don't wonder. Poor Lady Cairngorm's death was so sudden." He glanced at her_ searchingly. as if he suspec- ted some inner meaning to lie beneath her words. "Heart," he said, nodding his head.' "It appeal-3,811e had a weak heart." "Poor woinan. Though she always appeared gay, I'm sure she was not happy." "I'm certain she wasn't. But what could you expect? She married that miser Cairngorm for his money, and tha old curmudgeon allowed her an income that would hardly keep an miner housemaid in gloves. Then she ran into debt, and he kept het' locked up in his Scotch castle nine months in the year. He hated her because she hadn't given hum an heir; she detested him because he was her gaoler. Poor Isabel, it's all over with her new." "It's very sad." "Well, at all events, Rosy will have a better chance of happiness with me small as my income is. I really came to England to persuade her to marry me right off, and come to St. Petersburg on her honeymoon." "And will-she?" "Lam to know to-morrow." "It's very noble of you, Phil." "Not a bit of it. I want to take her away from everything that reminds her of this—of her trouble." "There are few women without them." "Except yourself," he mit in. At that she burst into tears. "Oh, you cannot imagine how miserable I am," she =obbed. " Have Darby and Joan had a tiff at last?" said he, wonderingly. "Ronald is the kindest, the best of men —" "I know he is the flower of chivalry, the salt of the earth, and all the rest of it; but I suppose he has been human enough to curse his cook, or do something else equally dreadful?" "Phil dear, this is serious, and J. am wretched." "Then, in heaven's name, tell me what it's about. for I can't bear to see you cry," said he, scared by the look on her face. He could scarcely restrain himself whilst she I told him, breaking down every now and then. 1, When she had finished, he walked up and down I the room excitedly. "The beast, the scoundrel " he muttered fiercely. If a men ever deserved , hanging, its that ruffian. I could tell you more about him. He would justify lynch law. And I ahe has dared to blackmail you also," he said, ' suddenly standing still and closing his fists with a force that swelled their veins. "Can you help me?" she asked, looking up. I "Of course I can. Don't bother yourself any more about these foolish letters. I will hand t them over to you before the clay is done, or I'm more of a fool and he less of a coward than I supposed." "Then you will see him?" she said, sudden rp- lief coming to her. "I wouldn't lose the opportunity of seeing him I for five thousand pounds; and if I had it I would give as much more if it would guarantee me the satisfaction of strangling him without creating a I scandal. But we shall hava to act at once, be. I fore lie gets word of my being in London. You must make an appointment to see him this</p>
        <p>evening." "I fear that is hardly possible, for we are en. gaged to dine out to meet the Servian princes, and it's too late to send an excuse." "You can keep your engagement. It will be so much better that you and Ronald will be out of the way. I will receive him on your behalf," her brother said, with a grim smile. "You mean to—" "I will tell you all about it later on, when I shall also take the oppoitunity of rating you soundly for having written foolish letters to that Lomat. But women wlll never learn that by their irresponsible scribbling they have done more harm to the world than Eve did in eating the apple. Herein endeth the first lesson. Now I will go out and send a telegram in your name asking Ernestine to call at ten and bring the letters with him. He will think he has frightened</p>
        <p>you into betraying a Cabinet secret. Won't fie. be surprised? Now I'm off, but I shall be back in time to meet him. Meanwhile tcll your ser- vants to shew him in here when he calls. Get more colour into your face before Ronald comet back, keep up your heart, and eat a good din nor; and so au revoir."</p>
        <p>Precisely at the hour nanied in the telegrant Ernestine was shewn into the Red-room. Tho pint of champagne which had given zest to his dinner had also added confidence to his manner and a flush to his face. Taking his seat in the easiest chair he could find, he crossed his short legs, and hummed an air, tolerantly patient until- Lady Ronald came, and by a few whispered words placed him in a position to realise a sum that would free him from the clutches of the. law and secure an independence. The song he was softly singing pleased his taste as a skilled musician, and he was prolonging its final note when the door was opened, closed with a bang, and locked. When Phil Carrington had put the key in his pocket and turned round, Ernestine, was too much surprised to stir, and could only stare at the man whom he had believed to be safe in St. Petersburg. His entrance was alarming, the expression of his face even more so, whilst his words left no doubt of his hostility. "You scoundrel, you blackmailer, you didn't expect to deal with a man," said Phil, with a contempt that made his hearer's blood boil. "I came here to meet Lady Ronald," ex- plained Ernestine, immediately assuming ia de- fensive air, ' and at• her request." "Did you, though? Well, you will not be pleased to hear that it was I who sent you the wire, and asked you to bring the letters. Now out with them. Innocent as they are, I will not leave them in the hands of a snake like you," Phil said, as with a leisurely, swinging stride he came forward.</p>
        <p>Emestine's dark eyes measured this tall. loosely-built man, with his robust air and fierce expression, and certain misgivings for his own safety beset him. However, he was determined. if possible, not to part with the letters, and said, sturdily, "I haven't brought them with me."</p>
        <p>"You Hart lam determined to have them, and if you don't hand them out you may tempt me to kill you," said Phil. All the manhood left in Ernestine suddenly jumped to the surface, and, without reckoning on results, he struck out, hitting Phil under the chin. Almost at the same instant Ernestine re- ceived a blow on the ear, which sent him staggering. On recovering his balance, he rushed at Carrington, but, before he came to close quarters, Phil had grasped him with one hand by the throat and held him at arm's length, struggling aimlessly. "How I should like to choke you," said Phil, his eyes glaring with anger, his fingers tighten. ing their hold. "Don't tempt me too far; out with them, or by—" Ernestine gasped, fumbled at the tail pockets of his coat, and flung a bundle of letters on the ground. Then, flinging him aside, Phil picked them up, snd secured them whilst Ernestine gasped for breath, and wiped the perspiration from his fayie; his body shaking, his eyes glowing. "Oh, if I could only give you what you de- serve," said Phil, furious that this was out of his power "But, look here; you must get out of London as soon as possible." Ernestine started at the words, but made no reply. ",For if I meet you in any club,. I swear wilr knock you down; blackmailer and mur- derer that you are, you are not fit to mix with decent men."</p>
        <p>Always cautious Ernestine listened in silence whilst he arranged his collar and waistcoat that had been sadly rumpled during the struggle. Though terribly eager to discover how much his assailant knew, he was afraid to question or contradict him, and he believed that Carring- ton's impetuous temper would soon make him bluster out all he knew.</p>
        <p>"You ought to be in the dock," said Phil, and, unheeding the greenish pallor that spread over his hearer's face, he continued: "You ought to be tried for murder; for it was you who killed Lady Cairngorm, as surely as if you gave her the chlorofoim that sent her out of life."</p>
        <p>- Ernestine gave a gasp o_ relief, for nothing was said of the money he had embezzled. "Lady Cairngorm died of heart disease," he muttered. "An obliging family doctor certified that she did; but you know better. You know that to escape the scandal of her husband seeing the letters she wrote to poor Foster, that you stole from round his neck when he died of enteric fever at Pretoria, alid which she had not money enough . to buy from you, she took an overdose of chloroform. But I don't want to talk to a low beast like you. Clear out of this house, and out of London, too, or I swear —" Before he ended, Ernestine, straightening himself in the hope of assuming something of defiance, hurried towards the door, anxious to get away as quickly as possible, . For now that there was no hope of his being able to re- fund the sum he had embezzled, his one chance was to abscond. Already he decided to cross the Channel that night, disguise himself, and escape to Spain or America. When Phil unlocked and opened the door of the Red-room, Ernestine al- most rushed past him, and a moment later he was in the square outside, looking round for a cab to take him to his rooms, where lie would pack his belongings. Not a single conveyance was to be found in the square, but in a diverging street he noticed a. hansom trotting its leisurely way in an opposite direction. As there was no time to lose, he hur- ried after it, calling as he came near to attract the driver, who, hearing him, quickly pulled up. As Ernestine bounded on to the step the horse suddenly started forward and threw him. Then as he strove to move, he uttered an oath, and for a second the pain of his left leg made him al- most unconscious. Recovering, lie boldly caught hold of it, anxious to discover the worst, and realised that he had broken it.</p>
        <p>A sound that was a combination of laughter and imprecation came from his dry throat, as he realised that his fate was sealed. For nothing could prevent his fraud- from being' discovered within the next three drys, or could hinder its being brought home to himself. .In that moment ell the horror, disgrace, and suffering of renal servitude rose before him, and when a coule of policemen, summoned by the cabby. flfilved to convey him to the hospital, he felt as if already he was their prisoner.</p>
