        <p>1</p>
        <p>LA MARGUERITE. On Sunday last La Marguerite, one of the most luxurious and comfortable pleasure steamers afloat, made her first run to Llandudno, Bangor, and Menai Bridge. The day was simply perfect, and "with her usual gun-like" promptitude La Marguerite steamed into each of her stations just on the stroke of time, passengers being able to make their train and other arrangements to the very minute. There is certainly no more enjoyable two hours' sea trip in this part of the world than that experienced in a run to Llandudno on La Marguerite, for she is as handsome in interior decoration as the is steady and speedy, the catering on board is really first-class, whilst the four hours allowed on shore at the Queen of Welsh watering places may. be as varied and enjoyable us some of the most charming rock and seascape views in the world can make them.. On and after to-day (Saturday), La Marguerite will sail daily.</p>
        <p>[Ma END.</p>
        <p>WIRRAL AND BIRKENHEAD SHOW.</p>
        <p>The forthcoming show to be held on the 15th and 16th July, under the presidency of Sir Gilbert Greensll, Bert., promises to he even more attractive than any previous exhibition held under the auspices of this old' established Society.</p>
        <p>While the Council has arranged for the usual interest- ing competitions to take place each day, classes are also again being included for four-in-band coach and tandem teams, and prizes are again being offered for coach horn-blowing ; further, several new classes for tradesmen's turnouts, competition confined to Wirral,- for which a ten guinea challenge cup is offered, in addition to similar classes open to residents in the Society's district (which includes all parts within a radios of 25 miles of the stow yard) are being included in this year's prize schedule. Arrangem•ents are in course of progress for a grand military tournament to take place in the evening of each day after the jumping competitions.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0024" n="art0024">
        <head>BAND OF HOPE UNION. CONFERENCE AT WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>BAND OF HOPE UNION. CONFERENCE AT WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Temperance workers assesaaled in large numbers at Warrington on Saturday when the quarteely meeting in connection with the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Rope and Temperance Union was held in the Bald-street ,„ aesieyan School. The Union is a very useful asset to 'the temperance cause and evidence of its good work Was to be found in the annual report which was lo the hands of the delegates. It showed that ' the Diamond Jubilee year of the Band of Hops move- s'lent, 1907, had been crowded with activities. In all aervartments there had been marked advance. The , work in bairN of hope attained a higher level. Many • new secieties had been established. Tears had been a ' Welcome accession of district organizations of temper- anoe friendly societies. The work of seientifle temper- anceis teaching in schools surpassed all previous records. :naaa lessons vrere given, attended by 3,419 teaehers, a' ' 121 673 eller scholars ; 4,795 submitted essay I.e.Ports for which the Union presented 25,925 certificates • or merit. 92 of the lectures were given to students ia I 1, attendance at Evening Continuation Schools. Remark-1 ,nble approval had been testified by heal teachers, aasPeotars and other education authorities. The report '▪ mowed that some educatiora committees now engaged cstPerts to teach hygiene and temperance, subjects that aad received recognition from the Board of Elucatioa the last issued code. The most notable feature of I "• 6 Year was the Scientific: Temperance Camp iign, an effort to awaken the interest of the thonghtful and thought-influencing classes. For this Mr. W. N. -cadwards, F.C.S., had been engaged, aril visited ; • of the lsrgest centres of population. , Toe : n'rell4th of the Union was now 83 town and , district organizations, with 1,803 Societies, a member- achiP of 279,150, and honorary workers to the number of Through the efforts of several workers, in psPons n e to a appeal by the Treasurer, the subscription list had increased. There was, however, great need for further effort, es the General Dapartment showed an overdraft of £758, and the Temperance Teaching in Schools' Scheme £1,e47. Indeed it had become , been , ssary to devise special means to liquidate these I difficulties, and to provide for the continu ince of the ' Wysork.Attention was specially drawn to the fact that the allien's Subscription List for Band of Hope work did ot reach £5OO, or less than one-hnndrodth part of the ysnil°l spent on intoxicating drink every day in the union's area. AN INCREASE CAMPAIGN. The general committee met in private tinier the Presidency of Mr. Henry Thornton, of Warrington, oh:Orman of the Union. There were present representa- tives from 74 town and district organizations. The linsiness was largely routine and concerned with the administration of the Union. Amongst the items of pnblic interest was the consideration of the proposal to Inaugurate on Saturday October 10th a campaign for the Purpose of increasing the membership and fonds of a e eountv erg tnization which now comprises 89 town and district organisations with over 2009, individual °ands of hope and nearly 300,003 members. A number °f lady vice-presidents were appointed. A welcome Iscas extended to the Blackburn, Heywood, Manchester, aPuth-West Manchester, Southport and Warrington aistricts of the Independent Order of Rechabites on snitling the Union. They represent a total of 397 Rechabite tents with a membership of nearly 17,009. A resolution was adopted thanking the County Borough Zsild District Education authorities for the welcome lue3' had given to the Union's scientific temperance 'ectures. The committee saw in such welcome hearty !cognition of the utility and desirability of temper- ance teaching and felt the time bad now arrived when T-loh teaching should form part of the regular corricii- ula of all schools. It was resolved to appeal to Parlia- aklna take tand to the President of the Board of Education e steps to ensure that systematic instruction in "Ygiene and temperance be provided by the responsible education authorities. PUBLIC CONFERENCE. BAND OF HOPE PROBLEMS AND THEIR SOLUTION. Following the committee meetings public conference to which local temperance workers, Sunday and day aohool teachers, clergy and ministers were invited, was "eld in Bold-street schoolroom.—Mrs. James Dodds (president of the Warrington Band of Hope Union) presided.—Amongst others present were the Revs. A. 11. jitchell R. E. Letheren, J. F. Matthews, Miss Bessie Raton, Messrs. H. Thoraton, J.P. (chairman), T. E. .liallswerth (hon. secretary), W. Chandos Wilson (seers- e.l.3) W. G. Townend, J.P. (Mayor of Middleton), E. , Bainbridge, J.P. (Ashton-under-Lane). T. Baldwin (Colne), Amos Clegg (Manchester), J. It. Gibson (Fleet- wood Councillor E. Ilaitsworth (Eccles.) Apologies vhad been received from the followine among. others : aaa• Canon Hicks, M.A. (president), J. S. Higham, LP, (treasurer), H. Knott, J.P. (vice-chairman), C. B. ?rierson (Preston), C. Kerr (Bootle), J. Young (Sand. The towns and districts represented were : 4,teoringtoo, Alirincham, Ashton, Barrow-In-Furness, 13_1?Aliburn, Bolton, Barnley, Bars:, Coaster, Chorley, le:lather:as, Coins, Crewe, Darwen Denton, Dukinfield, saieeles, Farnworth, Fleetwood, 'Glossap, Haslingden, aleYwond, Hollinwood, Hyde, Lancaster, Liverpool, Ja.Feleetield, Manchester, Middleton, Morecambe, pacalro, New Mills Northwich, Oldham, Oswaldwistle, ~s,am, Preston 'Radcliffe, Ramsbattem. Rawtenstall, ~ 0„-e‘ucRie, Rovto'n, Sandbach, Salford, Sale, South- /1;a Stalvbridge, Stockport, Todmorden, Upholland, /iPstorl, Walliden, Wallasey, Warrington, Whiteaeld, innn, Winsford, Sze. Mrs. Dons said she deemed it A great honour to reside over a meeting like that for she was told there There 74 representatives from other Unions. (Applause.) euere Was no doubt that union was strength and that vrily they had such a splendid gathering of workers afternoon. She was glad to think they were there 41: the cause of the children who would be the men and ta°,l3len of to-morrow and who, they. trusted, would themselves heard and felt in shaping the destinies 01 this country. (Applause). Tethe Rev. 'l'. H. HUNT, of Bolton (Connexional mimPerance Secretary of the Primitive Methodist asrDiurch) then read a- paper on " Some Band of Hope chlems and their solution." He said the one great floblent and the beginning of their movement was : to promote the Cause of total abstinence? There a s then a great fight against ignorance, prejudice and vjulgar habit. To overcome those amongst up-growus Well nigh impossible. An easier, more effective, 3aou more expeditious way was to begin with the Dr ling—and so during more than half a century it had a_uOved. The success of their movement was innestioned but that very succese created new larems as it created new opportunities and further a, 'Miens. The number of Band of Hope Societies aun„aaindoubtedly large, but probably one half of their Ish,""Y schools were still without them. In some as„ces where bands of hope were once strong they had ha„'arne weak or had ceased to exist. In some parts the ""`t of hope meeting had dwindled into a mere enter- tainmentbai with the maximum of amusement and the the Turn of temperance instruction. In order to meet in_ situation, the first thing they must do was to ofnaatnrata. Every Sunday School should have its lar!nd Itßa"4ll°P6 If it meant to be a protective and progressive solo"- It was a very poor excuse for managers of °ls to say they had no time, funds, or workers. of ‘h that spirit they never would have, and the funeral asauch a school could never be long delayed. A Sunday )3,1i?ool that had not spirit enough to start and sustain a I,7autd of hope was a soulless thing and there was nothing sucha I'o4 to die. If he had to carve an epitaph for Rai a school he would chisel very deeply "To die is used' though though not in the sense that the phrase was first (Laughter.) The initiation of a