        <p>no means the least significant pan ti. support of the Licensing, Bill. Toe agitation had only been male possible by the adulated santirnent resulting from the work done in the bands of hope and temper- ance societies irt years past. There was a time imme- diately before them when they would have to re-consider their methods, re-dolible their energies and adapt them- selves to new .11Ilki incremingly important conditions. When the Licensing Bill was through—it had got to go through—(applanse)—that measure would not solve the probl3n of intemperance. It would only help to a solution of Some of the c vises and there would lie upon the to tchers of the temperance move- ment the necessity of applying themselves to a recogni- tion of the fact that total atstinence bad to be again, as it was at the outset, the object of all their work. Let them prepare for the time when the time-limit would have expired and the future of licensing in this country would depend upon the character of puhlio opinion. In Lancashire and Cheshire they would have to consider what they as federated workirs were doing to bring the power and impulse of their organization to bear in securing the formation of a band of hope in every place where there was none. They would have to improve -the character of teaching in their bands of hope, making it not less enjoyable but more educational. Thy had not merely to shake hands with themselves and say" Well done. A big thing done in the past." They had to realize that a bigger thing remained to be dons in the future. (Applause.) Mr. J Iseph Owen (Warrington), proposed and Mr. Platt (Bury) seconded a vote of thanks to Mrs. Dodds for presiding and to Mr. Hunt for his paper. Miss BESSIE Durrox, in supporting, said it was a cause for rejoicing that they had so useful and welcome a lady as Mrs. Dodds in the chair. Mrs. Dadda was always glad to do what she could, and she did its) well and so kindly that they ought to be very hearty in their thanks to her. Mr. Hunt's paper would prove moat useful. She felt she could add nothing to it, and she was sure she would not like to take anything from it. Let them all ponder it in their minds and work and live by it. She (Miss Dutton) *mid always be glad to do what she could to help on the cause of temperance Mr. H. THORNTON said they were all delighted that Mr. and Mrs. Dodds were heart and soul in the move- ment. The vote was heartily accorded. Mrs. DODDS, in responding, expressed the grati Heati on of the Warrington Band of Hope Union that so many friends from other towns had attended. With regard to band of hope meetings, she believed that the young people should have the best speakers, singers, and raciters. They would never know the influence that children took from the meetings into their own homes. All who tried to help forward Church work knew that the greatest hindrance they hail to fight was the drink. She felt strongly that the greatest power they could have in temperance work was personal influence. The Rev, T. H. FLINT pointed out that in the United Kingdom there sere 34,033 bands of hope. Thera were about 23,033 places of worship belonging to the Free Churches and in addition they had to consider the Anglican and Roman Catholic churches. Ho therefore thought they could take it for granted that his state- ment that only half the Sunday schools had. bands of hope was accurate. 7ed by</p>
