        <p>were an old-fashionei as and he was al.. sumers would have to go on paying them a little longer. A reduction of 2.1. or 3d.in the price of gas would,how-. ever, compensate them. Alderman EVANS seconded. Councillor STOTT said he hoped they would not give the retort house hands 31. extra and expect them to do work equal to Gd. (Laughter.) As to the disposal of gas profits, he suggested that all consumers who lived in houses assessed at less than £lO a year should have gas free of charge for a year. (Laughter). The minutes were approved. THE WAFER, UNDERTAKING. In moving the adoption of the Water Committee's minutes, Alderman EVANS said they could not report a profit but they had the satisfaction of knowing that the deficit was £250 less than last year. It would have been still smaller but for repairs to engines. He had every confidence that in a short time the balance would be converted into a credit one. THE ELECTRICITY AND TRAMWAYS. The MAvon moved the adoption of this Committee's minutes and submitted the financial statement for the year ended March 31st. The gross profit on the tram- ways dopartment was £8,343 and after paying sinking find and interest charges there was a balance of £2,579 —an increase of £1,270 on last year. The working expenses had decreased one half penny per mile chiefly owing to less consumption of current and reduction of cost of repairs to track. The income from Sankey, Latchford and Stockton Heath routes had incrassed,the</p>
        <p>present moment at a infection (as the Education would have them believe) than the Health Committee proposed to place their school inspector. Where, he asked again, was the senti- mental objection of Mr. Jolley? Was not the danger of infection greater than in the present case, and should not pauper children and pauper patients have protection at least equal to those children whose cause ho now so loudly championed? (Applause.) He for one was at a loss to understand how this sudden wave of senti- mentality had arisen and seemingly overwhelmed the majority of the co-opted members of the Education Com- mittee. There was one point which stood out prominently throughout the whole of the controversy, and it wag this fact which seemed to rankle in the mind of his friend, Mr. Jolley, and that was that the Corporation should have been so foolish as to take over the administration of this Act themselves. He might mention that among many other towns who had been similarly foolish they had London, Manchester, Birmingham, Portsmouth, Leeds, Bradford, and recently their own great neighbonr, Liverpool. • Councillor BARTON seconded. Councillor BROADHURST said that as a parent he had the strongest objection to his children being medically examined by a medical man who lived in the hospital. He asked the Health Committee whether it was in the best interests of the people that the resident medical officer should examine the children. Councillor STorr said the Act was supposed to have been in emotion. for the last five months, and . .</p>
        <p>t largeskincreace being £473 on the Latchford section. The ,idcome from the Cemetery and Longford routes had decreased. They had saved something like £3OO in adopting a now electric light. The outstanding debt was £83,543 and the reserve fund amounted to £4,110. The gross profits in the electricity department amounted to £7,533 and after paying sinking fund and interest charges there was a balance of 4213, being £303 less than Last year. The income from current for private consumers had increased by £1,315 but the income from the tramway supply had decreased by £3lO. The working expenses had increased by £1,172, chiefly owing to the increased price of fuel. Out of the profits £2,030 had been handed over to the relief of rates and the balance carried to the reserve fund. The outstanding debt was £83,053 and the reserve fund amounted to £7,105. The department, con- tinued the Mayor, was being worked at an oat almost as low as any station in existence. He believed Warring- ton stood third or fourth in the lowness of working expenses. Councillor WITAKINSON seconded. Alderman BOLTON said he hoped the Committee would consider the electric light consumers and give them full value for their money. Sixpence per unit was too much. The minutes were approved. WARRINGTON BRIDGE. ALDERMAN BENNE OBJECTS TO THE</p>
        <p>yet Warrington had practically done nothing to carry it out. He had not much to say against the Education Committee objectify' to the medical inspector residing at the Aikin-streeChospital, but he did object to the way in which the matter was brought before that com- mittee at the last meeting because in effect it meant that the meeting of the Health Committee were deliber- ately agreeing to something which was dangerous to the health of the children attending the schools. He,there- fore, resented the interference. (Hear, hear.) He would much prefer living in the administrative block at the hospital than in Battersby-lane or Liverpool-road. Alderman BURTON said the Health Committee had been backed up by many medical advisers in the country, including their own energetio and painstaking medical officer and Dr. Joseph, who was an authority on these matters. There were some dwelling-houses only eleven yards away from the wards in which favor patients were lying, whereas the block in which the doctor would have his arirtmants was five times that distance away. Mr. Jolley's motion was simply sentimental folly. Alderman BOLTON said the Education Ocimmittee's contention was that no risk should be run, and they therefore did not think it right that the inspecting officer should reside in the Infectious Diseases Hospital. He did not support Mr. Jolley in the manner in which he brought the matter .forward, as four-fifths of what he said had notaing to do with.the.ques.tion.</p>
        <p>Alderman TONto:c said some hard and 'unkind things were said about him at the Education Committee meet- ing, and he regretted that he had not the proper pro- tection of the chair. He should support the amendment of Councillor Evans because he thought that this was a matter in which the Health Committee bad intended to consult the medical inspehion sub-committee, and that a scheme could be evolved which would be satisfactory to both committees. Councillor BALL said the Education Committtee bad a right to express their opinion on the views of the Health Committee. AlthAgh he did not think there was any fear of infection—he accepted the opinion of Dr. Joseph—they had to remember that unless they had the sentiment of the people behind them they were not going to achieve the object they set out to. They were going to create a feeling of resentment among a large number of parents against medical inspection if the doctor resided in connection with the Infectious Diseases Hospital. Councillor Fur.ToN-SAwrit,replying to the discussion, said if ever there was_an object lesson in the degrada- tion of debate they had it last week and that night. They had had the introduction of personalities in questions which should ,m treated according to their merits and the subject under discussion. He hoped Alderman Tinnion, when be came to be a chairman and had unruly members to control, would appreciate the difficulty of letting a man return blow for blow. A great deal had been made about friction between the two committees. He objected to the word friction, because the Education Committee had, in as graceful a manner as they possibly could,agreed that the Health Committee shonld appoint a medical officer, although it was their opinion, and his own present opinion, that the Board of Education never intended the Health Committee should media the appointment. Therefore he did not argue that point now. All they wanted was to have some communica- tion with the officer appointed, because all the informa- tion had to be given direct to the Board of Education, and they would hold the Education Committee respon- sible. Tho TOWN CLERK explained that the resolution passed by the Education Committee was not binding on the Council ; it amounted merely to an expression of opinion. • There voted for the amendment : The Mayor, Aldermen Pierpoint, Tinnion, Forshaw, Evans, Ellison, and Burton, and Councillors Wilkinson, Sankey, Evans, Btrton, Slcelland, Ashcroft, Wilson, Chantler, Toole, Cunnington, Dickenson, Steele Stott and Hawthorn- -21. Against : Aldermen Bolton and Bennett and Councillors Singleton, Rogers, Roberts, Broadhurst and , Ball-7. I The amendment was carried, and with this exception the Education Committee minutes were passed. ' HEALTH COMMITTEE'S RESOLUTION. TO MEET THE EDUCATION SUB-COMMITTER. The Health Committee at their meeting on May 12th, considered the Education Committee's resolution and resolved, that this Committee meet the Education Sub- committee to discuss the matter and renew the offer ' contained in a resolution passed bx them for a similar I meeting on the 22nd ultimo, communicated to the Director of Education on the 27th ultimo. I Councillor EvAss, in proposing the adoption of the I Health Committee minutes, said the matter had never gone further in the Health Committee than the sugges- tion and if a conference had been held there would have</p>
      </div>
