        <head>(f 'oppruhl.)</head>
        <p>(f 'oppruhl.)</p>
        <p>THE HAND OF FATE.</p>
        <p>BY ADELINE - SERGEANT, Author of " Caspar Brooks's Wooing," &amp;c.</p>
        <p>"I calls it the dead man's hand," said the sexton. "Why a dead man's hand 9" Michael Grimond asked. "Dead men's fingers don't point, as this hand on the stor.o is pointing." "Ali, there lays the point of the story," said the sexton, wisely. He was sexton and clerk of a lonely little church in the Fens. It stood on a slight eminence, with a green church7ard sloping away from it on three sides, and a high wall backed by a meadow en the fourth—which happened to be the north side, and where only a narrow footpath divided the vestry from the wall. It was here, close to the vestry door, that the two men were standing; and they were looking at a stone, at the corner of the buildingon which was rudely carved the figure of a pointing band. It seemed to point towards an iron i grating n the church wall, and Michael Grimond, who was a comparative stranger to the sexton, Inspected stone and grating with considerable interest.</p>
        <p>"Vi hat's the story?" he said at last. "It be all about bygone days," said Enoch, the sexton. "Tha knaws, mebbe, that there was a thirre when country-side was ravaged by the Daanes, and such bike?"</p>
        <p>"I've heard of it," said Michael, briskly. He was a tall, dark man, with piercing dark eves, a dare-. devil air, and a look of keen intalagence, nt =mingled with cunning. "This here church was standing then, as it stands now," said the sexton, "and it had a gradely store o' goold plate an' cups an' mcogs, rings set wi' precious stoans, goold coins, too, an' jools for the Vargin's neck---w "What became of them all ?" asked Michael. There was a greedy light in his dark eyes. "I'm coornin' to that, savin' yer pleasure," said the old man, a trifle sourly. "In one o' the raaids, as they called 'em, of these 'ere Daanes, they sacked th' church and robbed it of as many things as they could foind; but th' owd priest of th' place, he'd buried the best of 'em in a girt stoan coffin as stood down theer th' vault."</p>
        <p>Ife pointed to the iron grating, inserted in a low stone arch in the side of the church, and Grimond observed that it had a padlock and formed a door giving upon a flight of steps, leading, as Enoch had indicated, to the , vaults below the church. He nodded, and stood listening, with his hands in his pockets, to the rest of the sexton's story. "The enemy found it ne'ertheless," said Enoch. "And when th' owd-priest fowt for it, they killed him, and then fell to fighting with each other, and their men found 'ern both :dead beside the coffin arter all. But the English foalk was upon 'ern, and they'd nobbut time to drag the bodies away, lvavin' th' maain part o' gowd behind 'em. An' then they found that their chief's right hand was a-missing, and they said he'd left it down theer, clutching at th' gowd which he couldn't heft away." "Was it ever found?"</p>
        <p>"The hand? oa—nor th' god neither. People searched for it auver an' auver, and they still say as it's hid in one o' them stoan coffins clown the steps there, with the skelington band a•clutching "Have you never been down to ace?" said the younger man, laughing a little boisterously. "Not I, Mr. Grimond. , You Fee that hand cut on the stoan ? Some foalks say it's to point the way, and some foalks say it's of the devil's cutting. For it's towld as bow more than one has tried to find that gowd, and has found it; but no sooner found than lost; for the dead man's hand has flown at his throat and throttled the thief afore be couti get off wi' th' spoil." "The devil guards his own even in the tomb, I see. Come' Enoch, on your honour, haven't you turned over the coffins down in the vault, just to see if the treasure wasn't there?"</p>
        <p>Ile expected a denial, but, to his surprise, the old man seemed both confused and angry, and jingled his keys noisily as he replied: "What business' is that o' thine, I'd bike to knaw! If I had found owt, 'twould be for passon to taake it—not me. It's Church property, and the dead man's hand 'II throttle him as taakes what doan't belong t him." Then he shuffled away, and left Michael Grimond thoughtfully contemplating the grated opening into the vault and the rudsly sculptured hand upon the wall. _</p>
        <p>"This is an odd coincidence," he muttered to himself. "I'm not superstitious, but I come of a race of credulous folk. My old granny had a fancy for reading the stars and the lines of one's hand, and she always said that I was fated to find a hidden traasure. Who knows but what she may have been right, and that my opportunity has comb?"</p>
        <p>Ile laughed over his reflections as though anxious to persuade himself that he knew their foolishness and futility; but there was still a speculative gleam in his eye as he glanced again at the grated door. Then he turned once more to the carven hand. "Old 'Enoch calls you a dead man's hand," be said, apostrophising the figured stone as if it could hear. 'You are too much alive for that, I think. So you have gone on pointing to the vault for all these centuries of years ! If I were to penetrate the mystery of the coffin, and find the buried treasure after all, what should I call you? The Hand of Fate, I think, and not, as the stupid villagers name you, the Hand of Death." He hesitated a minute, then approached the grating, and after casting a glance around in order to make sure that Enoch was not in sight, he stooped and grasped the ironwork in his strong, supple fingers, with the result that, as he shook it, the corroded bars almost gave way, and the gate nembled on its rusty hinges. It was very plain that small force would be needed to detach it altogether. Michael smiled to himself as he rose to his feet again. "Some time when Enoch is out of the way I'll explore a little further," he said. "I should not wonder if there were some pickings among those old stone coffins and oak chests. Now I think of it, the old man once shoved me a gold coin—a rare one, too—which he said he had found in the.church- yard: Churchyard, quotha! Grave-clothes and dry bones, ri ter! I wonder no antiquity-hunter has found out the place." He walked slowly away, lingering a little in the hope of catching Enoch once again; but the old man had retired in some dudgeon to his house, and Michael Grimond therefore turned his attention to other things. He was a comparative stranger in the village of Crosby, and had given himself out as a travelling artist, but did not seem industriously inclined, for he spent most of his time at The alehouse, or wandering about the lanes, making love, at intervals, to the village innkeeper'c pretty daughter, Dulcie Bennett. The girls of 'the place were captivated by his dark eves, his swaggering manner, his- clothes, and his jewellery, both of which were of the cheap and flashy order; but their fathers, and brothers, and sweethearts looked sourly on the stranger, and murmured to each other stones bear- ing on his vicious habits and his profane and licentious tongue. Nevertheless, it was plain that he was a man of some reading and education, and of varied experience; but money—save what he won at play in a neighbouring town—was not plentiful with him; and ru•tics have no opinion of a man win has little money and yet "sets up " obe a gentleman. It was nothing to Michael Grimon3 that Dulcie was promised to arother suitor; he had no duties towards Will Fielding, as he said insolently enough when someone questioned him on the subject; if Fielding could not hold his own, and Dulcie preferred Michael to Will, he did not deem himself responsible. But of late an unlooked-for obstacle had raised itself in his way, and his brow was dark with the remembrance of it as he came through the churchyard' gate. Presently his face cleared. He had caught sight of a slim girlish figure, crowned with a broad straw hat, from which a cloud of short curling golden hair escape], forming an aureole (as he well knew) around a dainty girlish face. Dulcie was the prettiest girl in the district,and Michael shewed his good taste, as well as his determination, in choosing her. A few brisk strides brought him rtr to her side, and she returned his salutations with laughing face. "'Where are you going, if I may be so bold as to ask?" he inquired. "You never want for boldne-s," she answered saucily. "I'm going to see-my aunt, who lives at the other end of the village." "May I come with you?" "I can't prevent you," said Dulcie, darting a coquettish glance at him out of her pretty eyes. She was a bewitching little thing, and a saa flirt— as Michael knew; but he believed that behind her flirting ways there was a real preference for himself. He walked with her to her aunt's door, waited for her till she came out again, and then accompanied her back to her father's house. A zest was added to his enjoyment by the fact that they passed Will Fielding on the road, and that while he frowned and started, Dulcie blushed and tossed her pretty head. "flow black that fellow looks!" Grimond said. "He must bare a devil of a temper."</p>
        <p>EIFFEL TOWER LEMONADE. This article is undoubtedly the most delicious, cool, refreshing drink obtainable, so healthful, so convenient, so cheap-41c1. per bottle,</p>
        <p>" He tas a very zaa rcn-p-^, lata Mee DUlCie. "But 1 lijiuk niost (1) Lave." "I haven't," said Michael, at;tl: eiously. "You'vs cured it. If you wouH le uyv, ife you should lead ine in-a leash cf,silic." •</p>
        <p>"Ah, but that's out of the queElicin, you know. Father says so," relearned Cult i, mith the sweetest of roguish smiles. Muuatin it v, liu ith a cl anEe of counlenanCd which did not speak well for Lis boasted temper, Michael seized I;er Ly the aim. "But you don't E. ay so—if your fAther cousentes% you have said that you would he mina?" • °I don't think I ever said so."</p>
        <p>"Don't trifle with ire, child. You know how I lore you. You have let me think that you—that you loved men return." "I like you very well," said Dulcie, dropping-her eyes, "but--" "You like me—that's enough. And if I could get your father to say yes--" "But he never will," said the girl pettishly. "What's the good of talking about it? You know he says that you have no settled means of living, and that he would sooner see me in my grave than tramping about the world and drudging for any man. Not that I should mind," she added, with a suspicion of tars in her voice—"if I liked the man." _ _</p>
        <p>"You darling!" said Michael, appropriating the reference to the man whom she "liked in a way that she had never intended. "Well, I'll make your father consent—see if I don't. And you shan't be a drudge, either. There are a good many ways of making money besides painting twopenny pictures. You leave it to me."</p>
        <p>Dulcie looked at him with a half-frightened air. "I don't want to be married at all," she said. "And father 'll never give way." There was a little-touch of triumph in lur tone: perhaps she did not actually want her father to give way. But Michael would nct understand that. He took her round the waist and kissed her, almost in the middle of the road, and swore a great oath that she should be his and no other man's, by fair means or by foul. Dulcie escaped from him, panting and blushing, but more ashamed than gratified; for, as a matter of fact, she was very fond of IVill Fielding, and but for a passing quarrel with him would never have condescended even to flirt with another man.</p>
        <p>As Michael lingered in the road, half-Lopi' lz per- haps, that she wouli return, the burly innkeeper came to the door and stood there,with arms akimbo, his big white apron shining in the semi-darkness of the summer evening. Michael accosted him, with his usual assurance.</p>
        <p>"Ah, you're there, are you ?" said the innkeeper, in no well-pleased voice. "Well, sir, I'd advise you to keep out of the way; there are one or two people not very friendly to you in thisrpart of the world." "For Dulcie's rake, I suppose." -"You're very free with my daughter's name,young man."</p>
        <p>"Look here, Mr. Bennett," said Michael, lowering his voice, and speaking with sudden earnestness; "is it because I'm poor that you don't want me to marry your daughter?" "That reason 'll do as well as another."</p>
        <p>"If I had a few hundreds in hand and more corning in, that would make a difference, I suppose?" "I'm not saying but what it would. I don't want m 37 girl to throw herself away on a beggar. That's what you are at present, by all accounts, and so you'd better clear out and make way for a better</p>
        <p>"Is Fielding your better man? He shan't have her, if the -devil or man can prevent it," cried Miaael, angrily. And his loud tones penetrated to the little room upstairs where Dulcie was watching behind her white curtains, and made her tremble like a leaf.</p>
        <p>"He's got substance," said Bennett, slowly. "Whcn you can slim me as good a banking account as he's got, my lad, you may talk to me about Dulcie ; but not before." And he went back to the lighted bar-parlour, while Michael flung away in a , rage. • He lodged at old Enoch's house; and in the course of tl:e evening he tried to get the sexton to talk to him again about buried treasure and the stone coffins 'in the vault. But Enoch was in a bad temper, and refused to be sociable.</p>
        <p>"Ah tell 'ee what 'tis, young man," he burst forth at last with a growl of wrath; "if there be treasure down there, you'd best let it lay. That theer dead hand houlds it and knows bow to keep its own. Why, Ah've felt it at my throat as plaan as could be—but that's neither here nor Ulcer. It's got a mass on it, that goold bas—if it be theer."</p>
        <p>"The old sinner knows it is there and wants to keep me off it," was Michael's not unnatural reflec- tion. "What a set of dunderheaded fools the villagers must be! I daresay he scratched that hand on the stone himself, in order to scare other folks away. There are more gold pieces where the one on his mantelpiece came from, and if they are to le found, I'll have them—dead hand or not. Gad! what a hand of fate it might prove for me!"</p>
        <p>Ile relapsed into silence over his pipe, and spoke not another word; but his bright, keen eyes were busy wandering over the implements of Enoch's trade—over the pickaxe, spade, and lantern—and his mind was equally occupied with a consideration of certain smaller and sharper tools which might be required in a midnight expedition to the old church vault. Perhaps there was something in his silence which seemed significant to Enoch, for when it was time to go to bed he said, with portentous solemnity: "What was hid in the church belongs to the Church. It's what passon calls sacrilege to take Church things for private use." "There's a good deal of sacrilege in the world, then, old Enoch," said Michael pleasantly. "But I don't much believe in your buried treasures—it's a nice little story for the village folks to hear; but it dosen't make much impression upon me." Enoch shook his head warningly, and said no more.</p>
        <p>At midnight something roused him. He did not know what it was. Afterwards he declared that he had heard a voice calling to him to rise; but the parson always maintained that this was a figment of old Enoeki's imagination. He tottered to his feet, made his way downstairs with- out quite knowing why, and then, to his immense astonishment, discovered that the cottage door was open, and the night air blowing in. He struck a light and investigated the room with care. Had thieves been there? But what was there for thieves to take? And yet they had taken something—for the sexton suddenly recognised the fact that his pickaxe and his lantern, and, moreover, the church keys, were gone. To run upstairs to his lodger's room was the work of a moment. There was no one else in the house, for the wcman who "did " for him went away at night. As Enoch had instinctively divined, the lodger's room was empty. Grimond had gone out. "To the church' as Alfm a soul," said the old man, seizing his clothes and hastily donning them. catch him at it, by the Lord, Ah will!" .</p>
        <p>So saying be snatched up a stick, and with determined mien, although his limbs still trembled, he bent his steps towards the church. It was a dark, stilt night. But in the vault below the sacred building there was a faint reddish light; and now and then the dull stroke of a pick fell upon Enoch's ear. His heart faltered more than once; but be constrained himself to creep nearer and nearer, until be was close to the loosened grating of the vault, when he bent down and saw-- What did he see? Michael Grimond, indeed, as be had exrected; but Michael changed by greed and avarice into the likeness of something evil. He held a lantern high in his hand when Enoch first saw him, and the rays of light fell on his face, with its distorted features and staring eyes, and upon the contents of a great stone chest or coffin at his feet. It was with difficulty that Enoch restrained him self from dying out aloud. Michael had found the long-buried treasure—so much was evident—and it shone with dazzling reflected light even in the feeble rays of the lamp. Broad gold pieces there were; barbaric ornaments in gold and emcialds; platters and cups of precious metal; jewelled crucifixes and altar-vessels—all that the priests of old could find, in thEir haste to save the treasures of their church from the marauding hands of northern pirates. The precious heard had remained undisturbed from that day to this, although its tradition lingered still— and perhaps had saved it from thieving hands. Even Enoch, although he believed in its existence, and had prowled about the vaults in the hope of caning across it, had never penetrated the secret of that closed stone ceffin of which Michael Grimond had , raised the lie. Enoch watched the finder as if fascinated. A ' strange; and ominous smile was fixed upon Michael's lips; a curious glitter shed itself between his half- closed eyelids. After a little pause he stooped and drew out one of the gold ornaments: it was a neck- lace, with roughly-set red stones that drooped from the rendants like drops of blood. And then Grimond laughed aloud. "This will suit Dulcie's white near," he said, and crammed it into his pocket with satisfied haste. "Old Bennett won't object to me now, I fancy. What's that?" It seemed as if he had heard a sound, and be glared around so fiercely that Enoch drew back into the shadow—afraid to encounter those hungrily burning eyes. Hearing nothing, however, Michael again bent him to his task. Coins and ornaments lie 4 handled with jealous care. With the least bulky he stuffed his pockets full, and disposed others, about his garments. Enoch was astonished to see bow many treasures could be secreted about ono man's person—it looked as if Michael bad had previous experience in biding things away. But till, petrified with fear and amazement, the old man did not dare to move. At last Grimond appeared startled. He set down his lantern beside him, and fell to pulling at some- thing which seemed wedged between the coins. He got it out at last, held it up, and flung it from him fiercely, as if the thing repelled and horrified him in spite of himself. It was blackened bony thing, on which the dried skin hung in strips which moul- aaraa as tura.. fell.-,.the last . remains Qf a ehastls7</p>
        <p>---</p>
        <p>tletZ.</p>
        <p>stiectaclerfice' Guided ith treasure-I.:a ' man's hand! Then Enoch found voice. The memory of the legend gave him strength. lie rose ,to his full height and uttered a hoarse. cashing crv. "The hand! The nano:- he said. "The hand that protects its own! Michael Grimond, the dead hand is upon ye—bare a care!" Grimand started up: he was deadly pale, and the cold sweat stood in drops upon his Lrow. From the .Icok of terror on his face it was plain that, for the Moment, he regarded the sexton as no earthly visitant, and believed that the dead pirate of old had returned to guard his ill-gotten gains. He stumbled, and stumbling tell over his lantern and extinguished the light. tie must have grasped at something for support or defence; for the next moment there was a tremendous .crash, as of a heavy mass of stone falling to the ground, and a great cloud of duet and Woken masonry almost smothered Enoch as he groped his way in terror -to the door. Ile fled to the belfry, which was lucidly open, and grasping the rope cf the great bell between his hands. he sent a heavy tolling sound anon the silence of the summer night. In five minutes hall the villige folk were at the thurch. And there they found the body of Michael :Iriniond, crushed beneath a heavy slab of stone that had fallen upon him as he clutched at the side of the half-ruined vault, while the treasure that be had striven to steal was wet with his life's blood. But Enoch maintained to the last day of his life that there were purple marks of strangling fingers on the robber's throat, and that he owed his death less to the fallen stone than to the vengeance af the dead man's hand. Verily and indeed, for Grimond. a Hand of Fate. The treasure went to a museum, and Will Field- ing married Dulcie fiennett: but the story of Grimond's end is one that will never die in that little village among the Lincolnshire fens.</p>
        <p>ITllli END. 1</p>
      </div>
