        <p>"EXA MINER."</p>
        <p>GENERAL PRINTING WORKS</p>
        <p>In addressing the students SONGS—Goon at the Warrington Training AND BAD. College on Monday evening on the subject of the teach- ing of singing to young people, Mr. ARTHUR SOMERVELL, B.A. Miss. D c., Inspector of Music Ito the Board of Education, indulged in some criticisms of modern songs and passed not a few strictures upon present-day lyrical verse, which call for some consideration and examination. Dr. SOMERVELL'S object was to emphasise the import- ance of teaching children to sing only good music, and, as is now pretty well known from annual repetitions of the argument, he strongly favours a foundation being laid of the national songs of the country in the minds of young scholars. This is, of course, an altogether unobjectionable idea ; indeed it is one to be distinctly commended as tending to foster that love of folk-song which is recognised as a fine trait in the characters of the peoples of the earth. But it was, we submit, something of a mistake for Dr. SOMERVELL to cry down in a wholesale and uncompromising way the more serious songs and ballads which must eventu- ally succeed these simpler tunes as the pupil reaches maturity, for it surely cannot be pretended that the national songs comprise the sionntuin bonunt of vocal music of this or any other country. Dr. SOMERVELL took an all-round survey of the pro- duct of the song-writer's art. The comic song and the music-hall ditty of these modern days, though bad, deceived no one—they were frankly and admittedly vulgar, and could therefore be avoided as witch. But in speaking of the wide field of serious songs, and pirticularly of the sentimental or drawing-room ballad, is it not an exaggeration, to say the least of it, to condemn unreservedly all such as musically dangerous to the community ? These songs are, we are asked so believe, " a pile of awfdl trash " by which hundreds of thousands of people are " taken in " because they think that in singing them they are singing good music, when, as a matter of fact, they are not. While agreeing that a large proportion of the sentimental songs of our time possess little merit in either the verse or the musical setting, it must, on the other hand, be admitted that there are many such songs in vogue of which we, as a music-loving nation, have every reason to be proud. So sweeping a denunciation as Dr. SOMERVELL sought to make was most unfortunately, nay unhappily, backed up by allusions and by specific instances. In these, moreover, the discovery is made- that, though the work of the cornposer iscondemned by implication the verse-writer it is who is the real culprit. ADELAIDE A. PROCTOR'S " Lost Chord" came, in ' this connection, under Dr. SoetenveLn's dissecting knife in quite a pitiless way. If a singer " weary and ill at ease" were tempted to go to the organ for solace, would he or she, Dr. SOMERVELL asks, pull out the noisiest stops, for that is what is supposed to have been done when "fingers wandered idly over the noisy keys." Then there is, the speaker tells us, the impossibility to be considered of striking " one chord of music when, in order to sound like a "grand Amen," it would be necessary to use at least two chords. Nor could Dr. SOMERVELL imagine a person musical enough to play beautiful chords on the organ not being able to find them again, much less could he conceive the possibility of an unmusical person being able to strike any chord at all worth listening to. Now it may be fairly asked whether this is the right spirit in which to criticise a great song—a song which has held the premier position in popular estimation continuously ever since its inception over 30 years ago,—a song, too, which, perhaps more than any other, has profoundly moved in a good and whole some way the millions of the English-speaking race who have delightedly listened to it? Can we, in fairness to either the:authoress or the composer, deal thus literally with the text of " The Lost Chord "? Is there, we may ask, no such thing as reasonable poetic license, and does it require any great intellectual effort to discern that what is , meant by "a chord of music " is probably some illusive yet inspiring theme upon whieh the organ- player happened, and in which he or she found comfort and support. SULLIVAN'S masterpiece of song-writing has always been regarded as a I distinctly original and pioneer effort, preceding a long succession of kindred things of that " Consumptive Chorister "and " Ora pro nobis " order at which Dr. SemEnveLn railed with some show of justification. Simdarly Cowner's "The Children's Home," another fine pioneer song, was we take it, alluded to in a very proper denunciation of the numerous songs that have followed it embodying the banal story of the poor little match-selling beggar girl who is ultimately translated to Heaven in a cloud of triplets in the pianoforte accompaniment—always and necessarily triplets, as the speaker put it, though, it should be noted, this last little shot misses fire in the particular song which we have instanced as the forerunner of many which merit Dr. SomEnvELL's condemnation. But because plagiarisms of this kind are only too much in evidence, with the inevitable effect of nauseating a good theme, and because present-day good sentimental songs are largely intermixed with others bad and indifferent,a general condemnation Ofalall such as mere ,rubbish argues at once a lack of due discriminative power and that sense of refined judgment which might be ex-</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0036" n="art0036">
        <p>pected to be possessed in a high degree Iby a man holding Dr. SOMERVELL'S position. Curiously enough Dr. SOMERVELL is him- self known as the composer of at least one greatly admired song, and that, too, of a distinctly sentimental kind. Yet we should hesitate to con- demn so beautiful a vocal norc2au as "The Shepherd's Cradle Song " either by pretending to understand its verse literally or by regarding its musical setting as other than a most happy effort to clothe the charming imagery of the words with equally charming music, while the idea of placing such a song along with a hundred others in the category of useless musical rubbish is, of course, unthinkable.</p>
        <p>These two well - known MR. DODDS AND antagonists have once again MR. ROBERTS. crossed swords in public controversy, and the fact is sincerely to be regretted. Only a few weeks ago people were led to believe that the breach was healed by what occurred at the great Licensing Bill demonstration in Parr Hall. Moreover the continued eftlrts of such respected toWns- men as the Revs. John Yonge, C. Harvey Cook, Mr. C. E. Parker and Mr. E J. (best, were being put forth to find a basis of agreement between Mr. Roberts and Mr. Dodds. Seeing that the Non-Trading issue was at once raised by Mr. Roberts, it is not surprising that the strenuous attempts to bring about peace utterly failed. It was impossible for Mr. Dodds to recede one single inch from the position he main- tained in regard to the principle of Non- Trading, and it would have been sensible on Mr. Roberts' part if he had admitted and accepted the fact. The past cannot be " wiped out,' but it might have remained quietly buried had not Mr. Roberts thrown discretion to the winds by his letter to our contemporaries last Saturday. Instead of peace, he presented the gentlemen who ware mediating on his behalf with the point of the sword. It was a characteristically ruthless land inconsiderate act which can only redound to Mr. Roberts' discre4it sad discomfiture. In another column Mr. Dodds gives the facts in their proper sequence, with the result that the state- ments of Mr. Roberts quite fail to hold water. On reflection it will probably be painfully apparent to him that he has adopted the worst possible method of vindicating his honour.</p>
        <p>Prior to now we have MEDICAL refrained from commenting INSPECTION. upon the pretty quarrel between the Town Council and the Education Committee with regard to the important question of the medical inspection of school children. Both are agreed on the supreme necessity for carrying out the Act, but tiny part company on the methods to be employed. The Council wishes the work to be done by an assist- ant medical officer who will reside within the administrative block of the Infectious Diseases Hospital, and thus save the £3OO per year which would be required to maintain a special officer. The Educatiou Committee absolutely refuses to agree to the medical inspection being carried out by a doctor "from the hospital." Expert opinion is conclusive on the point that there would be no danger from such a practice, but the question of parental sentiment is exploited to tho full by the educationists. The unfortunate thing is that the , sentiment has been created by the squabble. Had the work been inaugurated quietly on the lines suggested by the Council in all probability it would have gone on smoothly and without any nervous tremors on the part of parents. But the agitation fostered both inside and outside the I Education authority has aroused parental alarm, and the probability is that the C )uncil will in the end have to consider sentiment before business. There is certainly a difficulty now, and it will have to be faced. Oa Tuesday the Town Council showed a stubbornness with which we have some sympathy, but it has to be remembered that , " circumstances alter cases."</p>
        <p>The sorrow felt by Warring- GENERAL Bora.mts ton people on the death of that distinguished warrior, General Sir Redvers Bailer, is heartfelt and sincere, it is but fifteen months since the General came to our town to unveil the soldiers' memorial in Queen's Gardens, and the events of that day will not readily fade from the memories of the townspeople. Sir Redvers won all hearts by his kindly, mild and affable manner, and by the inspiring words in which he paid a tribute to the departed heroes and dwelt on heroism in every- day life. There was not the slightest semblance of ostentation in his bearing and the warm interest I which he showed in the veterans who had mustered in honour of the occasion is now touchingly recalled. Whatever may have been his indis- cretions, General Buller never lost the admiration of the great mass of his fellow-countrymen and he was the idol of the men in the ranks. His bravery gained for him the Victoria Cross, and the soldiers who fought under him, whether in the sunshine of victory or under the clouds of temporary failure, never lost their confidence in the bluff, genial commander whom they regarded as a soldiers' general. He always gave his best to the work he had in hand, and it is a tribute to his straightforward and generous mind that he is one of the few Englishmen who have had to do with the 'administration of Irish affairs who have left behind them a memory cherished by Irishmen. In the capital city of his own county Devon, to which he was deeply attached, the singular honour was paid him some years ago of erecting a statue to him during his life. It is also significant that General Louis Botha and General Smuts have publicly acknowledged their esteem for the late Sir Redvers and their high opinion of him as a skilled and stern fighter. And an authority states that every Boer as every British soldier who was there, declares that the hardest•fought and most deserve fly-won battle of the whole South African War was that of Pieter's Hill, won by General Sir Redvers Buller, V.C., and in which our own South Lancashire Regiment played such a worthy part.</p>
      </div>
