      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0100" n="art0100">
        <head>POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</head>
        <p>THE POLITICS OF THE WEEK.</p>
        <p>BY OCR LONDON CORRESPONDENT. In politics there are spells of certain subjects just as there are in meteorology spells of weather. A few Ireeks ago nothing was heard of but Licensing Bill. Par a time there was a stir about Education. At present, mainly owing to the straggle to get the Budget Bill through, the political atmosphere reeks of finance and fiscalitis. A BROAD BASIS. . The Government majority keeps well up through it all. On Tuesday evening the Opposition mustered 124 for an amendment to the Finance or Budget Bill, 'while Ministers secured 307 votes, or another " thumping majority " of 213. The amendment was a "tactical " criticism of the Budget, intended to pro- mote Tariff Reform ideas without defining them too strictly. It is enough to show its extreme vagueness simply to state that it was one on which Mr. Balfour could speak without coming down from the fence which he generally mounts when Tartiff Reform is raging, on which Mr. Austen Chamberlain and Ur. ponar Law could preach Fiscal Reform without haring to explain exactly what it is, and which Mr. Bowles, a pledged " Free Fooder," could support without any strain upon his conscience. The main Purpose of the amendment was to express regret that in view of the growing liabilities of the nation, no attempt had been made to- increase the resources of the nation by broadening the basis of taxation. This fine-sounding phrase touched the sense of humour of the House of Commons. It may probably lead to a tew political nickname—the Broad-base's. The Government, who ;,11 seemed remarkably happy at the C,ose of the debate, are content with a basis as broad as 213. THE TARIFF REFORMERS' DILEMMA. It is not easy to see how the Tariff Reformers can survive Parliamentary debates of this kind; and it looks as if the impetuosity of the modern Protectionists were going to spoil their own game without much trouble to the Free Traders after all. Mr. Balfour was wiser than his party when he made a point of jading discussion of the tariff question in the last arliament. Every set debate in the House of Vurnmons (or as was evident last week in the House of Lords either) exhibits the Opposition in an rinfavour- able and even absurd light, simply because they are 1,11 a dilemma. They cannot both be explicit and united hey cannot answer questions at all, because they can- all answer them in the same way. All through thC debate, from Liberals and from Labour representa- three, came the challenge to the Unionists: "Say hat you mean by broadening the basis of taxation. "ay what you would tax. Are you going to tax corn or meat or dairy produce ? " AN ANSWER THAT COULD NOT BE GIVEN.</p>
        <p>As s - - p •• --- - ---- in revious debates, so in this, no answer could be given. It is an unhappy position for a statesman .I°,hea he cannot give a straight answer in debate. There is no cleverer man in England in getting out of a tight place than Mr. Balfour; he delights in dialectical exercises ; but even Mr. Balfour cannot "Pe with the difficulties of this situation. He is Obliged to hesitate and to flounder, so that it is a strain to follow him. On the other side there is propor- tionate decision and clearness and vehemence. The r'rinae Minister on Tuesday night was triumphant and el'ell contemptuous in his answer to the Opposition. Ris task was too easy for him. Besides, he was just ", little indignant that opponents with such a bad nnaneial record should criticise and lecture him on rtaligate finance; and this gave his speech a direct- ness and a fervour which contrasted most favourably with the hesitancy of his assailants. It is not only in nnraliAers that they are handicapped, but in debate, adien the Opposition are inveigled into a Tariff dis• nnssion. INCIDENTS ANP HRASES. „ Speakers on the Tory side being so much handi- Pped, it is not easy to do justice to them, or for them to do justice to themselves. It was very inter- e6tlofi, however, to note Mr. Balfour's chivalrous 1 , references to Mr. Churchill and Mr. Lloyd George. , 'xis allusion to Mr. Churchill as a fellow-sufferer who . ramanded his sympathies alike in his triumphs and • Is failures, was very pretty, considering how little . loreV can lie between Mr. Balfour's party and their ~, Zilliant renegade, as they must regard him. As to . ;tr. Lloyd George, it has often been noticed that Mr. even in the days when he had to submit to , fierce. attacks from him, paid him the compliment of : TelunBe° attention and undisguised admiration. On thissday night, Mr. Balfour made public confession of I.,„___ admiration, while reproaching him good- 'o'''"nouredly with having too ' clinging reminiscences ! i •,,, ? happy time as a guerilla chief below the gang- It was touches like these that gave to Mr. 1,?• I°lll''s speech its main interest on Tnesday,together tb tbe amazing profession that questions of colonial Preference and dumping ought not to come into the 1 present debate. It was the latter declaration,no doubt ,T,„at, suggested to Mr. Asquith the bantering remark, ea° Colonial Premiers are gone, it is true, but are tPY so soon forgotten ?" Mr. Balfour's speech was *lite enough to Mr. Lloyd George's.</p>
        <p>MR. CEIURCHILL'S SPEECH.</p>
        <p>Churchill's might have received even more -auee. The new President of the Board of Trade traCIWB daily in debating power. He was especially PPY in his way of expressing the economic fact that . a tariff is a costly business, taking, perhaps, sixteen rur illions from the consumer to yield only ten to the akvealle. Mr. George having said this before, Mr. e 1, t'orch ill had to enforce the same argument without / . ating the words of his colleague. He therefore Zia Pared the accumulated wealth of the country to a treservoir, and remarked that every outlet from the ,‘ ieservoir had its own leakage. " Multiply your out- and you multiply your leakages ; and we believe at our old-fashioned and carefully considered fiscal eys ti3trt has resulted in the collection of the maximum ve°aPible revenue with the least possible waste in the ta,,tlrs,e of collection." Mr. Churchill's definition of Waueaing the basis of taxation is also noteworthy. t"leans that we are to " make the poor pay more." a , another phrase, which Mr. Balfour noticed by ',,z,a°t,ation, Mr. Churchill said it meant that taxation Ishatt'd " press more heavily on the threadbare should press of the poor." ' OLD AGE PENSIONS. , 13 The publication of the Old Age Pensions Bill has a,r,englit this long-talked-of relief to the aged poor into Cit„al sight at last. The question that arose during •undget debate as to the position of trade unionists tia.tinpssession of superannuation allowances of ten hags a week is settled by the provision that the must not exceed hiwarleant;!silhe Pensioned person pounds five shillings a year." This leaves e ,anperannuated trade unionist with ten shillings a e.elz quite safe. He will not be disqualified for the east pension. It is stated, however, that there is at 1., one Union giving eleven shillings a week. The , re of this amount of superannuation will not under the benefits of the Bill as it at present ! °Land's. Nobody can mortgage his pension, and in - ajlas of bankruptcy it cannot pass to creditors. The age pension is a man's" very own." 1 • AFTER WHITSUNTIDE. ' t The shortest possible Whitsuntide holiday is given ~ i! ,legislators this year, only three working days, five aruding Saturday and Sunday. This is inevitable reter the long recess at Easter owing to the Ministerial eonstruction, and in view of the fact that the House going to rise at the end of July. There will be an thaPunn Sitting, but the few weeks between now and e ' end of July will be politically for the Liberal "one relvded hour of glorious life." There is an enormous , arse • 'lnt of good work going on in the Standing Com- . niatt i • ees, Bills like the Children Bill, the Irish Univer- ' ;altie.e Bill, and the Scottish Education Bill, making sePl,d progress. The output of social legislation this seealon promises to be far greater than has e'er been atrnnlislied before. At Birmingham, on the 18th I me 10th instant —Birmingham, its original home— National Liberal Federation will hold its meetings • "4 10 er to the Prime Minister it assurance of "hearty 4,4 Y.I support " in carrying " to a triumphant the legislative and executive work to which they ittsyte set their hands," and its encouragement to read- as t tb,' relations of the two Houses of Parliament so niallifi‘ prevent the verdict of the constituencies being • :. Doll ed by the action of the hereditary and irres- aible Chamber." . •</p>
        <p>Warrington Agricultural Show. tt,r--e 19th annual show of the Warrington Agricul- Atal Society rill be held in Walton Park,on Thursday, glist 13th. Prizes, cups and medals to the value la:d7aoo are offered. Saturday, August Ist, is the for entries. Ti h LYES have a tired sensation, if troubled light abitual headache, if the eyes cannot stand the pt the sight tested by JOHNSTON WATSON, 110eiv Specialist, 84, Bridge-street, Warrington. '"arges. Satisfaction guaranteed.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0101" n="art0101">
        <head>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</head>
        <p>NOTES AND NOTIONS.</p>
        <p>The brewers will somi ba convinced that the Heavens are leagued to compass their ruin, should the Licensing Bill fail. During the thunderstorm on Wednesday night Cobb's Brewery, Margate, was demolished by lightning, one side being cut clean away.</p>
        <p>* * * A meeting in support of the Licensing Bill is announced to be held to-day "on the New Brighton Sands, Red Noses." A correspondent suggests that this is carrying the war into the enemy's camp with a vengeance. He hopes, however, that the "red noses" will not be too prominent, otherwise there may be trouble.</p>
        <p>* * * Political compacts have a way of ending unhappily. There was one at Macclesfield, by which the parties agreed to equally divide the aldermanic honours. On Tuesday the Toriss broke the compact by elevating one of their own number, when the seat should have gene to the Liberals. As a protest, Col. Brocklehurv.t, M.P., who is an alderman, and fifteen Liberal members left the Council Chamber in a body. * *</p>
        <p>One of the effects of Empire Day : Extract from an Empire Day essay by a L.C.C. child (aged 7) : There are a lot of Empires, like Chinese Empire, Hackney Empire, Stratford Empire, and Russian Empire. Hackney Empire is different to ours because they sing there, and ours is places. The distinction between the two classes of Empire is admirably defined ; and it is further emphasised by a second child, who says : "The British Empire belongs to us, you can go fiee."—" Punch." * * * Malik; most distinguished soldiers, Sir Redvers Buller came of an entirely uninilitary family. His ancestors had for centuries been landowners in Devon and Cornwall, whose chief occupation had been farm- ing and sport, and it was as the squire-farmer that he was best known and loved in the West country. It will be remembered how faithfully Devon stuck to him in the dark days when half England was execrat- ing his name. A Conservative orator who was address- ing a gathering of Devonshire labourers at that time said it was not the Government they should be attack- ing, but the Generals, and especially Buller. This was the end of his speech, for his incensed audience stormed the platform and be had to flee for his life. * * *</p>
        <p>When the news of the battle of Waterloo arrived at Warrington—then, as now, a thriving place —the chief magistrate of the town declared that he should add to the projected illuminations by burning his own house down, and be actually did so. It seemed that the house was an old one, mostly wood, and that his worship had previously contemplated dismantling it and building a new one. But his example (says the " Liverpool Post and Mercury"), was contagious, and the owner of a considerable block of what would in these days be called "slum " property, happening to be a political rival of the other incendiary, burnt the ancient tenements as a counter-attraction. Unfor- tunately, a big house not contemplated in the scheme took fire also, but the crowd and the authorities, thinking that was all part of the show' did nothing to put this fire out, and even restrained the owner as a mad drunkard when he danced about, exhorting them to put tho fire out.</p>
        <p>o • The fusion of the two schemes for a national memorial to Shakespeare having taken place, and parties being now agreed that a national theatre would be the most appropriate tribute, the questions arises what a national theatre ought to be, and where the money is to be obtained ? The first is by no means easy of solution. For one thing (says the London correspondent of the " Liverpool Post and Mercury ") a national theatre should not be made the instrument of producing plays which no manager will put on the stage. It may be right that it should sometimes give dramas which are not likely to hit the popular taste and which yet show originality and power. But it must not become a receptacle for failures. " Nor should it be turned- ieto a mere Shakespeare house, where nothing except plays of the great dramatist would be heard. In a national theatre should be given the works of greet excellence, both native and foreign, rarely seen on the English stage. As to the question of money, that will depend partly on tho extent of the scheme and the readiness of men of wealth to assist. It is tolerably certain that no grant will be given from the Imperial Exchequer.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0102" n="art0102">
        <head>LOCAL NEWS.</head>
        <p>LOCAL NEWS.</p>
        <p>Where to go for the. Holidays!</p>
        <p>Next to the weather this is the all-important question. If you have not decided perhaps our advertising columns will help you. We publish weekly more advertisements of seaside and country apartments than any paper in the town or district. Refer to page lof this issue.</p>
        <p>NORFOLTi SUITS from 18s. 6d.-BAncs,Outfltter.-Advt. Local Wills The will of the late Mr. Hobert Anderton Naylor, llendip Park, Langford Churchill, Somerset, formerly of Cuerdea Hall, Theiwall, has been proved at 124,084. The will of the late Mr. James Mort, of Lymm, bas been proved at £O4O.</p>
        <p>The Territorial Army. Colonel J. A. Edelsten, secretary of the West Lancashire Association of the Territorial Force, sends particulars of the 'Strength attained by the various units of West Lancashire Territorial Force at the end of the second month. The total number that West Lancashire has been asked to provide is 18,252, and already 10,070 have joined the force, so that 55 per cent.of the total numbers required have been reached in the first two months. The total strength of the 4th Battalion South Lancashire Regiment is 517. Accident in Bridge-street. • •</p>
        <p>William Lloyd, 0, Monks-street, a carter in the employ of Mr. Llynekyme, butcher, Market-street, lies seriously ill in the Infirmary as the result of an i accident n Bridge-street on Saturday afternoon. Ile was sitting on the side of a cart in company with a man named Sidney Grounds, when in changing his position he fell and one of the wheels passed over his body. Ho was conveyed in a cab to the infirmary, suffering from a badly crushed abdomen. Bewsey-road Wesley Guild Presentation. A pleasing ceremony was witnessed on Monday evening in connection with the Bewseproad Wesley Guild,when the occasion was taken of presenting to Mr. W.Healey, the Guild secretary, a handsome mahogany clock,accompanied by two vases of Doulton ware. These had been subscribed for jointly by the members of Mr. Healey's Sunday school class and the Wesley Guild as a recognition of his many services and also in commemoration of his recent marriage. Mrs. Pitt in making the presentation complimented Mr. Healey on what he bad been able to accomplish in con- nection with the Guild. The Rev. S. Pitt also spoke, I mentioning the progress the Guild has made under Mr. Healey's efficient direction. These remarks were heartily endorsed by Messrs. Cade, Corbett, and I Sinclair, Mr. Healey responding on behalf of himself and his wife. Despite the inclement weather there was a good attendance and a short ramble was after- wards taken, the route being through Bewsey.</p>
        <p>CELL ULAR SEEIRTS from 2s. 6d.-BANKs,Outfltter.-Advt Property Sale. Mr. Leonard Bennett conducted a sale by auction of, house property and ground rents at the Blue Bell Inn, Horsemarket-street, on Wednesday evening. Lot 1 consisted of six messuages or dwelling-houses, Nc s. 2,4, 6,8, 10 and 12, Warburton-street, off Winwick- road, Warrington. There was no bid and the lot was withdrawn, but it is expected that 4 private sale will be effected. A similar course had to be taken in the case of lot 2, consisting of five messuages or dwelling-houses, Nos. 14, 10, 18, 20 and 22, Waiburton-street. Lot 3 consisted of six ground rents of £9 98. Gd., £2, £4 10s., £4 10e., £2 58., and £8 per annum, tuising out of land and messuages situate at the corner of Jockey-lane, Long- shaw-street and Hawthorne-street, Warrington. After ' a good competition it was sold to Mr. A. H. Clive for £195. Lot four consisted of eight ground reuts of £5 7s. 2d., £8 108.,19 4s. 8d.,13 Os. Bd., £O, /9105., .g. 5 12s. Od. aud ;CO per annum, wising out of land and messnages situate in Lovely-lane, Cartwright- street, and Bostock-street, Warrington. Mr. F. J. Carlisle's executors became the purchasers at £220. J. Longton was solicitor to the vendors.</p>
        <p>Exhibitlon at the Parr, Hall. Many of the leading firms of the country are repro- gsented at an interesting trades exhibition which opened in the Parr Hall on Tuesday afternoon, and will continue until the 12th inst. Enterprising manufac- turers readily avail themselves of such opportunities of bringing their productions before the public, and a visit to the hall is both entertaining and informing ' Much taste and originality is displayed in the arrangement of the stalls, and the effect is , bright and pleasing. —Wadsworth's, 59, Sankey- street, are, as in previous exhibitions, well to ; the fore with an admirable collection of pianos. One of the most artistic stands is that of Van Houten. , It is in the form of a miuiatnre refreshment saloon, I perfectly and artistically fitted up. The world-famed Van Houten's Cocoa is served at a penny a cup, this ! including a biscuit .and a dainty serviette. Another stand which is sure to attract attention is that of Price's Patent Candle Company, Ltd., of London, Liverpool and Manchester who exhibit a splendid stock of toilet soaps, candles, tapers, ac. Their chief specialities are Price's "Itegina " (Queen ! of toilet soaps), Price's "Court" toilet soap and Price's ! "Buttermilk " toilet soap. An accomplished ladies' orchestra gives m‘ist enjoyable concerts each after- noon and evening and there are numerous other : attractions. In the evening the illuminated fairy;: fountain presents a beautiful spectacle. There are cookery lectures daily from 3 to 4 and 7 to S by Miss A. M. Tomlinson, N.G.T.S.C. The exhibition is under the capable management of Mr. W. Goodall. BLOUSES, a grand selection from Is. 11A.— BANKS, Outfitter, Horsemarket-st.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0103" n="art0103">
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June 6, 1908.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0104" n="art0104">
        <head>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</head>
        <p>NOTES ON GAMES AND ATHLETICS.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN AND WIDNES.</p>
        <p>RUNCORN URBAN DISTRICT COUNCIL.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting was held on Wednesday, Mr. T. I Dean, J.P., presiding. The Medical Officer reported ; that during May there were 25 deaths and 48 births, as compared with 39 deaths and 54 births in May, 1907. The committee submitted a scheme for the orgauisation of the fire brigade.—Mr. Pringle, chairman of the com• mittee, an reply to Mr. Posuett, as to the condition of the are-engine, said the Council might give it to a museum or sell it for old scrap. (Laughter.) The local branch of the Amalgamated Society of Carpenters and Joiners wrote protesting, against the contract for the stalls at the market beiag given' to a non-union firm.— The communication was referred to commiteee.</p>
        <p>CORPORATIONS AND THEIR SINKING FUNDS.</p>
        <p>WIDNES BILL OPPOSED.</p>
        <p>I Many members of the House of Commons have received letters or telegrams from town clerks soliciting their vote against the motion put down by Mr. Ridsdale to recommit the Whines Corporation Bill on third reading in respect. of the clause which would empower that body to use their sinking fund in lieu of exorcising borrowing powers when such were granted. But not a few members (says the " Times" Lobby correspondent) are disinclined to adopt the view taken by corporations in regard to this matter. It is sug- gested, with point, that a corporation would scarcely desire to use their sinking fund in this way if the cor- poration stock were well above par. On the other hand, such use of the sinking fund might doubtless commend itself to a corporation if their stock were below par, though whether such action is in the public interest is the point at issue. As the third reading of the Bill has been postponed, it is hoped by some that in the meantime a committee may be appointed to investi- gate the practice,</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0105" n="art0105">
        <head>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</head>
        <p>EARLESTOWN AND NEWTON.</p>
        <p>Cricket. FORMATION OF A FISHING CLUB AT Local cricket enthusiasts will learn with pleasure that EARLESTOWN. Mr. James Dodds has accepted the presidency of the A meeting was held recently at the Prince of Wales I Hotel, Earlestown, of several enthusiastic anglers, for West Lancashire Cricket League in succession to the late Colonel Pilkington, of St. Helens. For his age, the purpose of forming a club. It was agreed to there is no more energetic out-door man in the district establish one under the name of the Earlestown than Mr. Dodds. Angling Association. It is intended to apply for Newton Lake as the chief fishing water, being close at hand, and convenient for the members. Several prizes re already assured for fishing competitions, &amp;c., and it Warrington's match with Preston at Arpiey on Satur- day was notable for the first appearance in the home I!. eleven of the Rev. Horace Gray, the headmaster of the is hoped that a respectable and social club will be th Grammar School, who gave a brilliant display, his result of the project. innings realizing 49. His chief hits were eight 4's. The ! • I match ended in a draw, Preston making the substantial "RINGING THE CHANGES." I total of 203 for five wickets and declaring, whilst At Liverpool intermediate Sessions on Tuesday, Warrington had compiled 132 for 4 wickets when play : Frederick Edwards (20), labourer, admitted having at ceased. j Earlestown, on April 23rd, stolen two shillings having, ' Edwar'd Curry and Ann Gutteridge.—For the prosecu- tion Mr. Greaves Lord explained that the money was obtained by "ringing the changes." In one case prisoner asked for the change of a shilling, which he secured without giving the shilling. In the other case he complained of the thickness of a shilling which he received, and he got another one, but did not return the one he had previously received.—The Chairman said the prisoner had been pursuing a career of crime for years. He was addicted to obtaining money by means of a trick, and he was a very dangerous character. He must go to prison for twelvemonths, with hard labour.</p>
        <p>In the Warrington and District League, Rylands' Bros. triumphed over Bowsey Wes luaus, E. P. Sanders making 43. St. James' had not much difficulty in dis- posing of Wilderspool Brewery J. F. Forster being the top scorer with 17.</p>
        <p>There were two Warrington and District Junior League encounters, Orford being vanquished by St. Mary's and Sankey accounting for Holy Trinity. The feature of the latter game was the fine batting of J. Bradburn, who made 47.</p>
        <p>Secondary School went down before St. James's, whose chief batsman was H. Ditchfleld (20), while L. Walker captured nine wickets for seven runs.</p>
        <p>WANTON DAMAGE BY BOYS.</p>
        <p>Six boys named John Herd, James Hardman, Samuel Archer, Edward Mather, Joseph Jones and Henry Maddison were summoned at Newton last Thursday for doing wilful damage to property belonging to Messrs. R. Evans and Co. in Station-road, Haydock.—Mr. Peace, of Wigan, prosecuting for the Company, said the damage done by young hooligmas on Sundays at Haydock had gone beyond the bounds of mere mischief and the Company had to take steps to deal with it. Two joiners had to be kept employed repairing the damage thus done. In the present case P.C. Sinclair found the defendants in the yard of an unoccupied house ; 22 coping stones had been pulled down and 100 tiles thrown about. The boys were throwing bricks at a door. The damage was laid at 10s.—P.O. Sinclair having given evidence to that effect, the Bench ordered the defendants to pay 7s. Gd. each.</p>
        <p>Monks, Hall and Co. accomplished a smart perform- ance in their match with Plank-lane. Their opponents batted first and declared their innings at six o'clock, having made 82 for 8 wickets. The forge men passed this score in 33 minutes, A. E. Steele batting finely for 30 not out. Monks Hall 2nd also registered a victory at the expense of Plank-lane 2nd, the majority being 27.</p>
        <p>The feature of the Municipal Officers' Guild match with Walker's Brewery on Saturday was the splendid batting of Dr. Smith who made 50. Perkins bowled well, capturing seven wickets for 27 runs. The muni- cipal officers triumphed by the narrow margin of 5.</p>
        <p>Latchford Baptists gave Frodsham 2nd a severe trouncing. For the winners E. F. Cliff° and James Cox batted splendidly for their respective scores of 53 and 50 (not out). T. Gorst was the only visitor to reach double figures, 27 being his contribution to a total of 40.</p>
        <p>ACTION AGAINST AN EARLESTOWN JEWELLER.</p>
        <p>In the Chancery Court, at St. George's Hall, Liver- pool, on Tuesday, before Vice-Chancellor Leigh-Clare, a motion was brought by Messrs. Thomas Russell and Sons, watch manufacturers, Church-street, Liverpool, to restrain Mr. William Jones, pawnbroker and jeweller Earlestown, from representing or passing off or seeking or trying to represent or pass off as the plaintiff's watches which had not been manufactured or sold or supplied by the plaintiffs.--Mr. Stuart Deacon (instructed by Mr. 11. G. C. Day), who appeared for plaintiffs, I stated that defendant had accepted an inter- locutory iujunction, restraining him from pass- ing off watches not manufactured by, the plaintiffs, as the plaintiffs' watches, and was now willing to submit to a perpetual injunction and pay the costs. The plaintiffs felt that they had been suffering a great deal of injury in consequence of cases such as that agairst the defendant.—His Honour. (interposing) said it was unnecessary to go into details. Plaintiffs' might rest content to take the order, and if they suffered further injury as a consequence of the similar cases, they could make proper representations. They could write to other tradesmen and say that they had got an injunction against one, and they would proceed, if necessary, against others.—Hr. Stuart Deacon : We shall be quite satisfied with that, your honour.</p>
        <p>Sankey Wire Mills were victorious over Knotty Ash by a majority of 23, J. L. Hawthorn being their top scorer with 25. The second eleven also registered a win at the expense of Knotty Ash 2nd, the feature of the game being C. Hawthorn's flue innings for 50 no out.</p>
        <p>AN EARLESTOWN QUARREL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0106" n="art0106">
        <head>Cycling.</head>
        <p>Cycling.</p>
        <p>Chester seemed enveloped in quite a holiday mantle on Saturday last. Whether it was that it had not yet settled down after the excitement of the recent Royal visit, or from the fact that the Warrington Cycling Club were amongst them of course it is impossible to say. Anyhow, the twedty odd who made the journey spent a very enjoyable time in the old city, which was reached towards five o'clock, a section of the members, not being satisfied with the twenty mile of direct road, taking a circuitous route, and travelling via. Dalamere Forest. An excellent tea was partaken of at the head- quarters, the Hop Pole Hotel. After a few slight showers in the evening, which served to lay the dust, the return journey was commenced, tyre troubles being very conspicuous during the ride home.</p>
        <p>At Newton Petty Sessions last Thursday, Thomas Bash was summoned for assaulting Arthur Jenkinson, on May 16th, in and near the New Market Inn, Rules- town.—Plaintiff said Bush accosted him in the public- house, said he wanted him outside, and would give him something to go on with, sayinr, which he knocked him under the table. Plaintiff weneout, and Bush assaulted him again outside, knocking him about with great violence.—By Mr. Knowl64 who defended : Bush lodges with my sister, Mrs.Broughton—more shame for her !—Why? To come up against me.—Elave you not scandalised her about Earlestown ?—No, she's scandalized herself. I'll admit telling my brother-in-law he had better go home and look after his wife. —Mr. Knowles said a man who brought a charge of assault ought to have clean hands. A working man had no remedy for slander except by clouting the slanderer's head, and that was what Bush did.—Thomas Broughton , deposed that Bush lodged at his house. Jenkinson had frequently made charges to witness against his wife. Water Polo. Ile had made, the charges loudly in public-houses. Warrington played their opening match at the Baths Witness had never had any complaint to make against on Thursday with Everton, and suppressed defeat by Bush.—Defendant was sworn, and said he went to five goals to nil. A large audience assembled to witness Jenkinson at Broughton's request to ask him for an explanation. As he would give none, be struck. Ile the encounter, nd the referee did not seem to find a favour with them owing to his frequent interruptions. (Bush) had never been before the Bench for any offence. In the first half Warringtorrhad the advantage of the His wife got a separation order against him, and he had shallow end, but the visitors were first in possession, to go to lodgings, but there was no truth in the charges e W. Ashton played well for the home team, and repeatedly made by Jenkinson.—Th Bench thought the assault , experienced hard lines of scoring. C. Roberts, the local was coumitted nudes great provocation, and they dis- goal keeper, defended his end very well, saving some missed the case with a warning to the defendant to stinging shots. From a foul IL Booth scored the first control himself. goal for Everton, thus bringing half time with the MR. AND MRS. E. LYNTON VICARS. score : Everton one goal ; NVarrington nil. On resum- ing Everton had all their own way with the ball, and , Mr. and Mrs. E. Lynton Vicars had a very cordial before many minutes had elapsed from the interval E. welcome home to EarlestoNvn on Thursday evening of Jones scored for the visitors, and was immediately last week, when they returned from their honeymoon followed by Skinner scoring from short range. War- tour. The Town Hall had been beautifully decorated rington had several attempts at goal, but did not for the occasion and it was to a very gay scene that succeed. Jones and Winn added to the score, Everton Mr. and Mrs, T. Vicars, of " The Knoll," Newton, thus winning by five goals to nil, invited their friends, numerous staff, and work people to dine and welcome home the bride and bridegroom Preceding the above match Warrington 2nd played The word " welcome" in bold relief,greeted the visitors Everton 2nd in a friendly encounter, and won by three on ascending the staircase, and along the balcony was goals to nil, the scorers being Sisson and Turner (two). ' expressed the sentiments of all. "-Success to the - bride and bridegroom." During the arrival of the guests, upwards of 330, Earlestown Orchestral Society discoursed pleasant airs from the platform. The catering for so large a party was placed in the hands of the local Co-operative Society, and they surpassed their already established reputation in doing the thing well. After tea Mr. T. Vicars rose amid great cheering and asked Mrs. Vicars to present a number of certificates " The Power of the Cross." and medallions which had been awarded to their employees in connection with the works' branch of the This drama by George A. De Gray is conceived on St. John's Ambulance Association, Dr. Valentine being original lines, the dominating character being Baron the instructor and Dr. F. J. Knowles the examiner. Cm zol a, a human vampire, whose fiendish work can This Mrs. Vicars did in a very gracious manner, the only be withstood by the power of the Cross, before winners beginning Messrs. J. Sankey, C. Appleton A. which he cowers helpless. With such a theme, the Bennett, F. Carney, W. Dawson T: Davies, It. and W, play is highly sensational and is followed with tense Littler. Owens, Li. and W. Valentine, and T. Vicars. jun. All the recipients were lustily cheered.—Mr. interest. The author gives a weird and awesome Vicars then asked Mrs. B.L. Vicars to make a presenta, portrayal of Baron Curzola. The company is a tion to the Surgeon-Instructor (Dr. Valentine.- capable one all round, one of the most striking which duty she performed very agreeably) features being the realistic acting of Mr. Robert Hall and Dr. Valentine briefly expressed his thanks as the dissipated Gerald Fairfax. Mr. Alexander J. The gift wt.s a very handsome volt meter —After tea the company filed out and the room was Harsland does well as Dr. Leslie Fairfax, the young doctor whose prospects are for the time being blighted speedily cleared for a high-class concert. The Earles- ; town Orchestral Society played the " Poet and by the vampire's work. Miss Lily Rosella as Rita Peasant," the Makerfleld Choir sang" Spartan heroes," Lascelles and Mr. A. Hill Menzies as Benjamiia Comrades song of hope," and" Alice where art Hawker, display abilities of a high order in unpopular thou ?" There were a number of songs, in each case parts. Mr. Willie Black as Timothy O'Dowd and I well sung and heartily applauded, the vocalists being Miss Etta Turner as Lolly Gray, are responsible for ' the Misses Eetley, hiss Winnie Williams, Miss Louie the comedy which relieves the story. Miss Marie' Jones, Messrs. R. Littler, T. Vicars, jun., J. Feather- Menzies is winsome and dignified as Margaret Seaton. stone, C. Appleton, and J. Swift (clog dancer). The accompaniments were skilfully played by Mrs. W'. R. The stage-management is excellent. liaison. Mr. W. D. Mead gave a very fine lantern dis- NEXT WEEK'S PROGRAMME. play.—The party closed with dancing, which was kept As a Whitsuntide up till the small hours. attraction the management I announce G. Carlton Wallace's magnificent production " The Lancashire Lad " a human story of Lancashire life. A capital company of twenty artistes will appear and there will be beautiful and elaborate scenery, with special and unique illuminations and lighting effects. Special features are a representation of Lancashire at play on the pier at Blackpool and a</p>
        <p>Tuesday evening found but a bare handful carrying Out the mid-week fixture to the pretty little village of Budworth, the uncertain-looking weather being mainly responsible for th 3 poor turaout—the smallest of the season.</p>
        <p>On Wednesday, despite the heavy showers during the early part of the evening, thirty members eventually put in an appearance at Sankey and another stage of tho bowling lmudicap was reached,</p>
        <p>The Club tours are undoubtedly becoming more popular each year. The usual Whitsuntide tour com- mences to-day and upwards of a dozen members are hoping to renew acquaintance with the beauties of Ellesmere and surrounding country, the majority of whom will be return'ag on Alonday evening.</p>
        <p>,000 THREEPENNY PIECES.</p>
        <p>At Blackburn a house-to-house collection on behalf of the extensions at the local Ragged School was made, and at the close it was found that out of a total of £2lO there were no fewer than 40,000 coppers and 3,000 threepenny pieces.</p>
        <p>great storm scene.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0107" n="art0107">
        <head>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</head>
        <p>ROYAL COURT THEATRE.</p>
        <p>ST. HELENS.</p>
        <p>ELECTRICAL EXTENSIONS.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens Electricity Committee have decided to apply for sanction to borrow £10,426 for extensions of the electricity undertaking. The balance sheet sub- mitted to the committee shows that during last year the income was £19,476 125., and the expenditure £19,209 125., leaving a balance of £267, which has been placed to the reserve account. ST. HELENS CONSTABLES' PAY.</p>
        <p>The St. Helens Watch Committee have decided, on the recommendation of the Chief Constable, to grant an extra allowance of 2s.Gd per week to married constables OD duty at out-stations, and Is. Gd. per week to single constables. The oommittee have also granted a reward of £1 to Police-constable ACK Moon in respect of the capture of two burglars at Parr. FIRE IN A MOTOR GARAGE. VRis A fire, which practically destroyed a large car,occurred 'on Sunday morning in the motor garage belonging to Mr. C. ArLean, Duke-street, St. Helens. Mr. M`Lean had arranged to take a party to Blackpool, and the car had been prepared. The engine was started before the car was taken out, but a quantity of petrol had leaked, and this shddenly took fire. Efforts were made to over- come the flames, and the Fire Brigade was summoned. By this time all the petrol in the tank had been con- sumed, and the fire was extinguished. A considerable quantity of petrol which was in a store close by was effectively guarded, and the fire confined to the car. ST. HELENS HOSPITAL'S EXTENSION.</p>
        <p>The annual report of the St. Helens Providence Free Hospital draws special attention to the efforts that are to be made to celebrate the completion of twenty-five years' service to the town. The hospital was started over twenty-five years ago in George-street by the late Miss F. M. Taylor, and since that time 10,05t3 patients have been treated. The institution has for many years been located in the building known as Hardshaw Hall, and it is now desired to mark the silver jubilee of the hospital by erecting a new operating theatre and wards for men and women. The cost of the entire erection and furnishing will amount to £6,000. A portion of the money has already been subscribed, but the bulk has yet to be provided. NEGLIGENT ST. HELENS PARENTS.</p>
        <p>Margaret Stevens, of Short-street, whose husband was on Friday last sent to gaol \for three months for cruelty to children and stealing from their furnished apartment, appeared before the magistrates on Monday.. —Mr. E. W. Swift prosecuted, and suggested that the bench ought to inflict the maximum penalty. That punishment would be none too mud], because the trouble was all due to the repeated drinking bouts which the woman wilfully indulged in, against all warnings. She had been sent to prison, but began drinking heavily as soon as she came out.— John Dolan spoke to a large number of articles being stolen from the furnished apartments that were let to the defendant.—Mrs. Stevens alleged that Mrs. Dolan had some of her money in hand, and said, "Talk about moneylenders! You and your mother are the biggest vampires in England."—Mrs. Stevens had been con- victed eleven times, and the chairman (Sir Christopher Gamble) said the charge of theft would not be dealt with, but for the neglect she would have to go to gaol for four months.</p>
        <p>COLLIERY ACCIDENT NEAR ST. HELENS. SIXTEEN MEN AND BOYS HURT.</p>
        <p>Shortly after half-past five on Monday morning sixteen men and boys, who were going to work at the Lea Green Colliery, St. Helens, had a terrifying experi- ence, and all were more or less injured.—The men were mustered at the pit head in the usual way and were sent down the shaft sixteen at a time, when the cage descended too fast in thisinstance and violently bumped on to the landing place about 450 yards below the service.—The men in the cage were thrown about in all directions, and some narrowly escaped falling down what is known as the dib-hole, where the water collects to a considerable depth it the bottom of the shaft Assistance was at once rendered, and when the men were got out of the cage it was found that all, with the exception of a youth named Hugh Thomas, aged sixteen, had escaped without broken limbs.—Thomas, who resides at 152, Elephant-lane, That° Heath, was con- veyed to the St. Helens Hospital. Dr. Jacksonsand Dr.l Bailey attended to the others, who were cut about the head and face, and also injured about the body. Among those more seriously injured were Levi Dickson, Edward Peel, and other men named Forbes, Sheriff, Roberts, 'Thomas Pecketh, and James Pearce, who all reside in the neighbourhood of Elephant-lane and Nutgrove.—lt is stated that everything at the pit-head was in perfect order, but the cage was allowed to pass the stopping- place by about five feet. This was sufficient to give the men a nasty shock, but beyond the boy with the broken leg the injuries are not very serious.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0108" n="art0108">
        <head>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</head>
        <p>PALACE AND HIPPODROME.</p>
        <p>Patrons of this place of amusement are again treated to a highly interesting and original programme this week. First on the " bill " comes Little Carl, an eccentric comedian of the first water. His make-up is grotesque and his fun irresistible, and there is laughter loud and long at his curious antics. The climax is reached when he is seized by a huge bull-dog and makes a novel exit by way of tlio flies, the dog never relaxing its grip. A wonderful turn is given by the Ten Ji Troupe of Japanese mahicians who quite mystify the audiencs. One feat in particular is perhaps unique. A girl is placed in a light box and the leader of the troupe at once begins to thrust swords through it in all directions, ending by forcing a spear down the centre. The weapons are then extracted and to the amazement of the audience the girl steps out unscathed. The Lannons, vocal duettists and champion double high pedestal dancers of the world, are associated in an entertainment which is at once clever and pleasing. Their pedestal dancing is remarkable Will Lannon, who is the holder of the champion belt, excelling in this respect and calling forth rounds of applause. Lieutenant Travis is one of the smartest ventriloquists who has appeared in Warrington, his impersonations being greatly varied and true to life. Another attractive hem is Mdlle. Haydee's miniature vaudeville theatre, a capital pro- r a mune being gone through on a small but elaborately rabellishe4 stage by marionettes whose movements appear quite natural. Ridiculous Recco amuses the audience with his burlesque acrobatic performance in which he displays originality and skill. Some popular songs are contributed by Marion Scott, a pleasing comedienne. Raymond's Bio-Tableaux as usual show some of the latest things in animated photography, chief among them being pictures of the disaster to H.M.S. Gladiator.</p>
        <p>NEXT WEEK'S PllOOll, AMIE. Another excellent company will appear next week. It is headed by Bella and Bijou, the renowned society entertainers, who will submit their latest successes, including "The Spanish Dancer " and " The Suffragette." A splendid turn rill be provided by the Scliavonis Troupe of comedy acrobats which includes Miss Ella, the only lady catching one gentleman on each hand whilst they are throwing somersaults from the ground. Phern and Phern musical speciality artistes' will appear in thei ' " phone act," introducing the " marimbaphone," thr, " pernaphone " the "edeophone"and the "pealaphone: The other artistes will be :—Billie Burford, chi comedienne ; Stewart and Evans, comedians and dancers ; Takio, the only Japanese mimic of sounds ; and Flo Burnett, vocalist extraordinary.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0109" n="art0109">
        <head>Alleged Theft of Postal Order.</head>
        <p>Alleged Theft of Postal Order.</p>
        <p>Robert James Lytlagoe, of Jolley's Yard, pleaded guilty at the Warrington Police Court, on Thursday, to stealing a postal order, value :0 155., the property of Samuel Woods, a parcel clerk at the Cheshire Lines Station. On Wednesday he was sent to cash the order, and instead of returning with the money he kept it and got drunk. A portion of the money was found concealed under a mattress at his house. He was remanded Until to-day.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0110" n="art0110">
        <head>CYCLIST'S MARVELLOUS ESCAPE</head>
        <p>CYCLIST'S MARVELLOUS ESCAPE</p>
        <p>FROM DEATH.</p>
        <p>During his holidays Mr. C. E. Curtis, of 648, Chats- worth-road, Chesterfield, went with the members of the Brampton Life-boat C.C.,for a cycle run to Buxton. Nearing the town they were descending a steep hill at a furious space when at a sharp turn Mr. Curtis was dashed into a wall while his machine rebounded across the road and was smashed up. - Mr. Curtis's friends, fortunately, had with them some Zam•buk which they smeared over his many cuts, bruises, and bleeding wounds on the head shoulders, and face. Thus the pain was eased and the wounds kept clean. "Next day, writes Mr. Curtis, " I saw that all danger of festering and blood-poison- ing had been averted, and the wounds were beginning to heal. The stiffness and soreness went away, and a fortnight after the accident Zarn•huk had mended me up so wonderfully that to the astonishment of my friends I was able to return to work."</p>
        <p>The lesson of Mr. Curtis's experience is that no cyclist should start out without a box of Zatn-buk in his possession. It is the ideal first-aid remedy.</p>
      </div>
