untel bowl Miss Ivy Allan, Worcester cream jug Mr. and Mrs. H. Woods, silver napkin rings Miss Sloane (Chester), silver-mounted inkstand Mrslames Wyatt. silver fruit knives Mr. W. Milner, Sevras vases Mr. and Mrs. F. Monks, rose bowl Miss Bennett, coffee cups Mrs. Davison, fruit spoons Mrs. Pickrnere, silver vase Mrs. Glover, china Mr. and Mrs. Stokes, picture Mr. and Mrs. Mills, silver mustard and pepper pots The Rev. and Mrs. H. It. Browne, cheque The Rev. and Mrs. J. McGranahan, cheque Mr. W. Clover, cheque Mrs. Middlebrook, cheque Miss Sloane, tea cloths Messrs. Leggett, spirit dscanter Mr. and Mrs. A. W. Glover, standard lamp - Mrs. and Miss Ridgway, silver cream jug Mr. and Airs. R, Ridgway, silver sugar sifter Bev. J. A. and Airs. Forest, silver sugar sifter Mr. and Mrs. L. Franceys, picture Mrs. Fletcher, silver sauce boat Dr. and Mrs. Bower, silver salt callers Mr. and Mrs. Morris, silver candlesticks Captain and Mrs. Wyatt, and The Rev. E. B. and Mrs. May, silver salver Mr. and Mrs. Judd, silver ink stand Miss D. Catterata, cigarette lighter Mr. and Mrs. D. Fel rclough, scarf Mother of bride, house linen Father of bride, cheque Mr. and Mrs. C. Mo Caw, sauce boat Captain J. G. W. Casson, silver dish and knife Mr. I'. Daley, silver napkin rings Mr. J. C. Law, silver cigar box Rev. A. E. Hughes, silver cigarette box Major Whewell, silver cigarette case Mr. and Mrs. A. S. Craig, silver gong Mr. T. E. O'Connor, barometer Miss Glover, coal box Mr. tied Mrs. J. Fleming, brass kettle, spirit lamp and tray Miss Annie Glover, tea cloth Mr. J. S. Glover, canteen and piano Dr. J. IL Glover, dining room chairs Mr. and Mrs. 1:1.R. Whelans, tantalus Mr. R. Dymond and W. Addison, silver spoons and tongs Mr. A. A. Carroll, silver-mounted carvers Mr. Harris (London), crocodile silver-mounted dressing case Mr. and Mrs. J. L. Jones, silver muffin dish Mrs. Wynne-Jones, mats Mr. and Mrs. Charles Walker, one dozen cut glass finger bads and crumb scoop</p>
        <p>-0 -</p>
        <p>The question of the cost of Education is still a sore point with many local authotities. The local rate has grown with alarming rapidity while the contribution from the *National Exchequer has been stationary. The local cast of Education is becoming an oppressive burden and many authorities are asking for a larger contribution from the State. Mr. McKenna's Bill proposes to give an additional .£1,400,003. Warring- ton's share would probably reduce our education rate by fourpence or fivepence ia the pound. But then the grant is accompanied by conditions—and conditions that some are not inclined to accept. We are somewhat surprised .that our local economists have not given this bill a heartier welcome. It seems a big price to pay for that nebulous thing the denominational atmosphere, in some of our schools, that our tradesmen and manu- facturers cannot secure this welcome relief in the local burden.</p>
        <p>-0-</p>
        <p>Taking the country through in 1902, sixty-eight per cent of the cost of education came from the National Exchequer and thirty-two per cent from the rates, while in 1907 only fifty-ono per cent came from taxes and forty-nine per cent from the rates. Education is national c mcern, yet the nation as such only pays half its cast. The time has come when local authori- ties should unite in asking for larger grants from the National Exchequer. More stringent regulations as to staffing, school buildings, apparatus are frequently being issued. New duties which entail great expendi- ture are laid upon the Education Committee. No new grants are given to meet the new expenditure. No one yet knows to what extent we shall be compelled to go in expenditure for medical examina- tion. If the examination is to be followed by treat- nient,the provision of spectacles,,or surgical appliances, then the expenditure will not be nearly met by any provision so far made in the estimates of the health and Education Committees. And that these things will follow is inevitable. To appoint new officials merely to make records and to collect statistics would los exasperatingly ludicrous. The expenditure is not therefore at an end when provision is made for the salaries of nurse and doctor. The NVarrington Committee would be well advised in losing no time in joining with other authorities in urging upon the Government the need for increased grants if the beneficent intentions of the memorandum on the medical inspection of school children are to be realised.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0112" n="art0112">
        <head>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL LEAGUE. M. A. 1-1. Crosfield, M.P. Presents the Dodds Challenge Shield.</head>
        <p>ASSOCIATION FOOTBALL LEAGUE. M. A. 1-1. Crosfield, M.P. Presents the Dodds Challenge Shield.</p>
        <p>The annual meeting of the Warrington and District Association Football League was held last evening at the Lion Hotel, the most interesting feature of the proceedings -being the presentation of the handsome challenge shield given by the President (Mr. James Dodds) to the successful teatn—Burtonwood. Mr. Dodds presided and Mr. A. 11. Crosaeld,ll.P. atteued to present the shield. The PRESIDENT commented upon the value of foot- ball in developing, in conjunction with education and other moral and mental influences, all that was best in the human frame and mind. He expressed special , satisfaction that in that League the talent was local and they did not import " foreigners." (Applause.) Mr. Cllosrmr.n in presenting the shield to the President of the Burtonwood Club (Mr. Miers) referred to the important part which athletics had played in the formation of national character and said that in these days when something like 75 per cent of the people lived in towns it was especially necessary that all forms of outdoor recreation should be encouraged within reason, for the sake of the manhood and physique of our race. They were all glad to know that in old England games were still played very largely for their own sake, and that we had not too much playing by proxy. He had been engaged in business for many years, although latterly he had got mixed up with another occupation —(laughter)—but he did not wish to see too much of the business element introduced into our ' sports. (Applause.) What one particularly valued in our English sport was the fact that with all the keen rivalry engendered CVQ still had the spirit of fair play to use a good old phrase we could "play the game." (Applause.)</p>
        <p>On the motion of Mr. Dodds seconded by Mr. Mien a hearty vote of thanks was accotded to Mr. CrosEeld, the company singing " For he's a jolly good fellow," and giving hearty cheers.</p>
        <p>Late in the evening Mr. W. H. Smalley (chairman of the League) presented gold medals to the members of the winning team and a silver cup (presented by Mr. Sugg, of Liverpool) and silver medals to the champions of the second division—Orford. An enjoyable musical programme was gone through.</p>
      </div>
