      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0118" n="art0118">
        <head>Licensing Bills</head>
        <p>The Licensing Bills</p>
        <p>forbade absolutely the appearance of the young under a certain age in a public-house. It was certainly a movel in the right direction. So also with the publican himself. The Act of 1904 didn't touch him. Lie was left com- pletely to the tender mercies of the brewer, and their mercies were proverbially cruel. Now, however, the publicans' interests were detached, and made distinct from those of their unscrupulous employers. In any question of compensation which might arise he would at least have an amount assigned him equivalent to a couple or three years' profits. The Bill before Parlia- ment assumed a question to which it was an answer. Who was to be the ruler of the kingdom—the people or the brewers ? Neither the nation nor the Commons had the slightest intention of delivering up its domestic rule into the hands of this particular class. They had In connection with the quarterly meeting of the exploited the people long enough. Get that Bill through Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union, two and their power, their day as a reline class, waso gon e. open-air meetings in support of the Government Mr. R. A. POTT, Manchester. referred to the influence cee Licensing Bill were held at Warrington on Saturday of temperance upon the life of the people generally. evening. Mr. E. J. Guest, who presided at a well- -Mr. T. H. GIRSIDE, Salford, also spoke, da eso u- attended meeting at Bridge Foot, said the man who tion was unanimously passed in favourof fi rinhe Burl gave the Band of Hope Union its title must have been ! under inspiration, for every word of it was full of meaning. They were united in various pirts of the Band of Hope Union's Support. country in a great and glorious work. Men and women At the quarterly meeting of the General Committee of various views and feelings were engaged in that of the Lancashire and Cheshire Band of Hope Union blessed work. Their creed was one. They believed held at Warrington on Saturday. a resolution was passed very devoutly that alcohol was an evil, and was doing appealing to the Government to afford the earliest a great mischief in the country in many ways, that the possible facilities for the due and adequate considera- excessive use of liquor was degrading and impoverish- tion of the Licensing Bill, coupled with the hope that ing and making miserable multitudes of people. They Parliament would expedite its law. passages into were united in their love for the children, and they had an earnest desire that the little ones should grow up SIR T. P. WHITTAKER'S HANDBOOK. pure, holy and righteous. There was a great tendency in these times to trust in the material rather than in the Licensing Bill : Some Facts sod Arguments in S moral and spiritual. Men were putting their hope in armies ' port " has attained a very wide circulation. A third and navies." We believe " said Mr. Guest " in the army edition making the 110th thousand has just been issued and navy and in making them efficient. There is a. by the Temperance Legislation League, Parliament great hope in the wealth of the country. Men and women are grasping for money as they perhaps never alansions, Victoria-street, S.W. Among other subjects just treated off are :—"The Present Position and How grasped after it before. We believe in wealth and we it Arose,"" The Trade and the Public," "The Financial pray God to prosper the trade and commerce of this Phase," "Why Brewery Shares have Fallen," " Death country, but we hope the time is coming when there Duties," &amp;c. The book is published at sixpence but shall be a large distribution of the wealth of the country and less poverty and misery. We believe also will be supplied in quantities for ,gratuitiouscirculation in good laws but the laws of the country will only be • improved as, at greatly reduced rates. the morals of the people are improved. We may have perfect laws and yet the people may be law- breaker:. Our hope is in the children of this country. We believe that if they grow up to be physically strong, mentally vigorous and morally pure, the future of this country depends entirely upon them. The great work of Ulla Union is to teach the children that the use of alcohol will do them physical injury, will blight their intellects and tend to morally unfit them brute." (Applause.) Mr. W. J. LEWIS (Weaste) moved : " That this public out-door meeting assembled at Bridge Foot heartily approves the Licensing Bill and urges the Government to facilitate its speedy passage into law ; and that the Chairman sign the resolution and forward , it to the Prime Minister and the local Member of I Parliament." He quoted the opinion of Mr. Balfour that " intemperance at this moment is one of the ! ereatest social scourges we have to undergo, for it is the parent and producer of countless evils, and if we could put an end to it by any means we should deserve , the gratitude of posterity." Lord Curzon had also said ' in opening a wnrkingn3en's club in Derbyshire that he was glad drinking was prohibited there. It was to be regretted, he added, that the evil was increasing and eating its way like is cancer into the hearts of the working-classes. In face of such statements there was ample reason why his Majesty's Government should bring in a Pill in order to reduce the excessive drink- ing. In contradiction of the assertion that the Bill would not promote temperance, Mr. Lewis quoted the opinion of a " trade" conferenee held in Birmingham that it was clearly shown by Parliamentary returns that the vice of drunkenness was in proportion to the number of public-houses and the facilities for obtaining drink. The Government Bill, because it Provided for a gradual diminution of public- bruises would. continued Mr. Lewis, bring about a diminution Of thinking and drunkenness. It would undoubtedly mean temperance in that it would do away with a great amount or extraordinary drinking. At the end of the fourteen years time limit the people would recover the rights they inherited as free born English- men—the rights to protect their children and their homes—and they would have the power of saying whether they intended to he cursed by having pablic- houses in their midst. Tt was quite wrong to say that at the expiration of the time limit the State was going to take over the traffic and carry it on at its own profit. Tf the drink traffic was destroyed at the end of four- teen years it would be because the rising generation by their better training had turned out to be nobler citizens and had realized that the trade was blocking the way of progress end were determined to do all they could to destroy it, (Applause.) The Rev. T. ff. liner (Bolton), seconded and said they hoped that in a few weeks the Bill would pass even the House of Lords. They were not sure of that, but if it did not, it would be so much the worse for the Lords. (Lauehtere They believed the Bill to be reasonable and righteous and the Government had intro- dnced it in all faithfulness. The Government had a merelete for the introduction of such a measure for if the last general election meant anything. it meant that when the Government Came into power they should deal with the liquor question. The more they rend the menenre the more they must be convinced that it was statesmanlike and helpful to the community and it was only giving hack to the nation that which should never have been taken away from it. They supported the Bill intelligently, reesonahly and righteously, with the full assurance that it would pass into law. (Applause). Mr. ALEX THOMSON (U.K.A.), in supporting said one vital and democratic principle in the Bill was the principle of local option whereby the power which had been exercised in the past by magistrates and landowners would in future be exercised by the poorest people in the country. Licences to sell drink were not granted in order that people might make money, but to satisfy the supposed requirements of a neiehbonrhood. De saw posted on the wall of the public-house opposite a hill headed " Whose turn next ?." That reminded him that one of the things the liquor people were trying to do now in order to defeat the Bill was to bring to their aid the wealthy and aristocratic classes, the owners of property. They had tried to prejudice the working men against it but they hid fsiled—at any rate they had failed to prejudice organized labour. Now they were turning their atten- tion to the wealthy people, and were saying that the Bill was a great scheme of Socialism, that it was the beginning of a time when all property should he swept away from private hands. When they said that they were doing a great disservice to the cause of property. because if those who owned land and property owned them on the same ground that people held licences it wonld he a very easy thing for the Socialists to get hold of them, because a licence was not property, hut merely the privilege to sell drink for one year. 'The Bill aimed at accomplishine two things—reducing the excessive number of public-houses and giving tne people full control over the liquor traffic. Mr. ' Balfour by introducing the 1904 Act admitted that there were too many houses. The Government Bill would reduce them in a scientific and businesslike wnv. instead of in a haphazard manner. At present the uhlic houses were most numerous in the poor districts n here the people were herded together in houses which were unfit to live in. I\l r. Asquith's Bill proposed- that at the end of 11 years there should be only one public-house to every I,oeo inhabitants in those areas. The Bill would bring hope and light into the lives of men and women and little children in those districts which were a disgrace to our civilization and to our Christianity. The measure would not only promote sobriety but would tend to the improvement of neighbourhoods which had been hindered by the excessive number of public houses. Mr. Thomson refuted the nrgnment advanced by Mr. Lyttelton that for every public-home extinguished a new club would spring up, pointing out that under Mr. Tlalfonr's Act 2778 1 cences were extinguished whereas the number of new clubs was not more that 120 per annum —a total of under f,OO. The displacement of labour under the new Bill would be so gradual as not to he felt. The brewers' argu- ments its to unemployment were hollow and insincere as well as exaererated. Mr. Balfour'e Bill was brought in at the bidding of the brewers who at that time were well represented in Parliament. Mr. Balfour said that when the measure became law it would reduce licences at, a much greater rate than hitherto. Why did not the brewers protest against a Bill which would throw men out of employment? They did not care one jot about that. They were only concerned with their own interests and the •1904 Bill was brought in to block temperance reform and to give them security of tenure. If money which was now spent on drink was diverted into other channels it would benefit the individual and the prosperity of tbe country generally. (A man in the crowd exclaimed : " I have had some to-day brit I wish public-houses were all closed.") The Bill was a wise and courageous attempt to deal with a great national evil. The great battle was as to who should be the master, the people or the brewer ? We stand on the side of the people, continued the speaker, and we believe the people will win. On the other side there , is great wealth, great social and political influence, but on our side there is a power which is even greater— the power of the people, and once the people of the country are roused—and I never knew them to be roused as they are to-day in favour of this Bill—it is not the brewers, or the House of Lords, or any other great. power that will withstand their will. This Bill puts into our hands the instrument by which we may emancipate ourselves from the tyranny of the liquor traffic and win our way to higher and better levels of social progress and of betterment for those who need our help. (Applause.) The resolution was then put to the meeting and carried with about six dissentients. IN BEWSEY-STREET. Another meeting was held near the Farmers' Stores, Bowsev-street, and there was an attendance of about rm. The Rev. J. F. Matthews presided. He said he had always believed in open air meeting, and no finer opportunity occurred of expounding the Licensing Bill than such an occasion as that. As a Band of Hope Union they were naturally devoted to the interests of little children. The Llcensing Bill was one of four or five forms at least in which the present Government were taking to heart the needs of child-life. It., aimed at protecting the young by preventive agencies. It</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0119" n="art0119">
        <head>OPEN AIR MEETINGS.</head>
        <p>OPEN AIR MEETINGS.</p>
        <p>The Insincerity of the Brewers'</p>
        <p>Arguments.</p>
        <p>TRAINING COLLEGE ONCERT.</p>
        <p>Dr. Somervel's Criticism of Modern</p>
        <p>Music.</p>
        <p>Dr. Arthur Somervel conducted the annual exernina- tion in music at the Warrington Training College on Monday. The collective test in the evening was, as usual, made the occasion of the College concert, and a largo number of guests assembled at the invitation of the Principal. The works performed were Coleridge Taylor's " Hiawatha's Wedding Feast" and Macfarren's " May Day." The students were essisted by tenor and bass voices drawn from local choirs, and a small orchestra was in atteadance. Notwithstanding the oppressive atmosphere, most pleasing interpretations of the music were given, and reflected credit on all con- cerned. The chorus responded intelligently to the hatea of Mr. W. S. Woods who, as usual, ably conducted. The soloists were Miss Ethel Jackson, a second year student, and Mr. Harry Evans, of Liverpool Cathedral. Miss Jackson is a vocalist of considerable promise, and sang with taste and expression the May Queen's song in Macfarren's " May Day," thoroughly meriting the cordial applause which rewarded her efforts. Mr. Harry Evans possesses a pure-toned tenor voice, and he did justice to that delightful solo " Onsway ! Awake, beloved!" which is one of the most ecceptable features of " Hiawatha's ! Wedding Feast." He infused the requisite feeling into his rendering and it afforded much pleasure to the assembly. The concert throughout weli in keeping with , the high traditions of the College—a fact upon which ,1 all who participated in it are to ho congratulated. The orchestra was composed as follows :-Ist violin, Mr. R. H. Pearson ; 2nd violin, Mr. A. Knowles; viola, Mr. G. Johnson ; violincello, Mr. H. Bond ; double batra, Mr. J. Sudworth ; pianoforte, Miss Hackett ; harmonium, Mr. IV. H. Payton, Mug. Bac. At the conclusion of the concert Dr. SOMERVZI. delivered an address. He said the intonation of the chorus brad been remarkably good, considering the atmospheric conditions. He supposed every examiner attached the greatest importance to his owl' particular subject and he considered music quite the most important thing the students would have to teach in the schools. Wo were very careful in our education to see .that children were taught to speak and to express themselves rightly. In their moments of relaxation we tried to give them good books to read because we knew that when a person expressed himself hastily, ill-ternpereclly and badly it was generally a sign that there was something wrong inside. (Laughter). The person who read trashy literature generally did so because he had a trashy mind. ( Laughter). It never seemed to strike people that it was almost more important what children should sing than what they should say, because words were a very poor way of expressing. oneself. Music was one of the easiest and earliest ways in which we could express ourselves thoroughly. These things acted and re-acted on one another and if we sang nice things they acted on UR inside and, he hoped, made us nicer. (Laughter). Dr. Sornervel referred to the great importance which was attached to music in China in the old days,pointing out that the class of music sung was taken as a criterion of the good or bad government of a province. if the Commission which was sent out to inquire as to this, found that bad music was being sung, the governor was beheaded. (Laughter). What would happen if a Commission were sent over England to see what kind of songs the people wore singing now ? He thought we should get a most awful pile of trash. (Laughter). We should get very bad songs, a certain number of which he would not put into any category. We should , get comic songe which were not all particularly bad hut extremely chill. (Laughter.) Wci should get vulgar music hall songs and a larger stock still of songs which ha thought were more dangerous musically than the comic song or the vulgar music hall song—he meant the sentimental drawing-room ballad, because that song pretended to be good. The • vulgar song did not pretend to be anything bat vulgar and the comic song did not pretend to be anything but comic, but the sentimental drawing room ballad pretended to be a good song and it took hundreds and thousands of people in. They sang it, thinking they were singing good music and they were not. The kind of song he had in his mind was a song about match girls who died in the snow and went to heaven—to the accompaniment of pianoforte triplets,of course.(Langhter.)Having referred to the "waltz refrain" introduced in songs about " con- sumptive choristers' "Dr.Somervel passed some criticisms on " The Lost Chord." He said the person mentioned in the song was weary and ill at ease. He asked anyone who had any experience of the organ if on going to solace himself on the organ when he was weary and ill at ease be pulled out the noisy stops, because that was what the person in the song did. (Laughter.) It said "My fingers wandered idly over the noisy keys." Dr. Sornervel also remarked on the impossibility of striking "one chord " which would sound like" Amen " and continuing said he could not conceive of a person who was musical enough to play a beautiful chord on the organ not being able to find it again, nor could he conceive of anyone who was unmusical being able to strike any chords worth listening to. When they analysed the song it was rubbish, and all songs oL that kind were rubbish too. Three hundred years ago the British Isles were the most musical place in the world. He did not want to undervalue what the Puritan did for England—they did a great deal to make our char- acter what it was—but there was no reform that was ever carried out which had not at the same time done some berm and he thought the harm the Puritan move- ment did was that it stopped the people from expressing themselves in a beautiful way in music whenever they were happy or wanted to enjoy themselves. ThrO result was that the country became extraordinarily dull. England became a commucial nation, and people flocked into the towns and had nothing around them but sheer blank ugliness and the struggle for life. The result of that part of the Puritan movement was that English people took to public-houses and also got the music halls to start. The music halls and public houses found congenial soil in that dull and stupid people manufactured by the putting down of music. Now we could teach the children to sing and dance and the more we did so the more we should make them happy and, quite beyond mere happiness, we should really aid their characters. Dr. Somervel strongly advocated the teaching of national songs. One genera- Urn of children brought up on decent music would make the present style of music ball impossible, because he did not think they would listen to the trash which was at present provided for them. He hoped the students would carry away the great I, traditions of music which they learnt in the Warring- 1 ton Training College and teach the children nothing but the very best of music. (Applause.)</p>
        <p>WRECKED NERVES. • Weak, thin, and unable to waik. -- A Missionary's Great Cure by DR. CASSELL'S TABLETS. Mr.O. Abbott, a well-known lecturer and police court missionary, of 35, Gipsy-Road, Leicester. writes : About four years ago 1 suffered dreadfully from paralysis of the nerves and chronic dyspepsia, f was very weak and fleshless, and quite unable to walk. I was confined to my bed for over six months, and my doctor told me I should never get up again. I tried many different medicines but all to no use.</p>
        <p>"At last I tried Dr. Cassell's-T:biets and after ten days' treat.nent I was able to walk across the bedroom with the aid of a stick, and gradually improved until now lam strong and in the best of health, and new flesh has been built up. and I am back again to my old weight, viz., 13 stone." Dr. Cassell's Tablets are a safe and certain remedy for nerve troubles of all kinds, loss of flesh, awemia. stomach and kidney dislrders. and nerve and physical exhaustion. Price, 10.1,d., Is. lid. and 2s. 9d. (containing nearly four times the lOid. size), of all chemists.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0120" n="art0120">
        <head>WARRINGTON.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON.</p>
        <p>Athletic Success. At the Whitworth C. and F. C. sports on Saturday, G. Watrnough. Warrington A. C., won the mile handicap off the 112yds. mark in 4min. 27 4-ssec. Ship Canal Deepened. Manchester Ship Canal has been deepened. Instead of twenty-six feet, the whole length of the canal from Eastham to Manchester is now twenty-eight feet deep.</p>
        <p>Dishonest Warrington Lodger. Arthur Nicholls, formerly lodging in Fairclough's- avenue, Warrington, pleaded guilty at the Warrington Police Court on Monday to stealing a silver watch and chain, the property of Mrs. Burgess.—lt appeared that the prisoner lodged with the prosecutor up to March 6. On that date he left the house, taking the watch and chain with him. The watch had been pledged at Liverpool.—Prisoner, who was arrested at Derby, was sent to gaol for two months. £l4 14s Good second-hand piano.—WooD,London rd., Stockton Heath. Tunings and repairs.—Advt. Drowning Mystery Solved. Mr. S. Brighouse, coroner, held an inquest at the Warrington Police Station, on Monday, on the body of Thomas Jones Moss, a flatman (22), Terrace-street, Widnes, whose body was recovered from the Mersey at Warrington on Friday last. It appeared from the evidence that deceased was employed on the flat Mary Ellen, which was being towed down from Liver- pool on March 31. Whilst throwing the anchor over- board he fell into the water, and nothing was seen of him until the body was recovered on Friday.—The Imother-in-law of the deceased stated that she could nit identify the body, as it was so badly decomposed, I but the clothing was that which was worn by the deceased.—The jury returned a verdict of" Acci- dentally drowned." Paraffin Lamp Tragedy. An inquest was held at the Warrington Police Station, on Monday morning by Mr. Brighouse, coroner, into the circumstances of the death of William Daniels (69), of Booth's Hill, Lymm, who died in the Warrington Infirmary on Friday from terrible burns about the head and neck. It was stated by the son of the deceased that on Monday night last week he was aroused out of bed by a noise at the bottom of the stairs. On looking there he saw his father with Lis bead surrounded with flames. He immediately extinguished the flames, and sent for a doctor. On making an examination he found a broken paraffin lamp, and it appeared his father, who was troubled with rheumatism, had slipped while going upstairs, and the lamp had set fire to his clothing. —A verdict of "Accidental death " was returned. PUBLIC BENEFIT—UNDER THB CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LA.NC.A.SIIIRE.—Advt. Level Crossing Accident. . . • - - -</p>
        <p>A singular accident occurred at the London and North-Western Railway crossing in Gateworth-street, Sankey Bridges, at half-past ten on Monday morning. Thomas Barker (40), a labourer in the employ of a cleansing contractor to the Corporation, and living at 5, Lytligoes-lane, was passing over the crossing in charge of a horse and what is known as a " tumbler cat " when the 1057 train from Arpley Station dashed into the cart just as it was about to clear the line and swerved it round. It struck Barker in the side and he was injured internally. He was at once picked up and conveyed to the Infirmary and, although his injuries are of a serious nature, he will, it is expected, recover. The horse was uninjured, but the tumbler was completely wrecked. The man was leading the horse at the time of the accident, and was on his way to Mr. Grimshaw's farm. Reduction in Sugar. ASTlLEY'S,l,Buttermarket st.—Advt Fire at a Doctor's Stables.</p>
        <p>At two o'olock on Saturday morning a fire was dis- covered to have broken out in the stables adjoining the residence of Dr. Sephton (Culcheth), medical officer to the Warrington Rural District Council. The fire, which was first observed by Miss Sephton from her bedroom window, originated in the loft above the stables. She at once aroused the household, and the doctor rescued the valuable horses and removed them to a place of safety. The alarm having spread, help was soon at hand. Mes=rs. W. Peel and Ellis ran for the fire extinguishing apparatus belonging to the Leigh Rural Dista jet Council, and a good supply of water , was soon playing on the flames. A messenger was also sent for the Glazelmuy Mill Fire Brigade, but before their arrival the villagers had obtained mastery of the fire. The fire brigade, however, continued at , work until all danger was past. The origin of the fire is unknown. The damage is roughly estimated at</p>
        <p>Premier in name, Premier in quality. PREMIER Crm,Es from 6 guineas.—J. S. UARIAND, 51 to 53 Bridge-street, Warring ton.—Advt. Praise for the Museum. Writing in the " Agricultural Economist and Horticultural Review " A.C.S., refers to. the Warring- ton Museum as follows : - Warrington which possesses one of the oldest and one of the best arranged museums in the country, has extended the scope of its usefulness by inaugurating and maintaining a wild-flower table. Sa successful has the idea proved that an additional table has had to be provided. Last year more than 2,500 specimens were used, each one being named, its life history, and other instructive and interesting points being noted. The winter exhibition of fruits, sedge's, rushes, and grasses has been particularly instructive. The idea is one well worth following by other similar institutions, as it cannot fail to be of immense advantage in inculcating a love for the larger field of Nature. That this is so is proved by the numerous visits of school children, making notes for lessons, applications for the naming of specimens, and in other ways. The work is under the aegis of an enthusiast in the person of Mr. G: A. Dunlop, the assistant curator, who is ably seconded by a band of voluntary workers. It is interesting to note that the family of our Editor, Mr. Edward Orea Greening,was instrumental in founding this museum.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BEVEFIT.-UNDER THE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCASIHRE.—Advt• Arpley Railway Bridge. . . . .</p>
        <p>Thousands of people assembled in the vicinity. of Arpley on Sunday to witness a unique engineering feat—the removal of one of the old iron girders on Arpley Railway Bridge. The work was undertaken by the L ndon and North-Western Railway Com- pany's own engineers. Preparations began at an early hour of the morning, almost, in fact, as soon as it was daylight. tinder the bridge (which was built in 1834) were placed two heavy barges containing large quantities of timber, and they formed a very strong pontoon, and when the tide was at its height, namely, at about one o'clock, the 190 feet girder on the nridge-street side of the bridge was slowly pulled along by a powerful engine in the station yard. The girder was thus moved a few feet each time, and it was not until nearly ten o'clock at night that it was dragged into the station premises except one end. ! which hung two or three yards over the roadway, Every possible precaution was taken for the safety of the limner, us body of men engaged in the task who, with their engineers, are to be warmly congratulated on such a successful operatien. The old girders will be replaced by much stronger ones, and the new bridge will be able to take all the traffic for many years to come.</p>
        <p>Reduction in Sugar. Asnr.eY's,4,Buttermarket st.—Advt P.S.A. Meetings. The meeting of the Wycliffe P.S.A. on Sunday was well attended. Mr. W. Roberts presided and spoke of the pleasing memories of friends the pulpit reminded him of, of the godly men who had passed away. He referred to the great need of providing for the enjoyment of the young in connection with the churches, even to dancing. He would be in favour of providing this healthy recreation for our young people on the church premises.—Mr. John Harrison, of Liverpool, said he enjoyed all the Chairman had said, but did not agree with all his remarks. What was wanted as more enthusiasm, more Christ-minded- ness. If that church was full of earnest young men they could move Warrington. He contended for decision of character ; he did not like whittlers, but men who could take an intelligent and firm stand. (Applause.) Mr. 11. Blanc and the Secretary also took part in the meeting. Messrs. John Willett and Nathan Cook sang a duet, " Watchman, what of the night ?" Mr. E. Walker also sang a solo with good effect. The total number of members is now 459. The Secretary proposed a vote of sympathy and con- dolence with Mr. Percy Prosser and his mothet, who were bereaved of father and husband, also a vote of sympathy with Mr. T. R. Ashworth, whose wife had passed away.—The weekly meeting of the Warrington Society was held in the Co-operative Hall. Mr. Williams presided, and spoke some very encouraging words to the members. A solo was also ably rendered by Mr. Williams, and an addrs3 Wa3 delive red by Mr. E. England on " Christian politics, or its true place in a Christian Church." The lesson was read' by the President.</p>
        <p>Music in the Park. The League of the Cross hand will occupy the stand on Sunday next, under the conductorship of Mr. T. Hynes, Programme :—Hypm, "0 Salutaris," Arr T. Hynes; march, " Gunner,' J. Robinson ; selection, "La Lonnambula," Bellini ; air varie, " Rogseau's Dream," H. Round ; euphonium solo, "Village Blacksmith," W. H. Weiss ; soloist,Mr. J. W. Brown ; selection, " Recollections of Carl Rosa," H. Round ; " God save the King." 21st Birthday Celebration.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening the 21st birthday of Miss Jessie Forster the eldest daughter of Mr. and Mrs. John Furster of 71, Lira-street, was celebrated in Cairo- street schools, about 70 persons being present. After tea, songs and &amp;c. were given by Mrs. Dutton, Messrs. Hannon,Birch,Hindle, Caldwell, Brookes and Singleton. During a brief interval Mr. Frank Birch in a very able speech proposed a vote of thanks to Mr. and Mrs. Forster for their hospitality, and Mr. A. Dutton seconded. The proceedings terminated at about 12 o'clock, after a very enjoyable evening. Miss Forster received numerous handsome presents.</p>
        <p>PUBLIC BENEFIT.—IINDER TRE CLOCK FOR BEST BOOTS IN LANCISIIIRE.—Advt. Motor Attendant's Compensation.</p>
        <p>At the Warrington County Court on Thursday, before Judge Shiress Wills, K.C., Mr. W. H. S. Oulton (instructed by Mr. T. S. Steel) applied to his Honour to record an agreement under the Workmen's Com- pensation Act, 1906, with reference to Albert Gibson, of Robson-street, Warrington, who was injured in the course of his employment as a motor attendant at Messrs Rylands' works, in that he was entitled during incapacity to a weekly sum of 12s. 63.—Mr. Day, of Liverpool, appeared for the respondent, and after a consultation with Mr. Oulton said they had agreed to file a memorandum into Court stating terms. , A Dangerous Practice, Margaret Campbell, Sh ip-yard, Warrington, pleaded guilty at the Borough Court on Thursday to a charge of stealing 561bs of coal from the permanent way of the L. and N.-W. Railway at Arpley new sidings on Wednesday afternool.— Detective Coward stated that be saw the prisoner get through the railing.; and cross two sets of metals. She then picked up a quantity of coal, which she placed in a bag, and she was going away when he arrested her. Witness had previously cautioned women of the prisoner's class, as they exposed themselves to considerable danger. Prisoner's husband pleaded for leniency, and after being warned she was ordered to be released on the rising of the Court.</p>
        <p>Six GUINEAS will purchase a fully guaranteed PREMIER ...CYCLE.. .Inspection invitel—J. S. GAR- . . . . . .</p>
        <p>AND, 51,53, &amp; 55 Bridgc;--streat, Warriagtoa.—Mvt. Unfit for Work.</p>
        <p>George Peplow, King-street, Warrington, cask and sack dealer, was summoned at the Altrincham Sessions on Monday for working a horse whilst the animal was in an unfit state, at Altrincham.—Defen- dant said the animal was lame but it was not lame when he started out. The horse had kicked itself on the way to Altrincham. —lnspector Brian of the R.S.P.C.A., said the horse vas exceedingly lame. It was sweating freely ; was aged, and was in poor con- dition. It was suffering from fever in the feet. He called attention to the animal, and defendant said, " I have not had it long. It has just come up from grass, I only want to g) to Broadheath with it. I came from Warrington this morning." Witness told him he must have been over dtivinp.; it.—Mr. Archibald Munro, veterintary surgeon, Altrincharn, said be saw the animal the same night, and it was an old animal with weary feet, becoming very lame, and it was not fit for work.—Defendant said he had never been up for a case of this kind before. He had only had the horse a week. It had been working in Warrington, and not been stopped. The Chairman (Hr. J. W. Sidebotham) said the society had called a veterinary surgeon, and defendant must pay samething towards the cost. Reduction in Sugar. ASIILEY'S, I,Buttermarket st.—Adv t House Collapses in Colborne-street.</p>
        <p>A remarkable accident occurred in Golborne-stre2t, at about half-past ten on Tuesday morning, the front of a house (No. 14), occupied by Mr. Henry Barnes, milk dealer, suddenly collapsing. It appears that on Monday and Tuesday morning a number of workmen had been engaged in excavating along the footpath and had formed a trench in which to put some pipes , to hold the telegraphic cable between Warrington and Manchester. The pipes were being laid close to the foundations of the houses and this, combined with the heavy rain which fell on the previous evening, may possibly account for the catastrophe. The front of the house was completely wrecked and the contents of both the bedroom and the room on the ground floor were exposed to the view of the•public. The men at , work in the trench hearing a noise made a rush for ' safety and all escaped but one, whose arm and wrist were slightly injured by the falling bricks. So hurried was his flight that he left his jacket behind him, end it was buried in the debris. Miss Barnes was in the room downstairs machining when the accident occurred, and fortunately escaped unhurt, although she received a great shock,and her work was covered by the debris, which fell outwards. Some of the furniture fell into the street and the other part was removed as soon as possible to a place of safety. TRY GILLETTE SAFETY RAIORS for comfort in shaving.—Aaent, J. S. GARLA.ND, 51-55, Bridge- street, Warrington,—Advt. The Leather Thefts.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court, on Tuesday, Charles Steele. Arthur-street, Joha Maginn, Erwoocl-street, and Frederick Broomfield, Gonlden-street, were cSarged no remand with breaking into and entering the works of Randall Bros. Ltd., Tanners-lane, at about ten p.m. on Monday, May 25th and stealing eight bends of leather valued at £7.—Mr. Browne prosecuted. on behalf of Messrs. Randall Bros.— The evidence showed that the works were left secure between seven and eight o'clock on Monday night, May 25th. The following morning the woodwork of one windows was found to be torn off,and that the bottom shutter had been removed. On investigation it was discovered that the leather was missing. Broomfield sold some of the leather to Charles Harris, shoemaker, 28, Green-street, and attempted to sell more to Aaron Clarke, boot and shoe dealer„ 97. a, Liverpool-road. Clarke, however, was suspicious and communicated with the police with the result that Broomfield was arrested by P.C. Woolacott. When charged Steele and Magian said : "Yes, we took it ; " and Broom- field replied : "I didn't break in ; they brought the leather doxn to me last night."—The prisoners were committed to the next Liverpool Quarter Sessions.— Steele, Maginn and George Ellis, Elizabeth-street, were then charged with breaking and entering the same tannery on-April 10th, and stealing six bends of leather, valued at 103. —P.C. Woolaeott said he arrested the three prisoners on the afternoon of Tuesday, May 20th. He charged them, and they all replied "Yes, there were six bends."—Prisoners had nothing to say and they were co:n-nitted to the Liver- pool Quarter Sessions.—Broomfield and Steele applied for bail, but it was refused.</p>
        <p>Reduction in Sugar. AsaLEv's,4,Butteraiarket st.—Advt Solicitor Sues for Fees.</p>
        <p>At the Warriugton County Court on Thursclay,before his llonour Judge Shiress Will, K.C., Mr. Thomas S. Steel, solicitor, sued Mr. and Mrs. Once, of Penketh, for 303. fat professional services.—Mr. A. Willett appeared for the plaintiff and Mr. A Browne for Mr. Grice. —Mr. Willett stated that in January 199 i Mr. Steel was consulted by Mrs. CI rice with reference to taking proceedings against her husband for alleged cruelty. A summons was taken out but after some correspondence Mr. Steel suggested that the parties should meet at his office with a view to settlement. The male defendint (lid not attend but he (Mr. Willett) understood the parties went to live together ag tin. Application had lieen made to Hr. Grice each quarter for payment of the account but no communication of any kind had been received from him. It was perfectly clear that the costs of proceedings taken for separa- tion aad alimony were recoverable as necessaries, and the wife had the implied authority of the husband. Mr. Browne said the defence was that there was no necessity for the wife to consult Mr. Steel. Mr. Price had never seen him and had not received the accounts. It was the wife s debt and she could pay. A solicitor could recover costs agiinst the husband in the event of the wife consulting him if the suit went on to a successful issue, or if the probability of success cauld be shown. There was, however, no evidence of that, for the husband denied cruelty. The fact that the parties came together again showed there was no probability of the proceedings being successful.—His Honour said he believed the contrary to In the case, the husband, thinking that the wife would succed, giving way in time—There we; no defence to the action, and he gave judgment for the plaintiff with costs.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0121" n="art0121">
        <head>PENKETH.</head>
        <p>PENKETH.</p>
        <p>WESLEYAN MEN'S BIBLE CLA.SS.—This class held their usual open monthly service in the chapel on Sunday. In the absence of the President (Ir. C. E. Parker) who is away from home, Mr. W. Moores took the chair. The Bible class choir under tho directorship of Mr. D. Plumpton, gave a good rendering of the anthem " I will lift up mine eyes," Mr. J. J. Foden taking the solo. Messrs. Hampson, M. Buddin, S. Plum pton and E. Ashton sang in a quartette. 11,1r.Davey Smith, of Warrington, gave a rousing address, taking for his subject " The man with one idea." Mr. G. Weaver officiated at the organ. WESLEY G I;ILD.— On Saturday the members and friends of the Wesley Guild held their first ramble of the season. The place chosen was Lymm, which was reached about four o'clock. After a ramble round the pArLy partook of tea. The Rev. E. J. Gawne accom- panied the party, and the Secretary (Miss A. Davies) was also present. Penketh was reached again about nine o'clock, and all were agreed that a most enjoyable afternoon had been spent. . . .</p>
        <p>I NEW SCROOL FOR PENKETIL-GrOßt interest was ; aroused in the Penketh district on Monday, the occasion , being the formal opening of the new Council school by ' Mr. C. B. F. Borron (chairman of the Earlestown ,1 sub-district of the County Education authority). The ' school has cost from £4,000 to £5,000 and provides ! accommodation for 350 mixed scholars and infants. When the doors were opened on Monday morning 271 names were entered on the boQks. The building takes , the place of the old Wesleyan school and is to meet the educational needs of Penketh and portions of Cuerdiey, Bold, and Sankey, and those districts will have to meet three-fourths of the expenditure incurred ' in proportion to the number of scholars they send, the remaining fourth being contributed by the Comity Council. In.addition to a large playground, there is a plot of land which the scholars will use as gardens. This land will be in readiness as a site for an infant school when the needs of the district calla for the removal of tbe children from the present school.—At the opening ceremony Dr. Murray presided and reviewed the educa- tional work already accomplished in Penketh. Mr. Borron having opened the school was cordially thanked on the proposition of Mr.Withenshaw seconded by Mr. Midvrood.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0122" n="art0122">
        <head>NAIVE EXCLISES.</head>
        <p>NAIVE EXCLISES.</p>
        <p>Prisoner (at Highgate on Nlonday) : The bicycle was leaning against the dusthole, and I thought it was intended for the dustman. I took it to save a dust- man trouble.</p>
        <p>Defendant (at Acton, charged with stealing a lady's that worth L 5 ss. from a garden) : " I thought it had been thrown there by someone who was drunk."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0123" n="art0123">
        <head>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES.</head>
        <p>PHOTOGRAPHIC NOTES.</p>
        <p>By Veronica.</p>
        <p>NATURE PHOTOGRAPHY BY DAY AND BY NIGHT.</p>
        <p>Almost every year brings at least one nev line of work in which photographers are tempted to specialise, and this summer it seems as if a great deal of attention is likely to be given to night work in the open air. The example of Mr. Robert Dykes, one of the most success- ful of night photographers, and the first to write a book upon the subject, is being followed by many of his readers, and another author, Mr. F. C. Snell, has carried the subject still further by applying the methods of night photography to the study of natural history.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0124" n="art0124">
        <head>FLOWERS AWAKE AND ASLEEP.</head>
        <p>FLOWERS AWAKE AND ASLEEP.</p>
        <p>It is one of the peculiarities of photography that the moment it leads its votaries into a new Held it opens for them a whole realm of interest and useful work. Mr. F. C. Snell, in his" Nature Studies by Night and Day "devotes a part of a chapter to the sleeping habits of flowers and plants, a subject that is practically nnworked and that might well occupy a whole book, and keep an ardent photographer-botanist busily engaged for many years. Mr. Snell shows that the sleep of plants is even more comprehensive and intricate than the sleep of animals. Some species sleep merely by closing their flowers, which re-open the next day ; others compose themselves by complicated movements of the leaves, which are sometimes connected with, but at other times quite independent of, the closing of the flowers; and in some cases the flowers remain open while the leaves go to sleep. Even if only the sleeping habits of the flowers are considered, there is ample scope for a great deal of work.</p>
        <p>EASILY ACCESSIBLE EXAMPLES. Amongst the typical sleeping flowers photographed by Mr. Snell are the common wood anemone the wood sorrol (which sleeps with leaves as well as blooms), the goat's beard and the convolvulus or greater bind-wood. Of course, hundreds of others might easily be added, which are to be found in all parts of the country, even close to groat towns, and which can be photographed very easily either by day or by night.</p>
        <p>FLOWERS AND THEIR INSECT GUESTS. . It is well known that many flowers Are dependent upon their insect visitors for their fertilisation. with- out which their fruit would not mature and the flowers would cease to exist. The sleeping habits of the plants become so well-known to the particular insects with which they are connected that flowers which wake by night are visited and fertilised only by insects which sleep during the day. This connection between the flowers and the fertilising insects is one which, so far as I know, has never been illustrated by photography. except to a small extent, but very beautifully, by Mr. F. Martin-Duncan in some of his marvellouskinotographic living pictures of insect-life. CLOUD PHOTOGRAPHY.</p>
        <p>Almost all photographers photograph clouds more or less frequently for the sake of having negatives from which they can print clouds into their "bald-headed " landscapes, but few have realised the great interest and usefulness of a serious photographic study of cloud forms. One of the chapters in Mr. SnelPs nature study book is devoted to this subject, and he shows a con- siderable number of examples of different kinds of cloud. He reminds his readers that orthochrornatic plates, preferably slow ones, should be used for cloud work, and that they should always be supplemented by a yellow light-filter. He points out that the photo- graphing of clouds alone is not nearly so difficult as the securing of clouds and landscape on the same nega- tive. He recommends an exposure of from 1-25 to 1-15 of a second with a lens working at Fl 6, a slow isochromatic plate and a medium light-filter (yellow) ; or for sunset effects he recommends twice this exposure. Personally, I should be inclined to give very considerably longer and should still expect to obtain softly gradated and quite sufficiently plucky negatives. PROTECTIVE MIMICRY AND PROTECTIVE</p>
        <p>CONTRAST. Many photographers have dealt with those insects whish are concealed from their foes by their extreme similarity to the bark, leaves or twigs of the plants on which they rest, and Mr. Snell has dealt with this same subject, but has also given some attention to that extremely fascinating and recently developed theory of "warning " colours, which suggests that many insects have developed conspicuous colouring and marking to act as a reminder to insect-feeding birds that these particular species are unpalatable. The theory seems far-fetched at first sight, but it has the support of many careful observant nature-lovers, and it should be capable of being illustrated even more fully than Mr. Snell has done. PHOTOGR %THING MOTHS AT NIGHT. Mr. Snell gives a good deal of useftii suggestion with regard to this work, and has some curious unusual illustrations. He recommends the" sugaring" methods used by the butterfly and moth collector to attract his victims to a given tree, and points out that many species which are too small to be very effective as Isingle figures make interesting pioturea when a dozen 1 of them can be photographed together on a little patch 'of bark. Even wood-lice when attracted by the sugar form curiously interesting subjects for flash-light, reminding one of herds of pigs feeding. Earwigs, beetles, spiders and slugs form interesting subjects, and all may be treated very easily by flash-light. The only real difficulty. seems to be that if a light be turned upon the feeding Insects for the purpose of focussing many of them will move away at once. It is necessary, therefore, to focus by measurement or to focus on the spot before the insects come. For the photographing Mr, Snell recommends the familiar home-made tobacco pipe flash lamp with about half a thimble-full of magnesium powder when using an extra rapid plate.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0125" n="art0125">
        <p>r DEAR</p>
        <p>Quite a large number of essays on "How I spent Easter" have come to hand during the past week, and, I am sorry to say, are too late, as the prize one was published last week. Further down you will see the letter of thanks from A. Arnold for the prize I sent him. I hope my readers will remember the Conundrum Competition; I am sure many of you can send me quite a lot if you will try. I am afraid my members could not arrive at the correct answer to the "egg seller" puzzle. May Shortridge, Florence Dark, Gwendoline Kidell, and Bessie Lewis sent correct answers,</p>
        <p>The Whitsuntide holiday will soon be here. i hope you will all have a nice time. Next month I shall announce several new competitions in connection with the summer holidays. With love, Ulcers NED.</p>
        <p>Part I. In a forest in Germany there dwelt a poor wood cutter. His cottage was a tiny, tumble- down place, but he could afford no better .. house, and there he dwelt with his three , children and wife. He had to work hard cutting down the trees, sawing them, and taking them to the nearest town to be sold. It was a long way, and his wife often went with him -. in a poor little _donkey cart. One day the man and his wife had gone to the town and left ' their children, two boys and a girl, in the cottage. It was cold and they had no warm - clothes, being indeed very ragged; but they played about to keep themselves warm. By and by it began to rain, the raindrops pattering sharply on the roof. All at once the door was flung open and a tall, well-dressed man came _ in. Hie clothes were very wet, for he had no umbrella. Now this was the king himself, who was fond of walking about in the forest just ' , like a common man. He had hoped to find a , fire in the cottage and to dry his clothes there, but though there was none he asked the • children if he might stay with them till,.the % rain ceased. They were rather ally with the , strange gentleman, but he spoke to them kindly , and soon found out all about them. "Won't father and mother get wet?" said the eldest boy, after telling the man where they had gone. "They have left the umbrella behind." "Oh, . you have an umbrella?" "Yes, sir, a fine one, ; too. Would you like te• see it?" said the boy. . "I should, indeed," said the stranger. Part It. •• The boy stood on a chair and took tho um- brella from its place on the top of au old cupboard. It was an ugly thing, made of red . '. cotton with thick whalebone ribs, and was big enough to cover a whole family. "Isn't it a beauty?" asked the little girl. The king smiled. "Will you lend it to me?" he asked. The children looked amazed. "Have you far to go?" said one. "Yes; the walk will take me nearly an hour." • "Will you bring it back, sir? We should be scolded if we lost our ;1 fine umbrella." "Oh, yes, you shall have it to-morrow, full of florins, too." The eldest boy thought this was too good to be true. " That's easy to say," he cried. "Well, you will see. It is raining as hard as ever, and I want very P. much to get home, so I am sure you will lend it to me. 5' Yes, we will," said the girl. ".1 4 should not like you to get any wetter. Take care not to spoil it," she added. "Father 1 would be angry if it were broken." The king said that he would be very careful, and went away, walking as fast as he could, for he had ~.1 -A to meet his lords at the palace. Fancy their -4 surprise when he came in with a big, cheap, .. red umbrella! A</p>
        <p>Pare 111. By this time the wood cutter and his wife had reached home, very tired and cross, for they were wet to the skin. The children said nothing t 6 them about the umbrella, thinking there would be a fine surprise next morning. Next day it rained harder than ever, and the wood cutter went to the cupboard to find his um- btella. V Hullo, hullo," he said, not finding it. The children came up and told how they had lent it to a gentleman. "Lent it, indeed! Who was he and where does he live?" "I forgot to ask him his name," said the elder boy. "But he was a very nice gentleman, ' said the girl. "Nice, yes, but we shall never see our umbrella again. You children must not lend my things without leave, mind that." Just as he went outside to harness his donkey, a closed carriage drove' up, drawn by two horses. It stopped before the cottage door, and two well-dressed men got out, carrying with some trouble the big red umbrella. It was open and upside down, and the wood cutter was surprised to see that it was nearly full of bright shining silver coins. "Here, my man," said one of the gentlemen, "here is your umbrella. My master the king thanks your children for the loan of it, and sends you the present he promised." The men placed the umbrella in the wood cutter's hands and then drove away. You may be sure they were all pleased for that blessing, the reward of kindness. ANNIE BARNES. THE GRAND PARTY. Miss Nellie McCarty .gave a grand party,</p>
        <p>And who do you think were there? Marigold Mary and Viscount Canary, Red Tiger Lily and Joe Daffodilly, And Violet fragrant and fair. Saucy Miss Buttercup and Johnny Jump-up— A boat load from over the bay. They danced to a fiddle with "Hands dowa the middle," Ate oysters and ices, rich sauces and spices, And went to bed sick the next day. AMY DUDLEY.</p>
