      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0126" n="art0126">
        <p>-f--</p>
        <p>Welcome to • Dorothy Dudley, Ethel Owen, Violet Hearn, Elsie Chamberlin. Mirrors have been sent to Alice Wright, Hilda Wright (kindly send address), Hetty Rolling, G. M. Kendall, E. Chamberlain. •</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0127" n="art0127">
        <p>__+__</p>
        <p>Dear Uncle Ned,—Many thanks for the nice book you sent me. I am more than pleased with it. I did not think I should be j3o for- tunate, but then, there is nothing like trying. I think, as I have been successful this time, I may be so again, so I shall often send you stories. Again thanking you for the beautiful present, I remain, your affectionate nephew,</p>
        <p>A. A:RNOiD.</p>
        <p>WHAT SANTA SANTA CLAUS BROUGHT. Of course a doll at the top you'll see With her arms stretched out to you, And down the leg of the stocking `there'll be A whip and a trumpet too. Some nuts and marbles the ankle will hold, In the heel there's room for a ball, The foot is swollen with sweets I am told, And surely that muit be all. Not quite, for dear old Santa's so kind To the bairnies he loves you know, A bright new shilling at last you'll find Just here in the stocking toe. JAN]: BURGESS.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0128" n="art0128">
        <head>CRY CRY OF FIRE.</head>
        <p>THE CRY CRY OF FIRE.</p>
        <p>A workman had to leave home for his work every morning at six o'clock, and returned two hours leer for his breakfast He sometimes found that it was not ready for him. One morn- ing the fire had not been lit, and his wife was still fast asleep in. bed. He thought that he would try and cure her of her lazy habits, so he shouted as loud as he could, " Fire I Fire! His wife awoke, and hearing the cry of fire, came running downstairs in a great hurry. She asked him where the fire was, an he replied, "in every house but ours." HILDA WRIGHT.</p>
        <p>-+.-</p>
        <p>In a garden was laid .a most beautiful maid</p>
        <p>As ever was seen in the morn, She was made a wife the first day of her life And died before she was born. Answer: Eve. Why is the Isthmus of Suez like the first "u" in " cucumber?" Answer: Because It is between two seas.</p>
        <p>Suppose your footman-John knocks at your door, what great Scotsman would yoirthink of? Answer: John Knox. What three letters of the alphabet will name three English rivers? Answer: D. X. Y. (Dee, Eire, and Wye). ANNIE BARNES.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0129" n="art0129">
        <head>-4-- DOCTOR'S 4ORDERS.</head>
        <p>-4-- DOCTOR'S 4ORDERS.</p>
        <p>The boarders were alarmed one night by what sounded like a man running at a tremendous pace in one of the upper rooms. However, as it came from the second floor front room of the new boarder nothing was said. The next night the same running noises were heard. Still it was thought best to say nothing. But the third night the noise differed. The boarders huddled together in the parlour as the chande- liers shook, as the man above apparently came down with a thump thump that fairly shook the house. Two men were delegated to see what was the matter. "What in the world is the matter up here?" asked one of the men, as the door was opened by the new boarder apparently breathless. "Why," came the answer between gasps of breath, "I'm taking my medicine." " Medicine " echoed th 6 men. "Yes," said the man, as he dropped into a chair from sheer exhaustion. "It's tougher on me than it, is on you. But the doctor said I should take it two nights running and then skip the third night." GERTRUDE CARTER.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0130" n="art0130">
        <head>STILL CHEERFUL. CHEERFUL.</head>
        <p>STILL CHEERFUL. CHEERFUL.</p>
        <p>When the country was much flooded some years ago, a farmer driven to his roof by the rising water refused to be distressed._ _</p>
        <p>"Washed all your fowls away?" asked a man in a boat below.</p>
        <p>"Yes, but the ducks swam."</p>
        <p>, "Tore up your orchard trees?" next asked the boatman.</p>
        <p>"Don't mind it much," replied the farmer; the crop was a failure." . . . •</p>
        <p>• "But the water is right up to your windows, man l"</p>
        <p>"Well, them windows wanted washing," re- torted the farmer. ELSIE THURSEY.</p>
        <p>THE HIDDEN TREASURE. A farmer once, about to die, Called on his children to come nigh. "I leave," he said, "a small estate, But means enough to make it great. For know a treasure it contains If you to search will take the pains!" He died. The sons dug all the ground, But there no hidden treasure found. Yet so productive was the soil, The crop soon overpaid their toil. "This was the hoard our sire foretold," Said one, whenethey their corn had sold. Work, then, my boys, with heart and mind, And labour you will fruitful find! Your loving nephew, BEnTIE SMALL.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0131" n="art0131">
        <p>-+--</p>
        <p>RIDDLES. Why is a kiss like a seandal2—Because it goes from mouth to moutl3. Why was the first day of Adam's life the longest 2—Because it had no Eve. Why is a mouse like a load of hay I—Because .the eatt'll eat it. What is that which must stand before it can Sit I—An M.P.</p>
        <p>Why' is death like the letter E ?—Because A is at the end of life. FRED NUTTALL.</p>
        <p>__+__</p>
        <p>"Why didn't you come when I rang?" "Because I didn't hear the bell." "Well, when you don't hear the bell you mug. come and tell me so."</p>
        <p>_4___</p>
        <p>"I've learned a new word to-day," proudly exclaimed little Gladys. " T-o-o spells too." "Then what does t-o spell?" aGked grandpa. "I think it spells one," was the answer. Mother: "History repeats itself." Willie: "Well, why don't it repeat itself when I'm trying to learn it?"</p>
        <p>Talkative Lady (to fireman): "And what is that strap that goes under your chin worn for ?"</p>
        <p>Fireman: "lids strap, Madam, is given to us to rest our chins when our jaws get tired."</p>
      </div>
