      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0137" n="art0137">
        <head>AN MU:3T3VN PARAGRANI DO YOU LIgE GJOD NEWS?</head>
        <p>AN MU:3T3VN PARAGRANI DO YOU LIgE GJOD NEWS?</p>
        <p>GOOD NEWS FOR YOU?</p>
        <p>READ THIS.</p>
        <p>Mrs. S. Wyatt,l29, W4rgrave-raad,E4 des to wn, says : "My daughter suffered with rheurnitism in the arms and legs for about two years, sometimes she was nnich worse than others, especially in damp weather. We had a good deal of treatment for, her but she never got more than passing relief until she used Doan's pills and these did her a great deal of good; three boxes quite relieved her, and we can give the medicine the highest praise; it is the best thing she has ever used. I have much pleasure in recommending Doan's pills for rheumatism. (Signed), Sarah Wyatt."</p>
        <p>A headache, an aching back, pains in the limbs, spells of dizziness, broken sleep, a continual feeling of tired- nes% cold hands and feet, peiness under the eyes, twinges of rheumatism, urinary trouble of any kind, all these are warnings that your kidneys are not doing their work properly ; upon their work depends the health of the whole body. The kidneys filter impurities from the blood. If those deadly poisons remain in the blood, disease of the most painful and destructive character must follow, such as gravel, dropsy, rheumatism lumbago, Bcc. It is no use taking medicines to alleviate the painful sympto us of these maladies. Begin at once with Doan's pills which act directly upon the kidneys, stimulating and strengthening them. They restore diseased kidneys to health and keep them healthy.</p>
        <p>Doan's Btulatch° Kidney Pills are two shillings and ninepence per box. (six br,x,ss for thirteen shillings and niaepence). Of all chemists and stores, or post free, direct from Foster-MoClellan Co., 8, Wells-street, Oxford-street, London, W.. Be sure you get exactly the same kind of pills that Mrs. Wyatt had.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0138" n="art0138">
        <head>– APPLETON RIFLE CLUB.</head>
        <p>- APPLETON RIFLE CLUB.</p>
        <p>Mont hiy Prize Competition.</p>
        <p>The first of the series of five monthly competitions was held at the Range last Saturday when GO entries were received and spirited contests took place. All the competitions were fired at 100 yards range. under Bisley conditions. The following scores were made : Bronze Badge Class (Service Rifle). Seven shots 3 inch bull's eye; seven shots, disappear- ing figure. Possible 56. A. Down A. Warburton 2B J. E. Hobin 22 CLUB MEDAL CLASS (MINIATURE RIFLE). Conditions as last. Possible 56. • J. E. Hobin 46 F. M. Cowley 43 A. Warburton 3l. OPEN SWEEPSTAKES (ANY RIFLE). Seven shots, 3 inch bull's eye. Possible 33. P. D. Gardiner F. M. Cowley 29 F. S. Bennion 2B J. E. Hobin 2B T. I. Fletcher 27 A. Warburton 27 E. Plinston 27 H. Davenport E. Pollard A. Down J. Towers l9 A. Pitt l9</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0139" n="art0139">
        <head>NEW BRPGIITON TOWER.</head>
        <p>NEW BRPGIITON TOWER.</p>
        <p>The resumption of the regular Sunday Night Concerts at New Brighton Tower Theatre commence Whit Sunday with Madame Ada Crossley as the soloist, and as this will be the last appearance of this famous contralto prior to her extended tour of Australia a large and enthusiastic audience is expected. On Sunday night Madame Crossley will sing "Caro mio hen" (Giordani), "Look up, oh heart" (Teresa do el ltiego), "Nearer my God to Thee "(Lewis Carey), "Laud of hope and Glory" (Elgar). In addition under the conductorship of Mr. Thomas Manner, the full Tower Orchestra will render a -number of the best selections of their extensive repertoire. On Saturday, June 13th, the nth annual Brass Band Contest will take place on the open Plaza of the tower, commencing at noon, and continuing until all the bands have been judged. The prizes this year aggregate £2OO, and the contest promises to be the most spirited in the history of the Tower Festivals. The Cummins-Brown Wild West is the potent attraction to all visitors as well as a strong magnet to draw visitors to that resort.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0140" n="art0140">
        <head>Took it as a Ransom.</head>
        <p>Took it as a Ransom.</p>
        <p>At the Borough Police Court, on Puesday, before Mr. C. Jackson Holmes (chairman) and Mr. A. Jolley, Paul Wolfgang Brunt, 3, Froghail-lane, was charged with stealing from the dwelling-house of his father, John Brunt, 3, Froghall.lane, on Monday afternoon, a lady's jacket, valued at 53.—He pleaded not guilty.— Mr. Steel appeared to prosecute, and said that at about , 2.20 on M mday afternoon prisoner's sister, Mary Annie Brunt, missed the jacket. Later in the after- noon, prisoner's brother, Richard, met him in Scot- : land-road asith the jacket, and asked him where he had got it from. Prisoner replier I" From the house, and I will take everything there is." Richard Brunt communicated with P.C. Sankey, and prisoner was traced to a shop in Allen-street, where ho was arrested. When charged he said "My intentions were not to steal, but only for a lark." It was alleged that prisoner had disposed of other things in the house. Prisoner was formerly a student at the Schaal of Art and he hal artistic tendencies. Ile (Mr. Steel) believed that if he would only devote himself to his work he could earn a good livelihood. He would not, however, and his father was prostrated with grief though he did not wish to have him sent to gaol, and he (Mr. Steel) suggested that he should be bound over lunder the Probition of O.Tendera Act, and that one of the conditions should be that he most ko ,p away from his father's house.—Richard Brunt, Mary Annie -Brunt, and P.C. Sankey gave evidence—Prisoner said things at the house had been deliberately hidden, so that he could ha accused of taking them. The reason he took the coat was because a card, which he very much treasured, had been t Ikea from his pocket, and he was holding the coat as a ransom until he got his card back.—Prisoner was discharged with a caution</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0141" n="art0141">
        <head>PALPITATION AFTER MEALS.</head>
        <p>PALPITATION AFTER MEALS.</p>
        <p>Cured by Clias. Farde's Bile Beans.</p>
        <p>" Six or seven years ago," says Mr. Duncan Fraser, of 52, Huntley-street, Inverness, " I used to suffer awfully from palpitation after Teals, and at night when I went to bed I used to jump up very much frightened because I thought my heart was stopping. " When I went out for a walk I was attacked by fita of dizziness, and the palpitation was so severe that I had repeatedly to rest. The result of Chas. Forde's Bile Beans far exceeded my expectation, for soon 1 was able to go out for a walk without feeling dizzy or suffering from palpitation. Charles Forde's Bile Beans completely cured the indigestion which was at the root of my trouble. Then I could sleep soundly, enjoy my food, and find pleasure in life. I have not knotrn a day's sickness since." Chas. Forde's Bile Beans, the original world- famed family medicine, which cured Mr Fraser, are sold by all chemists, Is. 11d. and 23. 9J., per box, and can be obtained direct from The Bile Bean Manufacturing Co., 45, Cow Cross-street, London,E.C.</p>
