      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0160" n="art0160">
        <head>MR. DODDS &amp; MR. ROBERTS. The Truth about the Filleged Compact.</head>
        <p>MR. DODDS &amp; MR. ROBERTS. The Truth about the Filleged Compact.</p>
        <p>INTERESTING CORRESPONDENeE—NO SURRENDER OF NON.TRTIDING PRINeIPLES.</p>
        <p>Executive Committee of which have invited me to the meeting on Friday next. While a little doubtful whether one who has been an active member of the Licensing Committee of the Borough Bench as I have for several years past, should take any part in the public discuesion of a measure which one may have to help to administer later on, yet so strongly do I believe that the present Licensing Bill presents the finest opportunity for reform that has been offered by any Government in my time—not even excepting Bruce's Bill—that I should have put this objection on one side and taken my place along with others of our citizens in support of the principles of the Bill, bat for the fact that I cannot forget that the man who is to take the chair at the meeting on Friday, and who is the President of your League, is the man who, a few years ago, ' engineered an attack upon myself in my character as a public man, in which from a score of platforms in Warrington I was held up to scorn and contempt as one who was guilty of" immoral and illegal trading" and unworthy of public confidence. In consequence of the agitation thus engendered, I have been pre- vented for over five years from giving to my native town that full measure of public service which I had hitherto conscientiously performed. The present situation is somewhat ironical. lam specially asked to join in a meeting to assist in , promoting a great moral " movement, to be pre- sided over by a man who has frequently characterized me as an " immoral " trader. When Mr. Dodds has publicly withdrawn the aspersions so unjustly made upon my character and reputation as a public', roan, I shall be prepared to consider the desirability of associating with him in progressive work, but until then, my self-respect snakes this quite Impossible. It is due to me and to my many friends and co-workers in the Temperance cause in Warrington that the reason of my absence from Friday's meeting should not be misunderstood. I am.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0161" n="art0161">
        <head>MR. DODOS' REPLY TO M. ROBERTS</head>
        <p>MR. DODOS' REPLY TO M. ROBERTS</p>
        <p>-- Sir,-1 had hoped that Mr. Roberts' decision to fall I into line with the views so long expounded in War- rington by the Non-Traders would end a long-standing controversy, but the communication sent by him to your contemporaries last wertle has re-opened the whole matter, and in justice to the Non-Traders of Warring- ton and to myself, I am now compelled to take the public into confidence, and describe exactly what has taken place. I have drawn up separately a complete statement, which is appende 1, showing the correspon- dence which has passed, with such explanation as is necessary, an examinatioa of which will prove the entirely misleading nature of Mr. R3berts' statement of last week. First, so far as Mr. Roberts himself is concerned,l I have never verbally or otherwise withdrawn one word of the statements I made during the agitation.' At the meeting of April Bth, to which he refers, I stated distinctly that I bore him no personal that I had never done so, that I had never made the statements his letter to Mr. Guest requested that I should withdraw, and that in the form he had put the ; charges against the Slate Company and himself, they were not true, but as no one ever said they were true, I it is plain the only inference to be drawn from this is ; that Mr. Roberts' own draft of what the Non-Traders ! stated was slip-shod and incorrect. He has taken advantage of the position in a char- acteristic way. First he drew up a statement of what transpired at the meeting,in which by the substitution' of the little word " NOR " for" AND "an important, I indeed a most material change is made ! This docu- ment he presented to the four persons who formed the! committee, and secured their several signatures to it ' as a correct statement of what transpired. Strange to I say it bears the date of the meeting, but in actual I fact it was not signed until several days later, and the signatories do not appear to have noticed the significance of the verbal change I have referred to ! Moreover they overlooked the fact that whet Mr. Roberts' statement omits is of more importance (to everyone but Meßoberts) than what it includes. They heard me tell Mr. Roberts, for instance, that if he attempted to return to the Council while retaining his interest in the Slate Company we should fight him on the same grounds as before, The Corn nittee also heard Mr. Roberts thereupon toll me ; plainly and distinctly that it was his intention, before again seeking a seat in the Town Council, to transfer i I his interests n the Slate Company to his two sees, that his position in relation to the Non-Trading issue I should be absolutely unassailable. This of course gave ' away the whole show. It vindicated the whole con- tention of the Non-Traclers,it completed their triumph ! Yet Mr. Roberts with the implied acquiesence of the four members of the Committee deals with this as a side issue, in a parenthesis! "Subject to the ques- tion of principle referred to in the foregoing letter " (reads Mr. Roberts' precious summary) "J. Dodds also said he should be glad to see H. Roberts back again in municipal life, and that he would not oppose him doing so." Herein we have the whole contention of the Non- Traders aceepted —the most remarkable climbing down that has been seen (luring the Non-Trading struggle ! I rejoice in it, but it must be honestly carried out. We must have no more promises without performances as in the case of Mr. Roberts's now notorious declara- tion of November 20th, 1900, to the effect that he was glad the trading issue had been then introduced by Councillor Forshaw ; that every public man was then on his tile], and that he (Mr. Roberts) had decided that so far as be and his family were concerned, they would not participate in any profits derived from the trading of the Slate Company with the Warrington Corporation. Had Mr. Roberts carried out this undertaking I doubt if we should have had so pro- tracted a Non-Trading agitation. But it was not carried out ; the improper trading was never stopped and we have still ringing in our ears the historic remark of Mr. Roberts in the Parr Hall, in October 1001, and whilst he was Mayor of Warrington, that his trading " would go on " ! Surely the belated con- version of this trading" stalwart " should have had the foremost place in any " summary of the results " of the meeting of the Bth April last. As the one men whose approval of the summary signed by Mr. Roberts's four apparently unwitting dupes should have been obtained, I should, hail I been shown the precious document, have seen to it that the one out- standing result of the Conference, the result which had rendered it for ever memorable, was not hidden away in an unintelligible parenthesis. The curious thing is that the four members of the Committee, who were all men used to expressing their ideas in their own way, should have allowed Mr. Roberts to draw up a statement to suit his own case and his own ends, and meekly sign it at his bidding, notwithstanding its gross misstatements, its palpable inaccuracic3, and its astounding incompleteness ; and notwithstanding that it had not been submitted to me as mentioned. With this document in his pocket Mr. Roberts has all these men firmly in the hollow of his hand ; either they must so ppert him or acknowledge that their names should never have been attached to an ex p ret,t,untrue, and absurdly incomplete statement. I trust they may have the courage to follow the latter course, but even if they do not, I am not without evidence. The outcome of the meeting was a sugges- tion that I should write a letter which would make the way clear for Mr. Roberts to support me on Parr Hall platform, and for his return to the Council, subject of course to his disposing of his interest in the Slate Company, and so coming into line with the Non-Trading principle, and his own specific declaration of November 1900 already referred to. Three of the Committee have drafted letters which they thought with some alterations I might sign. The terms of these draft letters clearly , show that Mr. Roberts' statement did not convey my attitude at all. They are all included in the appended statement, along with the amended letters I offered to sign or actually signed. I presume these were sub- mitted to Mr. Roberts, and finding they did not go as far as he desired, he rushed into print last week with the trophy he had already won, and charged me with having failed to carry out a promise made by me on the Bth April, the truth being that in compliance therewith I had written no less than three statements, for publication, none of which would appear to have satisfied him. I leave the members of the Committee to explain their action as best they can ; to me it is simply incomprehensible ! One last word. If Mr. Roberts intends to return to municipal work and to cease trading withthe Corpora- tion through the Slate Company or otherwise, as he has finally declared is his intention, the N in-Traders, as they have stated dozens of times, will have no grounds on which to oppose him. I say this frankly and freely, but for me to go farther and to attempt to ; " vindicate " Mr. Roberts (as he terms it) as though! the past did not exist is what I have not done, and never can do. If Mr. Roberts wanted to clear himself he had the opportunity when the Non-Trading inquiry was sitting. He refused more than once to appear bef we the commissioners, and it is too lat now to utter vain regrets. The evidence given before there is on record for all time, Ind a fresh perusal of it has con- vinced me that Mr. Roberts would have been better advised to have left matters where they were instead ef reaping up the past.</p>
        <p>I am, &amp;c., JAMES DODDS.</p>
        <p>June 46, 1908.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0162" n="art0162">
        <head>History of the Negotiations.</head>
        <p>History of the Negotiations.</p>
        <p>A week or so before the Parr Hall meeting on April 10th last in supp rt of the Licensing Bill, the Rev. C. Harvey Cook reported to the executive of the newly. formed Citizens Temperance League, under whose auspices the gathering was being organized, that Mr. H. It oberts had indicated to him a desire to attend the meeting and support the Bishop of Liverpool upon the platform. In consequence, the Secretaries were asked to send to Mr. Roberts two platform tickets and a copy of the formal invitation to seats ,upon the platform. On receipt of this invitation Mr. Roberts wrote the following letter to the Rev. John Yonge, as Tice-president of the League : LETTER FROM MR. ROBERTS TO REV. JOTTN YONGE. 3.4.03.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Yonge, I see from the enclosed letter that you are Vice- President of the newly formed Citizens' League, the</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0163" n="art0163">
        <head>• Faithfully, H. ROBERTS. I regretfully return the tickets addressed to my wife and myself. The Rey. John Yonge, Vice President, Warrington Citizea' Temperance League.</head>
        <p>• Faithfully, H. ROBERTS. I regretfully return the tickets addressed to my wife and myself. The Rey. John Yonge, Vice President, Warrington Citizea' Temperance League.</p>
        <p>-- Mr. Yonge enclosed the above to Mr. Dodds with a letter marked "iwieofc." Mr. Dodds is not publishing any letter so marked and regrets he cannot therefore record in Mr. Yonge's own words his great amtiety that Mr. Dodds should do all he could to render it possible for Mr. Roberts to join in the Parr Hall meeting. Mr. Roberts' letter was read to the league executive on April ith and although it did not contain so far as Mr. Docida was concerned, a single accurate statement (for instance Mr.Doclds had never in a single instance held Mr. Roberts up " to public scorn as one who was guilty of immoral and illegal trading " nor had he " engineered " any attack upon Mr. Roberts) he, Mr. Dodds—never having had the least ill will towards Mr. Roberts and desiring to help as much Bs he could, the only man left,so far as Mr. Dadde knew, who resented his efforts for public probity in War- rington—offered to retire from the presidency of the Citizens' League in favour of Jr. Roberts, and work for the League as a private in the ranks. The Executive of the League would not listen to Mr. Dodds' offer but decided to appoint a committee of four of its members, to arrange for a meeting, of Mr. Roberts and Mr. Dodds, with a view to the removal of any alleged hindrance to Mr. Roberts' attending the Parr Hall meeting. This committee consisted of the Rev. John Yonge, the Rev. C. Harvey Cook, Mr. C. E. Parker, and Mr. E. J. Guest. The very next day, April 8:11, Mr. Dodds was asked to meet the committee at the Winmarleigh Cafti. On arriving he found Mr. Roberts was not present, but a letter from him addressed to Mr. Guest was handed to Mr. Dodds. Ile perused the letter and at once said he would read it to the meeting and give his answer to each of Mr. Roberts' demands as he went along. Here is the letter with answers by Mr. Dodds: AI R. ROBERTS' LETTER. MR. DODDS' REPLIES. Warrington, I.—l have never stated April Bth, 190 S. that the Slate Company Dear Mr. Guest, and Mr. Roberts have lam very much obliged traded illegally and by your kind letter of immorally with the Cor- yesterday, but I do not poration of Warrington, think that at this stage an and lam prepared to say interview such as you sug- so publicly at any time. gest would be mutually "illegal" trading was serviceable, never charged by ma; If Mr. Dodds is prepared the trading was legal, but to publicly withdraw the wrong or highly improper. allegations so frequently I may have called it made to the effect : "immoral,'! but I do not I.—That the Warrington think so. I do not like Slate Company Ltd. and I the word, it is an ugly' personally have traded word, but it is the one and immorally" generally used to describe with the Corporation of the improper trading I Warrington. complained of, and which 2.—That the Warrington had had in Warrington, Slate Company Ltd. was the effect of demoralising formed for the purpose of contractors, °facials, and carrying on such" illegal members of the Council. and immoral "trading. If any non-traders have 3.—That I have ever or charged Mr. Roberts and in any degree mod my the Slate Company with position as a public man "illegal (lad immoral"! for personal or private trading, I arn prepared to gain and finally, if Mr. say publicly that such a ' Dodds will say publicly statement is not correct, that he has no personal as the trading was legal,! ill-will towards me, and though repreheniible and ! believes me to have acted immoral, that is in the in my public life as an sense in which the word honest and honourable man is applied to improper might to act (subject to a trading. difference between Mr. 2.—1 have never said the Dodds and myself on a Slate Company was formed question of principle only) for the purpose of carrying lam quite prepared to let on "illegal and immoral bye-gones be bye-gones, trading," nor do I believe and to resumo such friendly any other non-trader has relations with Mr. Dodds dons so. I have never as our mutual interests in heard the suggestion until progres4ve matters and to-day. This I am pre- the good government of pared to say publicly and our town renders desirable. anywhere at any time. Please convey to your 3.—1 really must decline colleagues on the Corn- to say whether in my mittee t.y warm apprecia- opinion Mr. Roberts has tion of their and your ever used his position as a efforts to effect a satisfac- public man, for personal tory settlement of this old or private gain ; it is a and regrettable feud. qnestion I ought not to be Faithfully, asked. I have never had H. ROBERTS. the slightest ill will E. J. Guest, Esq., J.P. towards Mr. Roberts, and I have said so publicly time upon time.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dodds' replies in regard to point No. 2 wen regarded as satisfactory, but in respect to Nos. 1 and 3 the committee thought that Mr. Roberts would not be satisfied, and that it would be useless to ask him to join them. Mr. Dodds said he could go no further. Ultimately it was decided to ask Mr. Roberts to attend, in the hope that a meeting between him and Mr. Dodds might remove any difficulties standing in the way of ' his attending the Parr Mall meeting. Mr. Guest telephoned to Mr. Roberts, but if he assured Mr Roberts " that there was a strong probability that Mr. ' Dodds would agree to comply with his terms " (as alleged in Mr. Roberts' statement) he had no warrant whatever from Mr. Dodds for doing so. On Mr. Roberts' arrival, Mr. Dodds repeated what he had stated to the Committee, but he was silent in regard to the third point so far as 'it required from him a statement that in his opinion Mr. Roberts had never used his positioa as a public man for his personal and private gain. Mr. Roberts said he desired to assure Mr. Dodds that on the question of the right or wrong of the trading which Mr. Dodds Lad condemned he could not and did not expect Mr. Dodds to make any con- cession ; he, Mr. Roberts thought the trading was right, Mr. Dodds thought it was wrong, and there so far as the past was concerned matters must rest. He went on to say that he had been kept out of the Council for 6 years, that he loved public work, and though physically weaker (being six years older) he was mentally as strong as ever. Ile desired to get back into the Council, nnd he asked what Mr. Dodds' attitude would be should he seek a seat. Mr. Dodds at once said "If you are going to trade as before we shall fight you.' Mr. Roberts then stated that he intended to transfer his interest in the Slate Company to his two sons. Mr. Dodds replied " If you do that; if you say that you have done that, I shall not go behind your assurance, and I should in such event be delighted to see you in the Council." The question of publishing a statement was then discussed. Mr. Dodds said he could only repeat what he had said that day ; perhaps Mr.Yonge would draft</p>
        <p>what be thought should do, and he would if possible accept it. Mr. Yonge said he thought Mr. Dodds might prepare and submit a draft to the Committee. i Mr. Dodds having then to return to his business, said he would do his best, and communicate with Mr. Guest. He left the Committee and Mr. Roberts; they had arranged to have tea together.—Two days later, that is on April 10th, the Parr Hall demonstation was held and Mr. Dodds in introducing Mr. R. )berts, welcomed him as a representative of a family which for two or three generations hid been in the fore- front of the temperance movement in Warrington. On April 1401, Ur. Dodds wrote to Mr. Cluast as follows :</p>
        <p>Peuketh House, April 140,1908.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Guest, I have not been ablo to give a moment to the subject of the Winmarlsigh Cafe' Conference until now. _ .</p>
        <p>The position is very difficult ; never having said that Mr. Roberts's company traded "illegally " with the Warrington Corporation,or that his Company was formed to trade " illegally and immorally," I really cannot see how I could help Mr. Roberts by stating (which is all I could state publicly) that I have never said these things, but if they have been said, they are, in my opinion untrue. So far I can't find that they have been said at all,although I was under the impression at the Conference that Mr. Pemberton had said Mr. Roberts had traded "illegally." Why therefore should I be asked even to withdraw, privately or publicly, something I have never said, or something it is alleged has been said by somebody elso ? This disposes of points 1 and 2 : as regards 3, I really cannot see why I should be asked to express an opinion ; my contention always was that no one should put himself in a doubtful position ; and as regards 4, I have never had the slightest ill- will towards Mr. Roberts, and have already, and time after time, said so publicly. No one can conic forward and truly say I have over said an unfriendly word of him privately. I strongly and sincerely believe that any public statement by me on the lines Mr. Roberts suggests, even if I could consistently make it, would do more harm than good to the end he has in view ; in my opinion what passed at Friday's Parr Hall Meeting ought to clear the ground and suffice. Yours sincerely, JAMES DODDS. E. J. Guest, Esq., J.P.</p>
        <p>LETTER FROM MR. GUEST. 4, Palmyra-square, Warrington. 14th April, 1938. My dear Mr. Dodds, In reply to your favour of this date, I think you understand that my position has been one of neutrality, and that I have no bias in this difficult question. As the controversy wont on I realised that the object to be gained was to put an end to Municipal Trading which was an object worth striving for. I did not enter the arena because I felt that some who were associated with you were too personal. As far as Mr. H. Roberts was concerned, I told him at the time the only course for him to adopt was to stop all trading between the Warrington Slate Company and the Corporation seeing that his interest in this Com- pany was so large. I have never gone further in the matter than this, and being a lover of peace, I have been anxious to steer clear. For some reason or other I have been chosen as one of four to seek to make peace, and it will be a great joy to me if I can in any way contribute to this most desirable end. As far as I can see, Mr. 11. Roberts has had six years of intense suffering which has affected his physical and mental health. Personally I think he has taken this matter too much to heart. But he is a man of extremely nervous temperament. Ho is anxious to be reinstated in regard to public opinion. He fools that you can do much to bring this about. As this is his earnest wish, I think you might write an open letter to him in the Warrington papers going as far as you can in this direction. By so doing you will help him to get back to public work, and he will be useful.</p>
        <p>With icind regards, Faithfully yours, E. J. GUEST.</p>
        <p>James Dodds, Esq.</p>
        <p>Mn. GUEST'S SUGGESTED LETTER FOR IklR. DODDS'</p>
        <p>SIGNATURE.</p>
        <p>4, Palmyra-square, Warrington, 14th April, 1909. Dear Mr. Dodds, I make bold to suggest the lines on which an open letter to Mr. 11. Roberts might be written to the papers. Yours faithfully, E. J. G. _</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Roberts. _ _ As lam given to understand that you have kept out of public work in the Corporation on account of what was said at the meetings held to Secure freedom from NTunicipal trading in the town, and that you are willing to take up this work again if I will exonerate you from charges which you say were made against you in these meetings. I.—l never charged you or the Warrington Slate Co., Ltd., with " illegal or immoral " trading. 2.—1 never said that the Warrington Slate Co., Ltd., was formed for the purpose of carrying on such illegal and immoral trading. 3.—lnever had any personal ill-will towards you, and have only felt it to homy duty to point out that as you were so largely interested in the Warrington Slate Co Ltd., that trading between this Company and the dorporation should ceaseso long as you were a member of the Corporation. I considered and still consider that such trading is improper, though not "illegal or immoral." Knowing as Ido what valuable work you did for the town through many years, no one will be better pleased than I to see you take up again this service for the good of the town you love so well. If any. thing was said at any of the meetings over which presided by any of the speakers winch was of the nature of the charges which I deny having made, I can only say I hereby disassociate myself from them, • and regret that they were uttered.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dodds could not accept the foregoing draft and wrote Mr. Guest as follows : MR. DODDS' REPLY.</p>
        <p>Penketh House, April 16th, 1908. My dear Mr. Guest, I most sincerely thank von for all the trouble you have so kindly taken, and I only wish I could, without absolute inconsistency, fall in with your ideas. But T am satisfied that I could not, in view of all the facts, write such a letter as you have drafted. At the same time, anything I could consistently do, I am quite willing to do. Point No. I.—Mr. Roberts' words in his letter are "illegal AND immoral." I have never charged Mr. Roberts with illegal AND hal-lora/ trading. Your words are "illegal OR immoral " trading. I have certainly condemned Mr.Roberts's trading through the Slate Company as improper, and I have probably characterized it as immoral, but I have not used the word " immoral "in any offensive sense, but simply as the word by which such tmding as I have con- demned is usually characterized in this country. I have never charged _‘lr. Roberts with " trading, and if any non-.trader can be proved to have done so, I can only say that, so far as I know, there has been no ground for such a charge. As I have already said, and as I have publicly stated in Parr Hall on several occasions, I have no personal towards Mr. Roberts. I still strongly recommend, in Mr. Roberts's interests, that the matter should rest as it is. With best regards, Yours sincerely. JAMES DODDS.</p>
        <p>E. J. Guest, Esq., J.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dodds received no reply to the above letter, but on Apiil 21st he had a call from Mr. Parker who suggested that the letter was not quite in harmony with what the Committee had understood Mr. Dodds to say at the meeting of Bth April. They went over the letter together, and ultimately Mr. Parker stated that he then realised for the first time that he and the Committee had inadvertantly misunderstood Mr. Dodds' denial of having ever said the Slate Company or Mr. Roberts had traded " illegally AND immorally" into an admission that the trading he had all almg condemned was neither " illegal NOR immoral ! " This Mr. Dodds said he had never ' admitted nor could he ever admit. llis contention aas that the statement alleged by Mr. Roberts had never been made, and he was prepared to say publicly or otherwise that the statement as made was not true, as the trading had been legal although highly improper and absolutely wrong and immoral (though ihe did not like the word) n the sense of being diametrically opposed to sound public policy which had always dictated that there should be no personal interest accruing, directly or indirectly, to public representatives, as a result of their membership of public bodies. In proof of his contention Mr. Dodds reminded Mr. Parker that at the meeting of the Bth April when asked by Mr. Roberts what his attitude would be if he (Mr. Roberts) sought to get back to the Council, Mr. Dodds unhesitatingly said to Mr. Roberts " If you intend to trade again we shall fight you again," whereon Mr. Roberts distinctly stated that he intended before seeking to return to the Council to transfer his interests in the Slate Company to his two sons. Mr. Dodds then said that under these circum- stances he should be glad to see Mr. Roberts back in the Council. Mr. Parker said he then now quite understood Mr. Dodds' position,and would in a day or two submit to him such a letter as he thought should satisfy the Committee and Mr. Roberts. On April 24th Mr. Dodds received the following letter from him.</p>
        <p>_</p>
      </div>
