      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0165" n="art0165">
        <head>Examiner, Saturday, June 6, 1903.</head>
        <p>The Examiner, Saturday, June 6, 1903.</p>
        <p>LETTER FROU :NIA. PARKER. Peaketh. April 23rd, 1908.</p>
        <p>My dear Mr. Dodds, In accordance with my suggestion which you approved, I enclose a draft letter, which, if you approve, you could alter as you think well, which, in my opinion, ought to satisfy Mr. Roberts and does not in any way surrender the principle for which you contend.</p>
        <p>The only point about it of importance is the use of the word" honourable," which I think you might stretch a point to use, seeing that your position is so well safeguarded. This is the special point which Roberts is anxious about, it is introduced in such a general way that it could not be interpreted as any surrender of principle,all the more so as the principle is so clearly safeguarded. We were to meet Mr. Roberts to-morrow afternoon but I fear unless we get something of this sort as a basis of settlement, our meeting will be futile. The whole town is anxious that this difficulty should be settled, and it would be a thousand pities if, after getting so near, all should be disappointed. I quite recognise that Roberts has more to gain than you have, but it would be a hind thing to lift this cloud off his life if you can consistently do it. We should have permission to publish the letter. Yours,</p>
        <p>• -C. E. PARKER. SUGGESTED STATEMENT BY MR. DODDS, DRAFTED BY</p>
        <p>MR. C. E. PARKER.</p>
        <p>Penketh, Referring to the interview with Mr. Henry Roberts along with yourself and other friends, I should like to reaffirm what I then stated, that I do not entertain and never have done, any ill-will or personal feeling against Mr. Roberts, my whole contention, and that of my associates being one of principle, and this is unchanged. I never stated that the trading of Mr. Roberts through the Slate Company was "illegal and immoral," or that this Company was formed for the purpose of trading with the Corporation. But I do ' consider the trading of a private limited company with a Corporation of which a predominant share- holder is a Councillor is improper and very liable to introduce abuses which are inimical to the public welfare, and this, if need be, we will continue to oppose. As far as Mr. Roberts is concerned, should ho ever desire to engage in municipal work, it will be a pleasure to me (subject to the maintenance of the principle named) to see him filling as heretofore an honourable and useful place in the public life of the borough of which he is a capable son and citizen.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dodds decided be could not fully approve the above statement. He wrote the following 'letter (to Mr. Guest) which was as near to the substance of Mr. Parker's draft as be felt he could go.</p>
        <p>MR. DODDS' OFFERED STATEMENT, BASED ON MR. PARKER'S DRAFT. Penketh House, April 24th, 190 S. 'Dear Mr. Guest, Referring to the interview with Mr. Henry Roberts, along with yourself and other friends, I should like to reaffirm what I then stated :</p>
        <p>Ist.—That I do not entertain, and never have entertained, any ill-will or personal animus towards Mr. Roberts, my sole contention and that of my associates having been one of principle, and this is unchanged. 2nd.—l have never stated that the trading of Mr. Roberts through the Slate Company illegal and immoral. What I have said is that such trading, while /c gal is morally wrong, i c., immoral. lam not sure that I ever used the word "immoral " in respect of this trading. I think the.word I used was "improper." At the same time Ido not con. sider the use of the word " immoral "unjustifiable. 3rd.—l have never said that the Slate Company was formed for tho purpose of trading "illegally and immorally " with the Corporation, but I have always held that the trading of a private limited Company through a predominant shareholder therein with a Coporation of which such predominant shareholder is a member, is highly improper, and is certain to introduce abuses inimical to the public welfare. Such trading, if need be, I shall continue</p>
        <p>to oppose. So far as gr. Roberts is concerned, should he ever desire to re-engage in municipal work, it would be a pleasure to me, subject to his observance of the principle I have set forth, to see him re-occupying a useful and an honourable place in the public life of our County Borough, of which he is a capable son and citizen. .</p>
        <p>You may use this letter as you choose. Yours faithfully, JAMES DODDS.</p>
        <p>E. J. Guest Esq., J.P.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dodds never heard whether the foregoing state- ment was acceptable to the Committee or to Mr. Roberts. On May Bth Mr. Yonge wrote a letter marked " prirate "to Mr. Dodds who at once replied as follows :</p>
        <p>REPLY TO RE7. JOHN YONGE. Penketh, May 9th, 1908.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Yonge, I thank you for your yesterday's valued note, and I deeply regret that I cannot write such a letter as that you have kindly drafted ; that indeed I can- not go further than I have gone in my last letter to Mr. Guest, which was drafted, substantially as it is, by Mr. Parker, who suggested that I might vary the phraseolgy, which is all I did, as a comparison of his draft with my letter will shew. There seems to be a feeling that in some way I have changed since we all met at the CAN'. lam not conscious of the slightest change. Briefly what occurred (that is 110 W of moment) was that in regard to Mr. Roberts's letter, I said I had never chargod him or his Company with "illegally awl impnorally" trading, nor had I ever said his company was formed to so trade, that possibly one , person had said Mr. Roberts had traded illegally and immorally and that I had not, nor had I ever had, the slightest ill-will towards Mr. Roberts. I further said the trading had been quiet " legal " but improper, and that if I had used the word " immoral "I did not like it, although it was the word generally used to describe the trading condemned by rue and by non-traders. I also said I was prepared to publicly say or write a.mordingly. My position was made absolutely clear at the meet- ing with Mr. Roberts, as you and all present will remember, when he asked me what my attitude would be should he seek to re-enter the Council. lat once said, "If you trade as before we shall fight you." he said he intended to transfer his interest in the Slate Co. to his two sons before seeking a Council seat, and my reply was that if he ceased to be interested in the Slate Co. or if the Slate Co. did not trade with the Council whilst he was in it, I should be delighted to see him there. lean only reiterate that in my opinion Mr. Roberts would be well advised to let the matter rest where it is, and with sincere regards, I remain, Yours faithfully. JAMES DODDS. The Rev. John Yonge.</p>
        <p>P.S.—On re-reading the note, I think I see where misunderstandings have arisen. Mr. Roberts com- plained of a charge of "illegal AND immoral trading.' I never made such a charge, and I frankly said it was untrue. The trading was /!gal ; it would ' have been libellous to have said it was illegal ; if it had been illegal we should not have held meetings, we should have gone to the law courts. If Mr. Roberts's letter had read (as you quote) " illegal 011 immoral," I should at once have said we had never charged the former, but that the latter had been charged, though by me in a different word, and could not be withdrawn. I have never "admitted " that the trading was " neither illegal NOR immoral," nor can I ever do so.—J. D.</p>
        <p>Mr. Dodds received no acknowledgment of the above letter, but on May 15th he had a call from Mr. Parker, to whom he pointed out why he could not approve or sign the statement drafted by Mr. Yonge. (Hr. Dodds had not kept the draft.) On May 17 he received the following letter and amencledrdraft from Hr. Yonge :</p>
        <p>19, Wibon-Patten-street. Warrington, May 16th, 1903.</p>
        <p>_ Dear Mr. Dodds, Yesterday afternoon, Mr. Parker and Mr. Cook and I had a conversation touching the endeavour we are making to make possible an " approachment "satis- factory to yourself and Mr. Roberts. Mr. Guest was abseat. owing to the death of his son-in-law- Mr. Parker reported his interviews with you about- the memorandum I ventured to submit for your con- sideration. He quoted to us your difficulty about its first paragraph. quoted what I now enclose I have tried to modify that paragraph and to re-cast the memo, so as to come more in accordance, as I would hope, with your feelings in the matter. I need scarcely repeat how anxious we all are to bring this long misunderstanding to a final end, and I hope you will do all you possibly can to help us to success. . _ _</p>
        <p>I quite agree with you that it would be very inex- pedient to give any fresh publicity to this matter, and I hope that Mr. Roberts will not do anything to bring it about. I would hope that if you can accept some statement of the kind I now venture to send you, the matter may be laid to rest. Faithfully Tours, JOHN YONGE. James Dodds, Esq.</p>
        <p>MR. YONGE'S PROPOSED STATEMENT OR MR. DODDS'</p>
        <p>SIGNATURE. Desiring that any estrangement consequent upon the Non-trading Agitation of some time since should now, if possible, be brought to an end, I wish to say that in the part I took in it,l was not actuated by any personal ill-feeling towards Mr. Henry Roberts. It was with me entirely a matter of principle (to which I still adhere) that a Member of a Municipal Body should not "trade" with the Corporation to which he be!ongs. Such trading may not be</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0166" n="art0166">
        <p>" illegal," but in my judgment, it is highly undesirable and improper. If at any time I made Use of the term " immoral," I did not intend thereby to impute anything personally dishonourable to Mr. Roberts, but simply to indicate that, in my judgment, 1 the trading referred to was open to very serious objection indeed. I have been asked whether, in the event of 'Mr. Roberts at some future time seeking to return to Municipal life, I should ha likely to favour any opposition against his doing so. My reply is, that, provided he does not do so while having a substantial interest in a Company trading with the Corporation,l should only be too glad to see him return to the public service of his native town, of which he is so capable a son and citizen. I sincerely trust that these assurances may prove acceptable, and that they may lead to better future relationships.</p>
        <p>Before deciding as to whether he could consistently accept and sign the newly drafted memo, as above, Mr. Dodds felt he should carefully reconsider the whole position and the following letter to Mr.Yonge resulted. Penkoth House, May 23rd, 1903.</p>
        <p>Dear Mr. Yonge, I have delayed my reply to your letter of the 16th instant so that I might go into the whole matter afresh. I have spent a good deal of time in looking over the published report of the Non-Trading Com- mission, and I really feel that I have gone as far as I possibly can, and that, in justice to myself and others, and having regard to all the facts, I cannot do more. The draft you aro good enough to submit has an apologetic tone underlying it, which the circum- stances do not warrant, and I hear in various quarters that the negotiations that have already taken place have been misrepresented as covering an apology from me to Mr. Roberts.</p>
        <p>So far as I am personally concerned, there has been no "estrangement," and the two statements corn- plained of have never been made. I have nothing to withdraw or modify of all I have said or written,and if the assurances I have already given are net adequate I can only regret that the efforts I have made have proved fruitless. The whole question was one of principle, and the individuals concerned did not in any way influence my action. It is natural that individuals involved may feel their position ; in the case of Mr. Roberts I have gone as far as I can towards disposing of such feeling, which I have no wish whatever to be a means of perpetuating, and I once more and finally strongly advise that the matter should be allowed to rest where it was left at the Parr Hall meeting of the 10th ultimo. Believe me, Yours sincerely, JAMES DODDS. The Rev. John Yonge.</p>
        <p>19, Wilson Patten-street, Warrington, May 23, 1908. Dear Mr. Dodd; Your letter is to me a very great disappointment indeed. I had hoped for a better and happier result of all our time and trouble. I fear that it is impos- sible to persuade Mr. Roberts to let the matter rest. however desirable it may appear that such should be the case.</p>
        <p>lam bolding your letter back until I can see Mr. Parker and Mr. Cook, which cannot be until Monday. Very truly yours, . JOHN YONGE. - _ _</p>
        <p>James Dodds Esq.</p>
        <p>ANOTREIt MEETING.</p>
        <p>On Monday evening, May Mr. Dodds was asked by telephone to meet Messrs. Yonorfe, Cook, Parker and Guest at the Winmarlegh Cafd, and be called there on his way to the station. He was asked if he could not accept the amended Yonge draft dated May 10th, subject to the deletion of the last, the alteration of the first and a slight modification of the second paragraph. He undertook to draw up, sign and send to the Committee the next day, a memo going as far as he felt he could go in the direction of the draft of May 10th. The next day (May 26th) Mr. Dodds sent a copy of the following memo to each member of the Committee with a letter stating that it was his final effort to meet their wishes. MR. DODDS' FINAL STATEMENT. Penketh, May 26th, 1903. As there exists a desire that any feeling consequent upon the Non-trading agitation should be brought to an end, I wish to repast what I have so often publicly stated—that, in the part I took in it, I was not actuated by any personal ill-feeling to anyone. It was with me entirely a matter of principle, to which I still as strongly as ever adhere, that a member of a Municipal body should not trade with the Corpora- tion to which he belongs. Such trading, if done through a limited Company, is legal, but the principle is the same ; that is, the trading is highly improper, whether done through a limited company or other- wise I have been asked whether, in the event of Mr. Henry Roberts at some future time seeking to return to Municipal work,l should be likely to favour opposi- tion to his doing so. My reply is, that so long as he does not sea to enter the Council while largely interested in a concern trading with the Corporation, I should not think of furthering oppositioo to his return, certainly not on the same grounds as before.</p>
        <p>JAMES DODDS. Mr. Dodds received no acknowledgment of the receipt of the foregoing statetnent, and heard nothing further until Saturday last when his attention was called to the letter and statement; of Mr. Roberts in the "Guardian." He never saw the statement signed by the four members of the Committee until he read it in Saturday's papers, and he was told (by Mr. Parker) that the statement was drawn up by Mr. Roberts several days. after its date, that is after the meeting was held!</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0167" n="art0167">
        <head>WARRINGTON BANKRUPTCY COURT.</head>
        <p>WARRINGTON BANKRUPTCY COURT.</p>
        <p>FRlBAY.—Before Mr. Registrar Ridgway. Toe aCCONIST.S FA ILURE.—Ernost Wilmot Bathers', tobacconist, formerly carrying on business at 77, Orford-lane, and 393, Horsemarket-street, appeared for his public examination. He stated that he.commenced business at 77, Orford-lane on December 26th, 1905 with cash capital of £l4, his savings. He purchased the stock and fixtures for £6 ss. from Mr.Edvein Bradshaw, I receiver for the debenture-holders of Stanwav, Ltd. He opened another shop in February, 1907, at 39a, Horse- ! market-street at a cost of £35. His insolvency was due to bad trade, lack of capital, interest on loans from money-lenders, and ill-health of his wife. The falling ' off in turnover was attributable to a large number of workmen in Warrington having been placed on short time. He borrowed £l6l from money-lenders and ' signed promissory notes for £242. As the money had to be repaid quickly, the rate of interest would con siderably exceed 100 per cent, per annum. His liabilr ties amounted to £403 Sc. sd. and assets to £47 Os. 6d" —The examination was closed. EFFECT OF UNSEASONABLE WEATHER.—In the case of Frederick Whittaksr, residing at 34, Gol borne-street, and formerly carrying on business as a fish and game dealer at several shops, it was stated that the liabilities. totalled £321 15s. 6d. and the assets £B3 16s. 4el.— Mr. Browne appeared for the debtor, who stated that he commenced business at 6, Walton-road, Stockton Heath, in March, 1901, with cash capital of £75, his savings. He opened another shop at 204, Knntsford-read in February, 1907, another at 105, Orford-lane in October, 1907, and a fourth at 675, Knutsford-road, in November, 1907. He closed the Orford-lane shop in January 1908. His failure was due to insufficiency of capital for the purposes of his busi- ness ; loss sustained on four ponies ; heavy business expenses in proportion to turnover; loss of goods owing to nnfavourable weather last Christmas ; severe com- petition, and negligence of assistants at his branch shops. He believed he had sufficient assets to pay all his debts when he opened the first branch shop in I(nuts- ford-road.—The examination was closed.</p>
        <p>S EfluEL TO A COUNTY COURT ACTlON.—George Henry Barlow, Pear-tree Cottage, Knutsforl-road, coal dealer, appeared for his public examination. Mr. Browne represented the debtor, who stated that his failure was due to an action brought against him by a Mrs. Atwell, in the Warrington County Court, to recover damages for injury to her foot, caused by his lurry running over her (and in which a verdict was given against him for £2O and costs), to £25 loss on the purchase of a horse, and to loss of trade in consequence of the Warrington Co-operative Society haviug com- menced to hawk coal in, his district. His liabilities amounted to £135 9a. 2d., and his assets to £23 us.— The examination was closed.</p>
        <p>FORMER WARRINGTON PUBLICAN'S FAILURE.—TO the case of Arthur Starkey, licensed victualler and farmer, Horse Shoe Hotel, Kingsley, it was stated that the liabilities were £477 3s. and the assets £53 9s. 4d. Debtor stated that he commenced business at the Feathers Hotel, Warrington and left it in January 1899 because the Corporation had squired part of the site for street widening, removing to the Horse Shoe Hotel, Kingsldy. He attributed his failure to losses on horses and cattle and decrease of business owing to improve- ments at another Kingsley hotel. fie had borrowed £389 from money lenders and given promissory notes for £516. —The examination was cicse I.—Mr. Browne represented the ch,btor. No ASSETS.—In the ease of John Jones Palmer, monumental mason, &amp;c., Frodsham, it was stated that the liabilities were upwards of £2OO, and there were DO assets. His failure was mainly due to the cost of develop- a stone quarry and to losses on sale of houses.—The examination was closed. ,</p>
        <p>ASHTON'S LIFE TONIC repairs broken down systems, clears rind strengthens the brain and braces np the nerves, is. per bottle. Also in tablet form Is. per bottle from IL BLIASIITON, 63, Sankey-et., Warrington.—Advt.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0168" n="art0168">
        <head>MR. H. ROBERTS AND "THE PAST."</head>
        <p>MR. H. ROBERTS AND "THE PAST."</p>
        <p>FACTS NOT EASILY WIPED OUT.</p>
        <p>A Few " Landmarks" 0 the Great Agitation.</p>
        <p>At the conclusion of the remarkable statement with which Mr. Henry Roberts rushed into print last Saturday was the following significant sentence : "My honour having now been vindicated, I am content to wie out all the past, and to let this be my final word upon an old and bitter controversy." . We have carefully studied the contents of Mr Roberts' letter and can find nothing which is specially notable because it vindicates Mr. Roberts' honour, other than the published statement that at a private conference:</p>
        <p>" J. Dodds admitted frankly that the trading of the Warrington Slate Company, Limited and of H. Roberts had been neither ' illegal nor immoral,' and that there had been nothing in H. Roberts' public career which was in any shape or form inconsistent with the conduct of an honourable man."</p>
        <p>This seems to apply with a heavy brush the coat of whitewash apparently so much desired. But Mr. Dodds denies having given utterance to a statement so subversive of the whole non-trading principle, and we cannot imagine that any person in his senses would believe that he had. The correspondence published alongside shows that the statement was drawn up by Mr. Roberts himself, and signed by the gentlemen mentioned at his instigation, and probably under a misapprehension. ,But, even supposing these remarkable words had been uttered by Mr. Dodds, the honour of Mr. Roberts as a public man is not vindicated thereby if, as he admits, it has in the past been called into question. Mr. Dodds was not the solo complainant against Mr. Roberts, and in any case the withdrawal of his plaint would not alter facts already proven. We propose to re-capitulate some of these facts in order ta show Mr. Roberts that he cannot " wipe out all the past " by a stroke of the pen—and his own pen too !</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0169" n="art0169">
        <head>NON-TRPIDING IN ITS INPI2INeY.</head>
        <p>NON-TRPIDING IN ITS INPI2INeY.</p>
        <p>It will be remembered by most people in Warrington that the retirement of the Lord Mayor-elect of Manchester—Alderman Higginbottom—in November, 1900, as the result Of public acts which a Special Committee described as altogether improper," was the cause of attention being drawn in Warrington to the trading between members of the Council with the Corporation through companies in which they were interested. In our leading article on the affaire Higginbottom, on November 10th, 1900, we said : " There must, we should imagine, have been much searching of hearts in the corporate bodies of other towns than Manchester as a consequence of the charges against Alderman Higginbottom. There are few Corporations where there is not some such sailing near the line, and men who have any conscience at all, even though innocent of anything actually improper, must have felt that their combination of business and public service was not one affording unalloyed satisfaction and peace of mind. . . Such an equivocal poAtion as that Mr. Lloyd Higgin- bottom placed himself in we sincerely trust no mem- ber of the Warrington Corporation occupies." 71 FAMOUS DBOLAR7ITIONI. At that time Mr. H. Roberts was Mayor of Warring- ton, and chairman of the Warrington Slate Company, Limited,which contracted largely with the Corporation. At a meeting of the General Purposes Committee held on Monday, November 20th, 1900—a report of which was supplied to the Press—he made the following announcement : _ . .</p>
        <p>" So far as he was personally concerned, neither he nor any member of his family had any pecuniary interest whatever, either directly or indirectly in any contracts which the Warrington Slate Company had or might have with the Corporation, and this would continue as lone as he remained a member of the Town Council."</p>
        <p>During this same month of November, 1900 letters began to appear in the local press in reference to the municipal trading in Warrington. Mr. S. Kenyon wrote pointing out the legal right but moral wrong of a Councillor who traded with his Corporation through a limited liability company of which he was a member. Mr. John Pemberton wrote suggesting that the Mayor cause an inquiry to bo made into Corporation contracts and accounts either by a committee of members of the Corporation and others,or the Local Government Board. Mr. Martin Flanaghan followed with a letter referring to the Mayor's declaration to the General Purposes Committee, and asked " if we are to assume that the Mayor is posing as a philanthropist by sacrificing his share of the nrollts." Ihis brought the question of trading nearer home, and while the Mayor made several spirited rejoinders in the Council chamber he made no effort to afford facilities for the inquiry suggested by Mr. Pemberton.</p>
        <p>Pt CHALLENGE. In the same year, 1900, the Mayor threw out a challenge as follows : " I would give £lOO to the Infirmary if any single case of abuse or advantage that had been sought or obtained by me for myself or my firm to anybody who would prove such a case." On the 29th November, 1901, Mr.Pemberton accepted the challenge in writing by registered letter, naming Mr. Wainwright of Liverpool as arbitrator, but received no reply. Mr. Pemberton instructs us to say that he is still awaiting a reply and still willing to take up the challenge. The agitation once started continued to grow, the Mayor and his connection with the slate Company had become the forefront of the attack, and Mr. Dodds intervened in the public correspondence on July Ist, 1901, when he wrote that " nothing but the fullest investigation will now avail," and suggested that the question be discussed without heat. In a second letter he wrote in reply to a letter signed "Maxim " defend- ing the Mayor's position : "1 will only say that in my judgment there is a debit side as well as a credit side to his 16 years con- nection with the Corporation, and I doubt if the advantage has been with the latter." In a subsequent letter, Mr. Dodds said "the only proper course is for the Mayor to resign."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0170" n="art0170">
        <head>Th DRAMTITIO INeIDEINT.</head>
        <p>Th DRAMTITIO INeIDEINT.</p>
        <p>The first of the series of great Non-Trading meetings. which exposed an enormous amount of trading through private and limited companies by members of the Council with the Corporation, was held in the Co-opera- tive Hall on October 23, 1901. At a second meeting in Parr Hall the same month there occurred the most dramatic incident in the history of the whole agita- tion. Mr. Pemberton was speaking, and said that municipal trading was "still br,oirbvr on," when The Mayor, who had enteredtheHall said " And will do l" _ _ .</p>
        <p>There Was an immediate uproar, in which Mr. Pember- ton retorted : " That, gentlemen, depends upon the ratepayers "—a statement which was fully borne out by subsequent events. Public opinion eventually became too strong for the " traders," and the Council was purged of the evil which had assumed such menacing proportions. After the Non-Traders' sweeping victory at the Municipal elections in 1902, Alderman Roberts—who had been succeeded in the Mayoralty by Mr. J. C. Parr —resigned his position as Alderman, and wrote a letter in which he said : "I consider the recent Ward Elec- tions a vote of want of confidence in the Council." Nevertheless, Mr. Roberts was the only member of the Council who thought fit to resign. MR. ROBERTS RS BUYER RND SELLER,</p>
        <p>On July 16th, 1907, Mr. Roberts appeared as a witness before the Joint Committee of both H 03309 of Parlia- ment on Municipal Trading. In his evidence he stated that of the £17,250 subscribed capital of the Warrington Slate Company Ltd. he and his family held £10,870. He also stated that " for some time their sales to the Corporation had only amounted to £2,000 per annum." I Questioned by Mr. Powell Williarns,Mr. Roberts said , that as Mayor and a councillor it would be his duty to I see that all purchases for the Corpration were made as ' cheaply as possible. Mr. Williams : As a director of your company it would be your duty to sell as dearly as you could—to get good prices ? The witness : Yes. Mr. Williams : Did you not think the two positions were conflicting ? The witness : Not in my case, as I took no part in directing the Corporation, either as seller or buyer. Mr. Williams : Do not you think that in the case of a member of the Corporation who is a substantial holder in a private company it would be better for tho corn- I pally not to have any transactions with the Corporation? The witness : I think everything would depend upon the conditions under which the business was transacted. 1 Mr. Williams : But would not it be better that there should be no transactions as between the company and the Corporation under the circumstances I allude to ? The witness replied that with the safeguards he had referred to—advertisement and tender—he saw no objec- tion. Ile attached the greatest importance to the provision of these safeguards. His objection to the system of elective auditors was on the ground that other questions were often mixed up with the elections. WIRT THU NOI.TRRDI3RS' INQUIRY FOUND.</p>
        <p>In 1903-1 there was held the great inquiry by the Commissioners appointed by the Non-Trading Associa- tion to inquire into all trading by past and present members of the Council with the Corporation, either direct or indirect . . , . . in the period from the Ist April, 1896, to the 31st March, 1903. The Commissioners were Messrs. Adam Horrocks, John Pemberton, and S. Norbury Williams, and after hearing a mass of evidence into all kinds of municipal trading, they presented their report in October 1904. Mr. Roberts was among those N‘ho were invited to give</p>
        <p>evidence, but declined to appear before the Inquiry Commissioners. In their report,the latter stated" that the trading, both direct and indirect, which has been alleged at the public meetings in question has beer' amply proved. We include in this Report, a list of the persons and the companies with whom we find that. this trading has been carried on. We are convinced, from the evidencs that has been laid before us, that this network of trading has demoralised the municipal government of the town. The opinions and acts of both officials and members of the Corporation have, as a consequence of it, become loose and unsatisfactory. and a condition of things has been produced which has been of infinite harm to Warrington, and which, if it prevailed on the same scale throughout the boroughs of the country, would render municipal government a byword and a scandal."</p>
        <p>*Under the chapter headed THE WAHHINGTON SLATS COMPANY LTD., th 3 commissioners state in their report: " Mr. Roberts was the managing director during the seven years. . . . . . The company continou usly traded both directly and Indirectly with the Corp ora- tion during the period under review. The direct trading has amounted to RIGA/ It has been impossible for us to ascertain the amount of indirect trading, seeing that Mr. llenry Roberts has refused to come before us, and the officials have shown a remarkable ignorance on the subject We find that there is scarcely a work carried out by I the Corporation in recent years to which the Warrington Slate Company has not supplied a great share of the materials. . . . . . We would call special atten- tion to the case of the Tramway Contract executed by Mr. J. A. Ewart, and to tho steps taken by this cem- pany to become sub-contractors at a time when Mr. Henry Roberts, its managing director, was Mayor of the Borough and Chairman of the Tramways Committee, and himself opened the tenders, no other member of the Council being present. This case appears to us a most glaring instance of the evil effects which are brought about when members of the Corporation are allowed to dabble in and indirectly to profit by the work 3 executed by their Corporation."</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0171" n="art0171">
        <head>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</head>
        <p>LETTERS TO THE EDITOR.</p>
        <p>NOTICE TO CORRESPONDENTS. Authors of letters intended for publication must in all cases send their names and addresses to the Editor as a guarantee of good faith. OnsmtvErt.—We will try to find space for your letter next week.—En. "Ex." ;</p>
        <p>Mr. Dodds and Mr. Roberts. Sir,—As one of the honorary secretaries of the Warrington Citizens' Temperance League kindly allow me through your columns to qualify a statement made by Mr. Henry Roberts in last Saturday's " Guardian." In his letter to the public he says that on April sth he was the recipient of a pressing personal invitation to attend the great licensing meeting held in Parr Hall on April 10th. Might I remind Mr. Roberts that " the pressing invitation "he received was only one of many so sent out to other gentlernun, and that it was the sole object of the League to seek the co-operation of anyone whose desire was for temperance reform. In conclusion, sir, might I add that the present con- troversy will in no way obstruct the progress of our League. We were formed not for the purpose of entering into personalities but for the purifying of our civic life.—Yours obediently, GEORGE A. LEACH, Hon. Sec., C.T.L. Knutsford-road, Grappenhall. Juno sth, 1903.</p>
        <p>• The Equipment of the Technical Institute. As a former student of the Technical Institute I desire so support the statements of previous corres- pondents. The equipment of the school as regards apparatus required for the study of the higher branches of technical subjects is so poor that serious students now consider it wasted time trying to make shift with the materials on hand.</p>
        <p>After completing a session at the Institute under such conditions when one looks back on what has been done and what might have been done had the necessary apparatus been available, it is a matter of great discouragement to students and teachers alike. That students should be compelled to go out of town to obtain instruction in science subjects closely con- nected with the trades of Warrington, at great incon- venience and expense is not a very creditable reflection on the administration of higher education in the town. An elaborate building and syllabus are poor substitutes for the equipment necessary for compliance with Board of Education demands.—Yours faithfully, H. SHERBURN. Stockton Heath, June 4th, 1908.</p>
        <p>The Tariff Reform League and Unemployment. Sir,—Alderman Bennett desires me to reply to your correspondent, Anti Protectionist, more especially as the question referred to was, in the first instance, put to me personally. I stated then, and I repeat, notwith- standing the estimates and quotations mentioned that with regard to unemployment in America, the reports are much exaggerated, if they are intended to apply to the present, but if the estimates refer to the period immediately after the „financial panic, they may be approximately correct. But your correspondent, like many others on the fiscal question,seems quite muddled amongst causes and effects. If we assume for the gn ke of argument, that the state of affairs ire America is as bad as he says, there is no evidence that it is due primarily or generally to the fiscal policy which America has adopted. The indications are that the causes are to be found in that reckless speculation and unscrupulous finance for which America is notorious. Mr. George W. Russell, the American economist, said recently in the Boston Advertiser, "Non employment is the outgrowth, not of industrial principles, which civilization has adopted, but of the theories which anti-civilization has adopted. There is never any want of work in countries which do their own work. Only in countries which export money and raw products to pay for products made in other countries, is there any dearth of work." Of course Mr. Russell is tracing causes and effects when trade is in a normal condition, and the extraordinary progress made by Arnerich and Germany under Protection supports that view. I can assure your correspondent that Tariff Reformers are quite satisfied with the quality and purity of the oil they are using. The anti-Protectionist brand is probably some inferior German or American stuff. We only use British or Colonial.—Yours Sm., W. CIIRIMES. 679, Knutsford Road, Latchford.</p>
        <p>I</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0172" n="art0172">
        <head>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. Paddington Residents Concerned.</head>
        <p>RURAL DISTRICT COUNCIL. Paddington Residents Concerned.</p>
        <p>The monthly meeting of the Warrington Rural Dis- trict Council was held in the Bewsey Chambers on Wednesday, Dr. Murray in the chair. A letter v‘as read from the Postmaster-General asking the Council's permission to construct an overground telegraphic line along a portion of the Manchester-road at Paddington. A protest had since been handed in signed by a great many of the inhabitants of the district, stating that such an alteration would greatly disfigure and depreciate the value of the adjacent property and be highlY objectionable to all the residents in the locality. Mr. HOIIGIHON said that the matter was causing great alarm in the neighbourhood. A large number of trees would have to be removed and so disfigure the neighourhood. The wires had been under-ground for over forty years, and he asked the council ta oppose the alteration.</p>
        <p>The SuavEvon ('lr. Newcombe) said that the Rural District Council had really nothing to do with it. It was only a mere matter of form to get the permission of the local authority. It rested with the County Council end not with the Rural District Council. Mr. HOUGHTON moved that the council do not con- sent to the application. Mr. Mather seconded and the motion was carried.</p>
      </div>
      <div type="article" xml:id="PressMint-GB_1908-06-06_WidnesExaminer_art0173" n="art0173">
        <head>WHITE CITY.</head>
        <p>THE WHITE CITY.</p>
        <p>Those of our readers who intend visiting Manchester during the coming holidays, should not fail to pay a visit to the White City. Since last year many import- ant alterations have been made, and the thousands who have visited the White City since the opening day, May 16th last, have had nothing but praise for this delightful pleasure. resort. One of the most interesting and instructive exhibitions ever seen in England, is that given by the Bontoc Igerrotes from the Phillipine Islands. On Whit-Monday " Prinness Trixie " the beautiful horse, who is said to possess a human mind, will commence a long engagement at the White City. During the time the "Princess Trixie "was performing at the Palace Theatre, London, last year, she was "commanded " to appear before the King and Queen at Windsor, but nnfortuu" ately the "Princess" contracted pneumonia, and as a consequence was unable to obey. the Royal order, The Performance of Princess Trixte is truly marvellous. She spells words, adds up figures, picks out colours correctly, and even spells the names of people in tb audience. Mr. J. Calvin Brown, managing director or the White City, has made special arrangements to deal with the holiday crowds, and those coming from town! outside Manchester, will find a splendid service 01 trains and cars to convey them to the White City.</p>
